WEBVTT

1
00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:51.410
Theatre – it can show us who we are,
who we were and who we could be.

2
00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:55.620
It means imagining bigger,

3
00:00:55.750 --> 00:00:57.540
understanding better,

4
00:00:57.620 --> 00:00:59.830
feeling together –

5
00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:02.620
on our stages and on tour,

6
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.500
in schools

7
00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.200
and with communities,

8
00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.700
on cinema screens worldwide

9
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:10.830
and now at home.

10
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:12.870
We are the National Theatre.

11
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:15.910
We make theatre for everyone.

12
00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:29.200
Art helps us to see the world in new ways.

13
00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:32.370
The stories behind great works
help to bring them to life,

14
00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.450
and hearing them shouldn't be a privilege.

15
00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:37.080
That's why we built Bloomberg Connects.

16
00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.790
An app that lets you discover a growing
number of museums, gardens and parks,

17
00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:45.160
behind-the-scenes interviews
and public art from around the world,

18
00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:48.700
all in a single download
right in the palm of your hand.

19
00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:52.120
Bloomberg Connects offers
a world of inspiration.

20
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.750
Join the National Theatre community
and hundreds of cultural institutions

21
00:01:56.870 --> 00:02:00.080
to get a deeper look into the world of art
through Bloomberg Connects.

22
00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.750
(Wind howling)

23
00:02:14.870 --> 00:02:15.870
(Rain)

24
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.040
(Thunder rumbling)

25
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.290
(Metallic clinking)

26
00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:48.370
(Tree creaking)

27
00:02:55.040 --> 00:02:56.040
(Silence)

28
00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.540
(Soft instrumental music)

29
00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.790
Oh. It's gonna rain tonight.

30
00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:18.750
- Paper says so?
- Yeah, right here.

31
00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:21.790
- Then it can't rain.
- Hiya.

32
00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.290
- Hey.
- Hey, Frank.

33
00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:27.620
- Oh.
- (Laughs)

34
00:03:27.750 --> 00:03:29.910
- Gee.
- Oh.

35
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:31.790
- So, what's doing?
- Nothing.

36
00:03:31.910 --> 00:03:34.000
Walking off my breakfast.

37
00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:35.660
(Soft music continues)

38
00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:38.750
Gee, that beautiful?

39
00:03:41.120 --> 00:03:43.040
Not a cloud.

40
00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:45.290
Yeah, nice.

41
00:03:46.500 --> 00:03:49.500
Every Sunday ought to be like this.

42
00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:51.790
You want the paper?

43
00:03:51.910 --> 00:03:55.660
Oh, what's the difference?
It's all bad news.

44
00:03:56.290 --> 00:03:58.040
What's today's calamity?

45
00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:01.660
I don't know.
I don't read the news part anymore.

46
00:04:01.790 --> 00:04:04.000
It's more interesting
in the "want" ads.

47
00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:06.250
Why? You trying to buy something?

48
00:04:06.370 --> 00:04:09.790
No, I'm just interested.
To see what people want, you know?

49
00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:12.500
- Oh.
- Uh... For instance...

50
00:04:12.620 --> 00:04:14.410
(Laughing) Oh, here.

51
00:04:14.540 --> 00:04:19.660
There's a guy looking
for two Newfoundland dogs.

52
00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:21.620
(Laughs)

53
00:04:22.160 --> 00:04:25.540
Well, what does he want
with two Newfoundland dogs?

54
00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.410
- That is funny.
- Yeah.

55
00:04:27.540 --> 00:04:28.870
And here's a...

56
00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:34.250
"Wanted: old dictionaries.
High prices paid."

57
00:04:34.370 --> 00:04:35.540
(Chuckles)

58
00:04:36.290 --> 00:04:40.290
Oh, what's a man going to do
with an old dictionary?

59
00:04:40.410 --> 00:04:43.540
But why not?
He's probably a book collector.

60
00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.790
You mean he'll make a living
out of that?

61
00:04:45.910 --> 00:04:48.290
Sure, there's a lot of 'em.

62
00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.040
(Whistles)

63
00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:52.790
All kind of business goin' on.

64
00:04:52.910 --> 00:04:55.910
In my day, you were either
a lawyer or a doctor

65
00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.290
or you worked in a shop.

66
00:04:58.410 --> 00:04:59.540
Today... Oof.

67
00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:02.910
Well, I was going
to be a forester once.

68
00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:06.500
Well, that just shows ya.

69
00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:09.040
In my day,
there was no such thing.

70
00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:12.290
You look at a page like this

71
00:05:12.410 --> 00:05:15.120
and you just realize
how ignorant you are.

72
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.250
(Chuckles)

73
00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.370
- Hey.
- Huh?

74
00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:24.160
- What happened to your tree?
- Ain't that awful?

75
00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:27.500
Ah, the wind must've got it last night.
You heard the wind, didn't you?

76
00:05:27.620 --> 00:05:30.870
- Yeah, I got a mess in my yard too.
- Yeah.

77
00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:32.410
What a pity.

78
00:05:34.620 --> 00:05:36.040
What did Kate say?

79
00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.790
Oh, they're all asleep yet.

80
00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:41.000
I'm... just waiting
for her to see it.

81
00:05:42.750 --> 00:05:44.500
- You know? It's funny.
- Mm?

82
00:05:45.250 --> 00:05:48.500
Larry was born in August.

83
00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:52.500
He'd been 27 this month
and his tree blows down.

84
00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.790
Well, I'm surprised you remember
his birthday, Frank. That's nice.

85
00:05:56.910 --> 00:05:59.910
Well, I'm working on his horoscope.

86
00:06:00.500 --> 00:06:04.660
How can you make him a horoscope?
That's for the future, ain't it?

87
00:06:04.790 --> 00:06:07.790
No. Well...
What I'm doing is this.

88
00:06:07.910 --> 00:06:12.620
See, Larry was reported missing
on November 25th, right?

89
00:06:12.750 --> 00:06:13.750
Yeah?

90
00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:17.250
Well, then, we assume
that if he was killed,

91
00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.540
it was on November 25th.

92
00:06:19.660 --> 00:06:21.910
Now, what Kate wants is...

93
00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.870
Oh, Kate asked you
to make a horoscope?

94
00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:28.620
Yeah, what she wants to find out is

95
00:06:28.750 --> 00:06:33.160
whether November 25th
was a favorable day for Larry.

96
00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:38.250
What's that, favorable day?

97
00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:40.040
Well, a favorable day for a person

98
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:44.040
is a fortunate day
according to his stars.

99
00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:47.910
Well, in other words,
it would be practically impossible

100
00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:51.540
for him to have died
on his favorable day.

101
00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:54.790
- Well?
- Oh.

102
00:06:54.910 --> 00:06:57.370
Was that his favorable day,
November 25th?

103
00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.620
That's what I'm working on
to find out.

104
00:06:59.750 --> 00:07:01.910
It takes time!
See, the point is,

105
00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:05.120
if November 25th
was his favorable day,

106
00:07:05.250 --> 00:07:08.910
it's completely possible
he's alive somewhere.

107
00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.290
- Because I mean, it's possible...
- Is he talkin' sense?

108
00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:13.910
Him? Oh, he's all right.

109
00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:17.000
He's just completely out of his mind,
that's all.

110
00:07:17.120 --> 00:07:20.370
The trouble with you is
you don't believe in anything.

111
00:07:20.500 --> 00:07:24.540
And your trouble is
that you believe in anything.

112
00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:27.660
- You didn't see my kid this morning?
- No.

113
00:07:27.790 --> 00:07:29.160
(Chuckling) Imagine?

114
00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:32.290
He walked off with his thermometer
right out of his bag.

115
00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:34.160
Yeah. What a problem.

116
00:07:34.290 --> 00:07:37.410
One look at a girl
and he takes her temperature.

117
00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:42.250
Oh, God. That boy is going
to be a real doctor. He is smart.

118
00:07:42.370 --> 00:07:45.660
Over my dead body
will he be a doctor.

119
00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:50.410
- Huh! A good beginning too.
- Why? It's an honorable profession.

120
00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:53.910
Oh, Frank, will you stop talking
like a civics book?

121
00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:59.540
(Splutters) I saw a movie a couple weeks ago,
reminded me of you.

122
00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:02.660
Um...
There was a doctor in that picture...

123
00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:04.910
Oh! Um... Oh.

124
00:08:05.040 --> 00:08:07.000
(Mumbling)

125
00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:08.910
- Oh!
- Um...

126
00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:11.410
Oh, uh, Don Ameche?

127
00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:13.120
I think that was it, yeah.

128
00:08:13.250 --> 00:08:16.750
And he worked in his basement
discovering things.

129
00:08:16.870 --> 00:08:18.870
See? That's what you ought to do.

130
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:22.160
You, you could
help humanity instead...

131
00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:27.160
Oh, I would love to help humanity
on a Warner Brothers salary.

132
00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:29.660
Oh. That's a good one, Jim.

133
00:08:29.790 --> 00:08:30.790
(Laughter)

134
00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:33.040
Where's the beautiful girl?
Was supposed to be here.

135
00:08:33.160 --> 00:08:34.660
- Ooh.
- Annie came?

136
00:08:34.790 --> 00:08:36.410
Sure. She's sleepin' upstairs.

137
00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:39.160
We picked her up last night
on the one o'clock train.

138
00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:41.620
Oh, it's such a wonderful thing.

139
00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:42.910
(Laughs)

140
00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:46.540
Girl leaves here a scrawny kid.

141
00:08:46.660 --> 00:08:49.000
A couple years go by... (Whistles)

142
00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:50.660
...she's a regular woman.

143
00:08:50.790 --> 00:08:52.540
I hardly recognized her.

144
00:08:52.660 --> 00:08:56.540
She was running
in and out of this yard all her life.

145
00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:57.790
(Laughs)

146
00:08:58.410 --> 00:09:02.660
Ah, that was a very happy family
that used to live in your house, Jim.

147
00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:04.120
Well, I'd like to meet her.

148
00:09:04.250 --> 00:09:06.250
The block can use a pretty girl.

149
00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:09.410
In the whole neighborhood
there's not a damned thing to look at.

150
00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.040
Except my wife, of course.

151
00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:13.370
(Audience laughs)

152
00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:16.250
Mrs. Adams is on the phone, you dog.

153
00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:19.000
Such is the condition
which prevails.

154
00:09:19.540 --> 00:09:22.250
- My love, my light.
- Don't sniff around me.

155
00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:25.410
And give her a nasty answer.
I can smell her perfume over the phone.

156
00:09:25.540 --> 00:09:27.750
- What's the matter now?
- I don't know, dear.

157
00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:31.370
It sounds like she's in terrible pain,
unless her mouth is full of candy.

158
00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:33.120
Why don't you tell her to lay down?

159
00:09:33.250 --> 00:09:35.660
She enjoys it more
when you tell her to lay down.

160
00:09:35.790 --> 00:09:39.290
- When are you going to see Mr. Hubbard?
- My dear, Mr. Hubbard is not sick.

161
00:09:39.410 --> 00:09:42.410
And I have better things to do
than to sit there and hold his hand.

162
00:09:42.540 --> 00:09:45.540
It seems to me that for ten dollars
you could hold his hand.

163
00:09:48.410 --> 00:09:51.870
If your son wants to play golf,
tell him I am ready.

164
00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:57.290
Or if he'd like to take a trip
around the world for about 30 years.

165
00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:58.660
(Quiet chuckle)

166
00:09:58.790 --> 00:10:00.410
Why do you needle him?

167
00:10:00.540 --> 00:10:03.160
He's a doctor.
Women are supposed to call him up.

168
00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:05.290
All I said was
Mrs. Adams is on the phone.

169
00:10:05.410 --> 00:10:06.410
Yeah...

170
00:10:06.540 --> 00:10:09.040
- Can I have some of your parsley?
- Yeah, sure, sure.

171
00:10:09.540 --> 00:10:11.540
You know, Susie,
you were a nurse too long.

172
00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:12.660
You're just...

173
00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:15.910
too... too... realistic.

174
00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:17.660
(Chuckling) Now you said it.

175
00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:19.000
(Joe chuckles)

176
00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:20.910
Frank! The toaster... Hiya.

177
00:10:21.040 --> 00:10:24.120
- Hello!
- The toaster is off again.

178
00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:26.660
Well, plug it in. I just fixed it.

179
00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:30.290
Please, dear, fix it back
like it was before.

180
00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:33.910
I don't know why you can't learn

181
00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:38.000
to turn on a simple thing
like a toaster.

182
00:10:38.120 --> 00:10:39.500
(Laughs)

183
00:10:39.620 --> 00:10:42.000
- Thomas Edison.
- He's really very handy.

184
00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:43.290
(Chuckling)

185
00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.790
Woo. Did the wind get your tree?

186
00:10:46.290 --> 00:10:48.160
- Yeah, last night.
- Oh.

187
00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.160
- What a pity.
- Yeah.

188
00:10:50.290 --> 00:10:53.290
- Annie get in?
- Oh, she'll be down soon.

189
00:10:53.410 --> 00:10:55.750
Wait till you meet her, Sue.
She's a knockout.

190
00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:57.790
(Sighs) I should've been a man.

191
00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:00.540
People are always introducing me
to beautiful women.

192
00:11:00.660 --> 00:11:02.000
Tell her to come over later.

193
00:11:02.120 --> 00:11:04.120
I imagine she'd like to see
what we did with her house.

194
00:11:04.250 --> 00:11:05.620
- Yeah.
- And, oh, thanks.

195
00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:06.750
Sure.

196
00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:11.620
- Hm.
- Is she still unhappy, Joe?

197
00:11:12.160 --> 00:11:13.870
- What, Annie?
- Mm.

198
00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:19.790
Well, I don't suppose she goes around
dancing on her toes, but...

199
00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:22.500
...she seems to be over it.

200
00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.160
- Is she going to get married?
- Oh.

201
00:11:25.290 --> 00:11:27.000
Is there anybody?

202
00:11:27.790 --> 00:11:31.410
I suppose...
Say, it's a couple years already.

203
00:11:31.910 --> 00:11:33.540
She can't mourn a boy forever.

204
00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:37.790
- It's so strange.
- Hm.

205
00:11:38.790 --> 00:11:42.040
Annie's here, and not even married,

206
00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.500
and I've got three babies.

207
00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:47.660
I always thought
it'd be the other way around.

208
00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:48.790
Oh.

209
00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:52.410
- Well, that's what war does.
- Hm.

210
00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:56.040
I had two sons, now I've got one.

211
00:11:57.540 --> 00:11:59.910
It just changed all the tallies.

212
00:12:01.250 --> 00:12:04.790
You know, in my day,
having sons was an honor.

213
00:12:05.410 --> 00:12:08.620
Today, a doctor could make
a million dollars

214
00:12:08.750 --> 00:12:12.750
if he could figure out
a way to bring a boy into the world

215
00:12:12.870 --> 00:12:14.500
without a trigger finger.

216
00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:17.910
You know,

217
00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:20.660
I was just reading and...

218
00:12:20.790 --> 00:12:22.790
Oh! Hiya, Chris.

219
00:12:22.910 --> 00:12:25.040
(Frank) Lydia, come here!

220
00:12:25.160 --> 00:12:29.660
If you want the toaster to work,
don't plug in the malted mixer.

221
00:12:29.790 --> 00:12:31.660
Did I? (Laughs)

222
00:12:31.790 --> 00:12:35.120
And next time I fix something,
don't tell me I'm crazy!

223
00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:36.910
Now, come in here.

224
00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:39.410
I'm never going to hear
the end of this one.

225
00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:42.410
What's the difference?
Instead of toast, have a malted!

226
00:12:42.540 --> 00:12:44.410
- Shush.
- (Laughs)

227
00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:51.040
- Hey.
- Hey.

228
00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:54.910
Boy. You want the paper?

229
00:12:56.120 --> 00:12:59.540
Uh... That's all right,
just the book section.

230
00:13:00.290 --> 00:13:01.290
(Joe chuckles)

231
00:13:02.540 --> 00:13:06.250
You know, you are always reading
the book section

232
00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:08.660
and you never buy a book.

233
00:13:08.790 --> 00:13:12.290
Huh. Yeah. I like to keep abreast
of my ignorance.

234
00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.660
- (Joe chuckles)
- (Audience laughs)

235
00:13:14.790 --> 00:13:16.160
(Mumbles)

236
00:13:16.290 --> 00:13:17.910
- Thank you.
- Yeah.

237
00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:20.040
- Mm. You good?
- Yeah.

238
00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:26.000
Well, what is that now?

239
00:13:26.540 --> 00:13:28.870
Every week a new book comes out?

240
00:13:29.000 --> 00:13:31.870
Mm-hm. Yeah.
Lot of new books.

241
00:13:33.620 --> 00:13:34.660
All different?

242
00:13:36.040 --> 00:13:37.620
(Quiet laughter in audience)

243
00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:38.750
All different.

244
00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:41.290
(Whistles) Boy.

245
00:13:41.410 --> 00:13:42.790
Hey, is Annie up yet?

246
00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:46.660
Yeah. Mother's giving her breakfast
in the dining room.

247
00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:48.500
Ah. (Chuckles)

248
00:13:48.620 --> 00:13:50.750
You saw what happened to the tree?

249
00:13:51.540 --> 00:13:53.910
- Yeah.
- What's Mother going to say?

250
00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:57.040
- You're finally up!
- Oh! Bert's here.

251
00:13:57.160 --> 00:13:59.790
(Laughs) Hey! Where's Tommy?

252
00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:02.370
You know, he's got
his father's thermometer again.

253
00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:04.620
- He's taking a reading.
- What?

254
00:14:04.750 --> 00:14:06.790
But it's only oral.

255
00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:08.660
(Quiet laughter in audience)

256
00:14:09.660 --> 00:14:11.790
Well, there's no harm in oral.

257
00:14:12.410 --> 00:14:13.750
(Chuckles)

258
00:14:13.870 --> 00:14:16.910
- So what's new this morning, Bert?
- Nothin'.

259
00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:22.160
Well, then you couldn't have made
a complete inspection of the block.

260
00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:25.870
You know, in the beginning,
when I first made you a policeman,

261
00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.250
oh, you used to come in here
every morning

262
00:14:29.370 --> 00:14:30.750
with something new.

263
00:14:30.870 --> 00:14:33.040
Now nothing's ever new.

264
00:14:33.160 --> 00:14:35.790
Except some kids
from Thirtieth Street.

265
00:14:35.910 --> 00:14:39.120
They started kicking a can down the block
and I made them go away

266
00:14:39.250 --> 00:14:40.910
because you were sleeping.

267
00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.290
Now you're thinkin', Bert.
Now you're on the ball.

268
00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:49.370
Ooh! First thing you know
I'm liable to make you a detective.

269
00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:52.410
- (Gasps) Can I see the jail now?
- Oh, no, no.

270
00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:54.750
Seein' the jail ain't allowed.
You know that.

271
00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:57.160
Oh, I betcha
there isn't even a jail.

272
00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:59.540
I don't see any bars
on the cellar windows.

273
00:14:59.660 --> 00:15:04.250
Bert, you have my word of honor
there is a jail in the basement.

274
00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:06.290
Well, I showed you my gun, didn't I?

275
00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:07.870
But that's a hunting gun.

276
00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.790
That is an arresting gun.

277
00:15:09.910 --> 00:15:12.540
Then why don't you ever
arrest anybody?

278
00:15:12.660 --> 00:15:15.790
Tommy said another dirty word
to Doris yesterday

279
00:15:15.910 --> 00:15:17.660
and you didn't even demote him.

280
00:15:17.790 --> 00:15:19.500
Ah.

281
00:15:20.040 --> 00:15:22.870
That's a dangerous character,
that Tommy.

282
00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:24.870
What word did he use?

283
00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:28.250
- Oh, I can't say that.
- Well, gimme an idea.

284
00:15:28.370 --> 00:15:30.870
I can't. It's...
It's not a nice word.

285
00:15:31.000 --> 00:15:32.410
Oh.

286
00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:35.290
Well, then just whisper it
in my ear.

287
00:15:37.160 --> 00:15:39.410
- I'll close my eyes.
- (Audience laughs)

288
00:15:39.540 --> 00:15:41.040
Maybe I won't even hear it.

289
00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:44.040
- I can't, Mr. Keller!
- Don't make him do that.

290
00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:45.790
OK, Bert, I take your word.

291
00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:49.790
Now, go on out there
and keep both eyes peeled, huh?

292
00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:52.160
- For what?
- For what?

293
00:15:52.870 --> 00:15:54.410
Bert, come here.

294
00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:55.540
Look here.

295
00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:02.040
The whole neighborhood
is depending on you.

296
00:16:02.790 --> 00:16:05.370
Now, a policeman
don't ask questions.

297
00:16:05.500 --> 00:16:07.410
Now, go on. (Whistles)
Peel them eyes.

298
00:16:07.540 --> 00:16:09.410
- OK.
- And Bert...

299
00:16:09.540 --> 00:16:11.500
- Mum's the word.
- About what?

300
00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:14.540
- Well, just in general.
- (Audience laughs)

301
00:16:14.660 --> 00:16:18.370
Oh, and Bert, Bert, Bert, Bert,
be very careful.

302
00:16:18.500 --> 00:16:20.160
- OK.
- OK.

303
00:16:21.250 --> 00:16:22.250
Go on!

304
00:16:22.370 --> 00:16:24.790
(Wheezy laugh)

305
00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:28.750
(Snorts) I got all the kids crazy!

306
00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:32.790
One of these days, they'll all come in here
and beat your brains out.

307
00:16:32.910 --> 00:16:34.540
(Laughs) Yeah...

308
00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:37.870
Oh, what's she going to say, huh?

309
00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:40.160
Maybe we ought to tell her
before she sees it.

310
00:16:40.290 --> 00:16:43.120
- Uh... She saw it.
- No. How could she have seen it?

311
00:16:43.250 --> 00:16:45.250
I was the first one up.
She was still in bed.

312
00:16:45.370 --> 00:16:47.160
No, she was out here
when it broke.

313
00:16:48.160 --> 00:16:49.160
When?

314
00:16:49.290 --> 00:16:51.410
About four this morning.

315
00:16:51.540 --> 00:16:54.410
I heard it cracking.
I woke up, looked out.

316
00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:56.870
She was standing right here
when it cracked.

317
00:16:57.000 --> 00:17:00.290
What was she doing out here
four in the morning?

318
00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:02.910
I... I don't know.
When it cracked,

319
00:17:03.040 --> 00:17:05.540
she ran back into the house
and cried in the kitchen.

320
00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:08.040
Oh. You talked to her?

321
00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:10.290
(Laughs) No. No, no. No.

322
00:17:10.410 --> 00:17:13.540
I, uh... I figured the best thing
was to leave her alone.

323
00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:16.620
She cried hard?

324
00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:19.910
Uh-huh. Yeah, I could hear her
right through the floor of my room.

325
00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:22.540
What the hell was she doing
out here at that hour?

326
00:17:22.660 --> 00:17:24.040
Well... You know.

327
00:17:24.160 --> 00:17:27.750
She's dreaming about him again,
walking around at night.

328
00:17:27.870 --> 00:17:29.040
I guess she is.

329
00:17:30.040 --> 00:17:32.870
She's getting
just like after he died.

330
00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:36.620
- Oh, what's the meaning of that?
- I don't know the meaning of it,

331
00:17:36.750 --> 00:17:39.660
but I know one thing, Dad,
we made a terrible mistake with Mother.

332
00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:41.160
What?

333
00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.500
Being dishonest with her.
That always pays off,

334
00:17:43.620 --> 00:17:45.870
- now it's paying off.
- What do you mean, dishonest?

335
00:17:46.000 --> 00:17:47.620
You know Larry's not coming back.

336
00:17:47.750 --> 00:17:49.250
- I know it.
- Shh!

337
00:17:49.370 --> 00:17:51.790
Why do we allow her to go on thinking
we believe with her?

338
00:17:51.910 --> 00:17:55.660
- You want to argue with her?
- I don't, but it's time she realized

339
00:17:55.790 --> 00:17:59.290
that nobody believes
Larry is alive anymore.

340
00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:03.540
Why shouldn't she dream of him,
walk the nights waiting for him?

341
00:18:03.660 --> 00:18:05.540
Do we contradict her?

342
00:18:05.660 --> 00:18:08.370
Do we say straight out
that we have no hope anymore?

343
00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:10.410
That we haven't had any hope
for years now?

344
00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:12.540
- You can't say that to her.
- We got to say it!

345
00:18:12.660 --> 00:18:16.410
- How are you going to prove it?
- For God's sake. Three years?

346
00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:20.540
Nobody comes back after three years.
It's insane.

347
00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:22.790
To you it is. And to me.

348
00:18:23.370 --> 00:18:24.500
But not to her.

349
00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:26.790
Now, look, you can talk yourself
blue in the face,

350
00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:30.870
but there's no body and there's no grave,
so where are ya?

351
00:18:31.000 --> 00:18:32.910
Sit down, Dad.
I want to talk to you.

352
00:18:33.040 --> 00:18:35.160
The trouble is
the goddamn newspapers.

353
00:18:35.290 --> 00:18:37.750
Every month some boy turns up
from nowhere,

354
00:18:37.870 --> 00:18:40.040
so: "Oh, next one's gonna be Larry."

355
00:18:40.160 --> 00:18:42.620
- All right, all right, just listen to me.
- Oh.

356
00:18:44.540 --> 00:18:48.750
You know why I asked Annie here,
don't you?

357
00:18:51.750 --> 00:18:52.750
Why?

358
00:18:53.370 --> 00:18:54.790
You... You know.

359
00:18:54.910 --> 00:18:57.160
Well, I got an idea,
but what's the story?

360
00:19:00.040 --> 00:19:01.910
I'm going to ask her to marry me.

361
00:19:03.370 --> 00:19:05.290
Oh, well, that's only your business.

362
00:19:05.410 --> 00:19:07.040
Dad, you know
it's not only my business.

363
00:19:07.160 --> 00:19:10.500
What do you want me to do?
You're old enough to know your own mind.

364
00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:12.540
Then it's all right?
I'll go ahead with it?

365
00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:15.120
You just have to make sure
that Mother isn't shocked.

366
00:19:15.250 --> 00:19:17.540
- Then it isn't just my business.
- I'm just saying!

367
00:19:17.660 --> 00:19:19.410
(Mocking him) I'm just saying!

368
00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:22.160
Sometimes you infuriate me,
you know that?

369
00:19:22.290 --> 00:19:24.540
Isn't it your business too
if I tell this to Mother

370
00:19:24.660 --> 00:19:26.910
and she throws a fit about it?

371
00:19:27.040 --> 00:19:29.290
You have such a talent
for ignoring things.

372
00:19:29.410 --> 00:19:32.040
Well, I ignore
what I gotta ignore.

373
00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:35.870
- Look, the girl is Larry's girl.
- She's not Larry's girl!

374
00:19:36.000 --> 00:19:39.160
From Mother's point of view,
he is not dead

375
00:19:39.290 --> 00:19:42.500
and you got no right
to take his girl.

376
00:19:42.620 --> 00:19:45.620
Now, you can go on from there
if you know where to go,

377
00:19:45.750 --> 00:19:47.790
but I'm tellin' you,
I don't know where to go.

378
00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:50.290
You see? I don't know.

379
00:19:51.540 --> 00:19:54.250
Hey, come on.
What can I do for you?

380
00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:58.290
I don't know why it is, but every time
I reach out for something I want,

381
00:19:58.410 --> 00:20:00.910
I have to pull back
because other people will suffer.

382
00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:04.040
My whole bloody life,
time after time after time.

383
00:20:04.160 --> 00:20:07.870
That is because
you're a very considerate fella.

384
00:20:08.000 --> 00:20:11.290
- And there's nothing wrong in that.
- To hell with that.

385
00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:16.410
Did you ask Annie yet, huh?

386
00:20:16.540 --> 00:20:18.540
I... I wanted
to get this settled first.

387
00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:20.540
Well, how do you know
she'll marry you?

388
00:20:20.660 --> 00:20:23.250
Maybe she feels
the same way Mother does.

389
00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:27.120
Well, if she does, then...
yeah, that's the end of it.

390
00:20:27.250 --> 00:20:28.660
- Well...
- (Audience laughs)

391
00:20:28.790 --> 00:20:31.750
But from her letters, I think
she's forgotten him. I'll find out.

392
00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:34.910
And then we'll thrash it out
with Mother, right? Dad?

393
00:20:35.410 --> 00:20:38.660
- Don't avoid me.
- The trouble is you don't see enough women!

394
00:20:38.790 --> 00:20:41.620
- You never did.
- So what? I'm not fast with women.

395
00:20:41.750 --> 00:20:43.660
I just don't see
why it has to be Annie.

396
00:20:43.790 --> 00:20:44.910
Because it is!

397
00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:46.410
That's a...

398
00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:49.370
- No, that's a good answer.
- (Audience laughs)

399
00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:51.290
But, really,
it don't answer anything.

400
00:20:51.410 --> 00:20:54.750
I mean, you haven't seen her
since you went off to war.

401
00:20:54.870 --> 00:20:56.290
It's, what, five years?

402
00:20:56.410 --> 00:21:00.500
I can't help it. I know her best.
I was brought up next door to her.

403
00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:04.910
These years when I think of someone
for my wife, I think of Annie.

404
00:21:05.040 --> 00:21:06.790
What do you want, a diagram?

405
00:21:06.910 --> 00:21:08.660
(Soft music continues)

406
00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:12.000
No, I don't want a diagram, Chris.
Li... Listen.

407
00:21:14.040 --> 00:21:17.160
She thinks he's coming back.
Huh? No?

408
00:21:18.120 --> 00:21:20.370
You marry that girl,

409
00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:22.790
you are pronouncing him dead.

410
00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:25.040
Now, what's going
to happen to Mother?

411
00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:27.910
Do you know? I don't.

412
00:21:31.290 --> 00:21:33.620
- All right, then, Dad.
- Give it some more thought.

413
00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.000
Yeah, I've given it
three years of thought.

414
00:21:36.540 --> 00:21:39.410
I'd hoped that if I waited,
Mother would forget Larry

415
00:21:39.540 --> 00:21:42.160
and then we'd have a regular wedding
and everything happy.

416
00:21:42.290 --> 00:21:46.910
But if that can't happen here,
I'll have to get out.

417
00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:48.910
- What the hell is this?
- I'll get married,

418
00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:50.790
live someplace else,
maybe New York.

419
00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:53.540
- Are you crazy?
- I've been a good son too long, good sucker.

420
00:21:53.660 --> 00:21:55.870
- I am through with it.
- You've got a business here.

421
00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:59.540
- What the hell is this?
- The business! The business doesn't inspire me!

422
00:21:59.660 --> 00:22:02.910
Oh! Must you be inspired?

423
00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:05.040
Yeah.

424
00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:08.250
I like it an hour a day.

425
00:22:08.370 --> 00:22:10.790
If I have to grub for money
all day long,

426
00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:13.910
at least at evening
I want it beautiful.

427
00:22:14.040 --> 00:22:15.540
I want a family, I want some kids,

428
00:22:15.660 --> 00:22:17.910
I want to build something
I can give myself to.

429
00:22:18.040 --> 00:22:20.160
Annie is in the middle of that.
Where do I find it?

430
00:22:20.290 --> 00:22:22.620
Tell me something.
You mean you'd leave the business?

431
00:22:22.750 --> 00:22:25.660
- Yeah. On this I would.
- You don't want to think like that!

432
00:22:25.790 --> 00:22:28.290
- Then help me stay here.
- I will, but don't think like that.

433
00:22:28.410 --> 00:22:31.750
Because what the hell did I work for?
It's only for you, Chris.

434
00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:34.660
- The whole shootin' match is for you.
- I know that, Dad!

435
00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:36.660
- Just you help me stay here.
- I will! But don't think that way.

436
00:22:36.790 --> 00:22:38.500
I am thinking that way!

437
00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:40.290
(Both grunting)

438
00:22:40.410 --> 00:22:42.040
(Audience laughs)

439
00:22:43.620 --> 00:22:44.620
(Chris mumbles)

440
00:22:44.750 --> 00:22:46.160
(Both laugh)

441
00:22:55.790 --> 00:22:56.790
Come on.

442
00:22:59.040 --> 00:23:02.540
Aw, God.
I don't understand you at all, do I?

443
00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:04.790
No.

444
00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:08.250
- You don't.
- (Both chuckle)

445
00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:10.040
Ow!

446
00:23:10.540 --> 00:23:12.540
- I'm a pretty tough guy.
- I can see that.

447
00:23:12.660 --> 00:23:14.370
- Back off.
- You want a piece of me?

448
00:23:14.500 --> 00:23:16.040
- Joe?
- Hello, Mom.

449
00:23:16.160 --> 00:23:18.160
- Get away from me!
- Joe!

450
00:23:18.910 --> 00:23:21.000
Did you take a bag
from under the sink?

451
00:23:21.120 --> 00:23:22.540
Yeah, I put it in the pail.

452
00:23:22.660 --> 00:23:25.370
Well, get it out of the pail.
That's my potatoes.

453
00:23:25.500 --> 00:23:27.750
- (Men laugh)
- I thought it was garbage.

454
00:23:27.870 --> 00:23:30.000
- Will you do me a favor, Joe?
- Huh?

455
00:23:30.120 --> 00:23:31.290
Don't be helpful.

456
00:23:31.410 --> 00:23:32.870
(Chris laughs)

457
00:23:33.000 --> 00:23:34.040
Shh!

458
00:23:34.160 --> 00:23:36.370
Look, I can afford
another bag of potatoes.

459
00:23:36.500 --> 00:23:39.500
Minnie scoured that pail
in boiling water last night.

460
00:23:39.620 --> 00:23:40.910
It's cleaner than your teeth.

461
00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:43.790
All right,
and I don't understand why,

462
00:23:43.910 --> 00:23:47.160
after I worked 40 years
and I got a maid,

463
00:23:47.290 --> 00:23:49.120
why I have to take out the garbage.

464
00:23:49.250 --> 00:23:50.410
If you would make up your mind

465
00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:53.410
that every bag in the kitchen
isn't full of garbage,

466
00:23:53.540 --> 00:23:55.040
you wouldn't be throwing out
my vegetables.

467
00:23:55.160 --> 00:23:56.540
Last time it was the onions.

468
00:23:56.660 --> 00:23:58.540
Well, I don't like garbage
in the house.

469
00:23:58.660 --> 00:24:00.410
Then don't eat.

470
00:24:01.910 --> 00:24:03.120
(Joe sighs)

471
00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:05.910
- That settles you for today.
- Yeah.

472
00:24:06.040 --> 00:24:09.250
- I'm in last place once again.
- Mm-hm.

473
00:24:09.370 --> 00:24:14.540
You know, once upon a time, boy,
I thought that when I got money again,

474
00:24:14.660 --> 00:24:18.660
I'd have a maid
and my wife would take it easy.

475
00:24:19.790 --> 00:24:23.040
Now I got money, I got a maid,

476
00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:25.290
and my wife is workin' for the maid.

477
00:24:25.410 --> 00:24:26.660
- It's her day off.
- Jeez!

478
00:24:26.790 --> 00:24:28.910
- What are you crabbing about?
- Aahh!

479
00:24:29.040 --> 00:24:31.660
Hey, isn't...?
Isn't Annie finished eating?

480
00:24:31.790 --> 00:24:33.410
She'll be right out.

481
00:24:33.540 --> 00:24:34.540
Ooh.

482
00:24:35.160 --> 00:24:37.910
- The wind did some job on this place.
- Yeah.

483
00:24:38.790 --> 00:24:41.660
- So much for that, thank God.
- Sit down, take it easy.

484
00:24:41.790 --> 00:24:44.790
Uh-uh. I've got such a funny pain
on the top of my head.

485
00:24:44.910 --> 00:24:46.290
Can I get you an aspirin?

486
00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:47.540
(Sighs)

487
00:24:48.540 --> 00:24:49.540
No more roses.

488
00:24:50.500 --> 00:24:54.290
It's so funny. Everything decides
to happen at the same time.

489
00:24:54.410 --> 00:24:57.790
This month is his birthday,
his tree blows down, Annie comes.

490
00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:00.290
Everything that happened
seems to be coming back.

491
00:25:00.410 --> 00:25:02.790
I was just down the cellar
and what do I stumble over?

492
00:25:02.910 --> 00:25:04.790
- Huh?
- His baseball glove.

493
00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:06.660
- Oh.
- I haven't seen it in a century.

494
00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:10.620
Don't you think Annie looks... well?

495
00:25:10.750 --> 00:25:11.790
(Audience laughs)

496
00:25:11.910 --> 00:25:14.790
Fine. There's no question about it.
She's a beauty.

497
00:25:14.910 --> 00:25:18.040
I still don't know what brought her here.
Not that I'm not glad to see her.

498
00:25:18.160 --> 00:25:21.040
I just thought we'd all like
to see each other again.

499
00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.540
And I wanted to see her myself.

500
00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:27.910
The only thing is
I think her nose got longer.

501
00:25:28.040 --> 00:25:29.500
But I'll always love that girl.

502
00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:32.290
She was one that didn't jump into bed
with somebody else

503
00:25:32.410 --> 00:25:34.160
as soon as it happened
with her fella.

504
00:25:34.290 --> 00:25:38.040
- Oh, what are you talking about?
- Never mind. Most of them didn't wait.

505
00:25:38.160 --> 00:25:41.410
I'm just glad she came so you can see
I'm not completely out of my mind.

506
00:25:41.540 --> 00:25:44.660
Just 'cause she isn't married
doesn't mean she's been mourning Larry.

507
00:25:44.790 --> 00:25:48.910
- Why, then, isn't she?
- Well, could be any number of things.

508
00:25:49.040 --> 00:25:50.290
Like what, for instance?

509
00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:54.160
I... I... I don't know.

510
00:25:54.290 --> 00:25:57.660
Whatever it is,
can I get you an aspirin?

511
00:25:57.790 --> 00:26:00.290
(Audience laughs)

512
00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:03.910
- It's not like a headache.
- You don't sleep, that's why.

513
00:26:04.040 --> 00:26:07.160
She's wearing out
more bedroom slippers than shoes.

514
00:26:07.790 --> 00:26:09.540
I had a terrible night.

515
00:26:10.910 --> 00:26:12.370
I never had a night like that.

516
00:26:12.500 --> 00:26:13.910
What was it, Mom?
Did you dream?

517
00:26:15.120 --> 00:26:17.660
More. More than a dream.

518
00:26:18.910 --> 00:26:19.910
'Bout Larry?

519
00:26:23.160 --> 00:26:25.660
I was fast asleep, and...

520
00:26:27.660 --> 00:26:31.750
Remember the way he used to fly low
past the house when he was in training?

521
00:26:31.870 --> 00:26:33.290
(Chris) Mm-hm.

522
00:26:33.410 --> 00:26:37.040
When we used to see his face
in the cockpit as he was going by?

523
00:26:38.540 --> 00:26:40.040
That's the way I saw him.

524
00:26:40.750 --> 00:26:42.040
Only high up.

525
00:26:42.870 --> 00:26:45.540
Way, way up, where the clouds are.

526
00:26:46.660 --> 00:26:50.870
He was so real
I could reach out and touch him.

527
00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.500
(Melancholic humming and music)

528
00:26:53.620 --> 00:26:56.040
Suddenly, he started to fall.

529
00:26:57.000 --> 00:26:58.250
And crying.

530
00:26:59.040 --> 00:27:02.120
Crying to me: "Mom! Mom!"

531
00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:05.620
I... I could hear him
like he was in the room.

532
00:27:06.290 --> 00:27:07.290
"Mom!"

533
00:27:08.410 --> 00:27:09.910
It was his voice.

534
00:27:10.040 --> 00:27:14.370
I knew if I could touch him,
I could stop him.

535
00:27:14.500 --> 00:27:16.040
If only...

536
00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:22.660
I woke up and it was so funny,

537
00:27:22.790 --> 00:27:26.370
the wind was like
the roaring of his engine.

538
00:27:27.290 --> 00:27:30.540
I came out here,
I must've still been half asleep.

539
00:27:30.660 --> 00:27:34.790
I could just hear that roaring
like he was going by.

540
00:27:35.660 --> 00:27:40.410
The tree snapped right in front of me
and I, like, came awake.

541
00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:44.370
See?

542
00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:49.000
We should never
have planted that tree.

543
00:27:49.120 --> 00:27:51.410
I said so in the first place.

544
00:27:51.540 --> 00:27:54.790
- It was too soon to plant a tree for him.
- Too soon?

545
00:27:54.910 --> 00:27:56.250
We rushed into it.

546
00:27:56.370 --> 00:27:58.660
Everybody was in such a hurry
to bury him.

547
00:27:58.790 --> 00:28:01.120
I said not to plant it yet.
I told you to...

548
00:28:01.250 --> 00:28:03.540
The wind blew it down.
What significance has that got?

549
00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:05.250
What? What are you talking about?

550
00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:08.410
Mother, please,
don't go through it all again, will you?

551
00:28:08.540 --> 00:28:10.040
It's no good.

552
00:28:10.750 --> 00:28:12.410
- Huh?
- (Sighs)

553
00:28:12.540 --> 00:28:14.620
It doesn't accomplish anything.

554
00:28:17.620 --> 00:28:18.620
Mm?

555
00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:25.790
I've been thinking, maybe we ought
to put our minds to forgetting him.

556
00:28:25.910 --> 00:28:28.160
That's the third time
you've said that this week.

557
00:28:28.290 --> 00:28:32.250
Because it's not right.
We never took up our lives again.

558
00:28:32.370 --> 00:28:37.500
We're like at a railroad station
waiting for a train that never comes in.

559
00:28:37.620 --> 00:28:39.910
- Get me an aspirin.
- Yeah, sure.

560
00:28:41.000 --> 00:28:44.410
Sure. Let's break out of this, huh?

561
00:28:47.410 --> 00:28:50.660
Mom, I thought,
uh, maybe the four of us

562
00:28:50.790 --> 00:28:53.000
might go out to dinner.

563
00:28:53.500 --> 00:28:55.540
- A couple of nights, huh?
- (Joe) Yeah.

564
00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:57.910
- Maybe go dancing, huh?
- (Joe laughs)

565
00:28:58.040 --> 00:28:59.910
- Out at the shore.
- Fine!

566
00:29:00.040 --> 00:29:01.750
Huh? Fine?

567
00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:03.000
(Chris laughs)

568
00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:05.040
- We can do it tonight.
- Swell with me.

569
00:29:05.160 --> 00:29:07.790
Sure. We'll have some fun!

570
00:29:07.910 --> 00:29:08.910
(Joe laughs)

571
00:29:10.410 --> 00:29:12.540
You can start
with that aspirin, huh?

572
00:29:14.790 --> 00:29:15.790
(Joe) Aah.

573
00:29:18.000 --> 00:29:21.790
- Why did he invite her here?
- Oh, why does that bother you?

574
00:29:21.910 --> 00:29:24.040
She's been in New York
three and a half years.

575
00:29:24.160 --> 00:29:27.790
- Why all of a sudden?
- Well, maybe he just wanted to see her.

576
00:29:27.910 --> 00:29:30.790
Nobody comes 700 miles
"just to see".

577
00:29:30.910 --> 00:29:34.290
What do you mean?
He lived next door to that girl all his life.

578
00:29:34.410 --> 00:29:36.160
Why shouldn't he want
to see her again?

579
00:29:36.290 --> 00:29:39.540
Oh, no, no, no.
Don't you look at me that way.

580
00:29:39.660 --> 00:29:42.160
He didn't tell me any more
than he told you.

581
00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:43.410
(Audience laughs)

582
00:29:43.540 --> 00:29:46.370
- He's not going to marry her.
- How do you know he's thinking of it?

583
00:29:46.500 --> 00:29:47.750
It's got that about it.

584
00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:49.790
- Well? So what?
- Oh, what's going on here, Joe?

585
00:29:49.910 --> 00:29:53.370
- Now listen, kid...
- She's not his girl. She knows she's not.

586
00:29:53.500 --> 00:29:56.500
- You can't read her mind.
- Then why is she still single?

587
00:29:56.620 --> 00:29:59.910
New York is full of men.
Why isn't she married?

588
00:30:00.040 --> 00:30:03.410
Probably a hundred people told her
she's foolish, but she waited.

589
00:30:03.540 --> 00:30:06.870
- How do you know why she waited?
- She knows what I know, that's why.

590
00:30:07.000 --> 00:30:09.250
She's faithful as a rock.

591
00:30:09.370 --> 00:30:13.290
In my worst moments, I think of her waiting,
and I know again that I'm right.

592
00:30:13.410 --> 00:30:15.540
All right, but look,
it's a beautiful day, huh?

593
00:30:15.660 --> 00:30:17.750
What are we arguing for?
Come on.

594
00:30:17.870 --> 00:30:21.040
Nobody in this house
dast take her faith away, Joe.

595
00:30:21.160 --> 00:30:24.000
Strangers might,
but not his father, not his brother.

596
00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:25.910
What do you want me to do?
What do you want?

597
00:30:26.040 --> 00:30:29.660
I want you to act
like he's coming back, both of you.

598
00:30:29.790 --> 00:30:32.160
Don't think I haven't noticed you
since Chris invited her.

599
00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:34.290
- I won't stand for any nonsense.
- Kate...

600
00:30:34.410 --> 00:30:36.750
Because if he's not coming back,

601
00:30:37.410 --> 00:30:38.790
then I'll kill myself.

602
00:30:38.910 --> 00:30:40.290
- Oh.
- Yeah, laugh.

603
00:30:40.410 --> 00:30:44.410
Laugh at me. But why does that happen
the very night she came back?

604
00:30:44.540 --> 00:30:47.750
Laugh, but there are meanings
to such things.

605
00:30:47.870 --> 00:30:51.750
She goes to bed in his room
and his memorial breaks in pieces?

606
00:30:51.870 --> 00:30:54.660
- Look at it! Look, Joe!
- All right. Calm yourself.

607
00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:57.910
Believe with me, Joe!
I can't stand all alone.

608
00:30:58.040 --> 00:30:59.660
- Calm yourself.
- Only last week

609
00:30:59.790 --> 00:31:02.540
a man turned up in Detroit,
missing longer than Larry.

610
00:31:02.660 --> 00:31:04.500
- You read it yourself.
- Alright. Calm yourself.

611
00:31:04.620 --> 00:31:08.250
- You above all have got to believe.
- Why me above all?

612
00:31:08.370 --> 00:31:11.790
- What...?
- Just don't stop believing.

613
00:31:11.910 --> 00:31:14.000
No, no. What's that mean,
me above all?

614
00:31:14.120 --> 00:31:17.000
Mr. Keller! Say, Mr. Keller,
Tommy just said it again.

615
00:31:17.120 --> 00:31:19.410
- Said what? Who?
- The dirty word.

616
00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:21.290
Aren't you going to arrest him?
I warned him.

617
00:31:21.410 --> 00:31:23.790
Stop that! Go home, Bert.
There's no jail here.

618
00:31:23.910 --> 00:31:25.540
- Kate...
- There's no jail here!

619
00:31:25.660 --> 00:31:27.160
I want you to stop that jail business.

620
00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:29.290
- He's right across the street.
- Go home, Bert!

621
00:31:29.410 --> 00:31:32.290
I want you to stop that, Joe,
that whole jail business.

622
00:31:32.410 --> 00:31:34.870
- Look at you. Look at you, you're shaking!
- I can't help it.

623
00:31:35.000 --> 00:31:38.540
What have I got to hide, Kate?
What the hell is the matter with you?

624
00:31:41.040 --> 00:31:43.160
I didn't say
you had anything to hide.

625
00:31:43.290 --> 00:31:46.500
I'm just telling you to stop it.
Now, stop it.

626
00:31:46.620 --> 00:31:48.160
- All right.
- Hiya, Joe.

627
00:31:48.290 --> 00:31:50.250
(Chris) Take a breath of that air, kid.

628
00:31:50.370 --> 00:31:52.160
You never get air like that
in New York.

629
00:31:52.290 --> 00:31:54.750
Annie, where did you get that dress?

630
00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:57.910
Oh, I couldn't resist.
I'm taking it right off before I ruin it.

631
00:31:58.040 --> 00:31:59.660
How's that for three weeks' salary?

632
00:31:59.790 --> 00:32:01.540
Isn't she the most?

633
00:32:01.660 --> 00:32:03.790
- It's gorgeous. Simply gorgeous.
- (Chris) No kidding.

634
00:32:03.910 --> 00:32:05.870
Isn't she the prettiest girl
you ever saw?

635
00:32:07.410 --> 00:32:09.910
Gained a little weight,
didn't you, darling?

636
00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:11.290
(Laughter)

637
00:32:11.410 --> 00:32:13.370
It comes and goes.

638
00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:16.160
Look how nice
her legs turned out!

639
00:32:17.790 --> 00:32:19.500
(Audience laughs)

640
00:32:19.620 --> 00:32:21.500
Boy, the poplars got thick,
didn't they?

641
00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:24.160
Huh? Oh, yeah. Well, you know,
it's been three years, Annie.

642
00:32:24.290 --> 00:32:25.370
We're gettin' old, kid.

643
00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:27.250
How does your mom like New York?

644
00:32:27.370 --> 00:32:31.120
- Why'd they take our hammock away?
- They didn't. It broke a couple of years ago.

645
00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:32.250
What broke?

646
00:32:32.370 --> 00:32:34.910
He had one of his light lunches
and flopped into it.

647
00:32:35.040 --> 00:32:36.540
(All laugh)

648
00:32:37.790 --> 00:32:39.120
(Ann) Oh. Uh...

649
00:32:39.250 --> 00:32:40.660
- Excuse me.
- How do you do?

650
00:32:41.370 --> 00:32:43.540
Oh, she looks very intelligent.

651
00:32:43.660 --> 00:32:46.370
Ann, Ann, this is Jim, Dr. Bayliss.

652
00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:48.750
Oh, sure, he, uh...
He writes a lot about you.

653
00:32:48.870 --> 00:32:52.870
Oh, don't believe it.
He likes everybody.

654
00:32:53.000 --> 00:32:55.660
In the battalion he was known
as Mother McKeller.

655
00:32:55.790 --> 00:32:57.410
Oh, I can believe it.

656
00:32:58.040 --> 00:32:59.040
You know,

657
00:33:00.120 --> 00:33:03.120
it is so strange seeing him
come out of that yard.

658
00:33:03.790 --> 00:33:05.290
I guess I never grew up.

659
00:33:06.160 --> 00:33:09.750
It almost seems that Mom and Pop
are in there now and I...

660
00:33:09.870 --> 00:33:13.910
And you and my brother doing algebra,
and Larry trying to copy my homework.

661
00:33:15.040 --> 00:33:17.870
Gosh, those dear dead days
beyond recall.

662
00:33:18.000 --> 00:33:20.660
Well, I hope that doesn't mean
you want me to move out?

663
00:33:21.290 --> 00:33:23.410
Jim, come in here.
Mr. Hubbard is on the phone.

664
00:33:23.540 --> 00:33:26.000
- I told you I don't want to speak to...
- Dear, please.

665
00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:29.540
(Sighs) All right, Susie.
All right. All right.

666
00:33:30.870 --> 00:33:35.500
Ann, I've only met you,
but if I may offer you a piece of advice,

667
00:33:35.620 --> 00:33:39.290
when you marry,
never, even in your mind,

668
00:33:39.410 --> 00:33:42.250
never count your husband's money.

669
00:33:42.750 --> 00:33:43.910
Jim!

670
00:33:44.040 --> 00:33:46.290
At once. At once.

671
00:33:46.410 --> 00:33:49.120
(Kate laughs)

672
00:33:49.250 --> 00:33:51.410
I told her to take up the guitar.

673
00:33:53.410 --> 00:33:55.790
It'd be a common interest for them.

674
00:33:55.910 --> 00:33:57.660
Well, he loves the guitar.

675
00:34:01.790 --> 00:34:04.040
Hey! Hey, hey, hey, uh...

676
00:34:04.160 --> 00:34:06.660
Let's, uh... Let's eat
at the shore tonight, huh?

677
00:34:06.790 --> 00:34:09.160
Raise some hell like we used to
before Larry went...

678
00:34:10.540 --> 00:34:11.620
You think of him.

679
00:34:12.620 --> 00:34:15.370
- You see? She thinks of him.
- What do you mean, Kate?

680
00:34:15.500 --> 00:34:18.620
Nothing, dear, just that you remember him,
he's in your thoughts.

681
00:34:18.750 --> 00:34:22.290
That... That is a funny thing to say.
How could I help remembering him?

682
00:34:22.410 --> 00:34:24.790
- Did you hang up your things?
- Yeah. Say, you...

683
00:34:24.910 --> 00:34:26.370
You have sure gone in for clothes.

684
00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:28.290
- I could hardly find room in the closet.
- No!

685
00:34:28.410 --> 00:34:30.620
Don't you remember?
That's Larry's room.

686
00:34:31.790 --> 00:34:34.790
- Oh, you mean they're Larry's?
- Didn't you recognize them?

687
00:34:35.660 --> 00:34:38.660
Well, no, it never occurred to me.
I mean...

688
00:34:39.410 --> 00:34:42.160
- The shoes are all shined.
- Yes, dear.

689
00:34:45.160 --> 00:34:48.790
For so long I've been aching
for a nice conversation with you, Annie.

690
00:34:48.910 --> 00:34:50.290
- Tell me something.
- What?

691
00:34:50.410 --> 00:34:53.620
- I don't know. Something nice.
- She means: "Do you go out much?"

692
00:34:53.750 --> 00:34:55.410
Oh, shut up!

693
00:34:56.040 --> 00:34:57.790
(Joe) "And are any of them serious?"

694
00:34:57.910 --> 00:35:00.910
Oh, why don't you both choke?

695
00:35:01.540 --> 00:35:02.660
I'm telling you, Annie,

696
00:35:02.790 --> 00:35:05.790
you can't go into a restaurant
with this woman anymore.

697
00:35:05.910 --> 00:35:09.790
In five minutes, 39 strange people
are sitting at the table

698
00:35:09.910 --> 00:35:11.290
telling her their life story.

699
00:35:11.410 --> 00:35:14.540
- If I can't ask Annie a personal question...
- Askin' is all right,

700
00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:16.290
but don't beat her over the head.

701
00:35:16.410 --> 00:35:20.540
- You were beatin' her.
- Don't let them bulldoze you.

702
00:35:20.660 --> 00:35:22.870
Oh, you ask me anything you like.

703
00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:25.790
What do you want to know, Kate?
Come on. Come on, let's gossip.

704
00:35:25.910 --> 00:35:28.620
- She's the only one is got any sense.
- Ha.

705
00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:31.040
- Your mother.
- Mm?

706
00:35:31.160 --> 00:35:32.910
She's not getting a divorce.

707
00:35:33.540 --> 00:35:37.500
No. No, she's, uh...
She's calmed down about it now.

708
00:35:37.620 --> 00:35:40.290
I think when he gets out,
they'll probably live together.

709
00:35:40.410 --> 00:35:42.750
- In New York, of course.
- That's fine.

710
00:35:42.870 --> 00:35:44.410
Because your father's still...

711
00:35:44.540 --> 00:35:46.910
I mean, he's a decent man
when all is said and done.

712
00:35:47.040 --> 00:35:49.370
I don't care.
She can take him back if she likes.

713
00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:53.540
And you? You go out much?

714
00:35:53.660 --> 00:35:55.660
(Audience laughs)

715
00:35:55.790 --> 00:35:58.370
You mean,
am I still waiting for him?

716
00:35:59.500 --> 00:36:01.660
Well, no, I don't expect you
to wait for him, but...

717
00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:04.040
But that is what you mean, isn't it?

718
00:36:05.250 --> 00:36:06.290
Well, yeah.

719
00:36:08.120 --> 00:36:09.250
Well, I'm not, Kate.

720
00:36:10.870 --> 00:36:11.870
You're not?

721
00:36:12.910 --> 00:36:14.790
Isn't it ridiculous?
You don't really imagine...

722
00:36:14.910 --> 00:36:17.790
I know, dear, but don't say that it's ridiculous,
the papers are full of it.

723
00:36:17.910 --> 00:36:19.790
I don't know about New York,

724
00:36:19.910 --> 00:36:24.000
but there was a half a page about a man
missing even longer than Larry

725
00:36:24.120 --> 00:36:25.160
turned up from Burma.

726
00:36:25.290 --> 00:36:28.500
Well, he couldn't have wanted
to come home very badly, Mom.

727
00:36:28.620 --> 00:36:31.410
- Don't be so smart.
- You can have a helluva time in Burma.

728
00:36:31.540 --> 00:36:32.790
So I've heard.

729
00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:36.120
I'll bet you money you're the only woman
in the country who, after three years...

730
00:36:36.250 --> 00:36:39.160
- You're sure?
- Yeah, I am.

731
00:36:39.290 --> 00:36:42.250
Ha. Well, if you're sure,
then you're sure.

732
00:36:43.120 --> 00:36:46.120
They don't say it on the radio,
but I'm sure

733
00:36:46.250 --> 00:36:50.540
that in the dark at night
they're still waiting for their sons.

734
00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:54.410
- Mom, you're absolutely...
- Don't be so damned smart! Now, stop it.

735
00:36:57.370 --> 00:37:00.750
There are just a few things
that you don't know. All of ya.

736
00:37:01.370 --> 00:37:02.790
And I'll tell you one of them.

737
00:37:03.790 --> 00:37:05.250
- Annie.
- Mm?

738
00:37:05.910 --> 00:37:08.660
Deep, deep in your heart...

739
00:37:09.790 --> 00:37:12.040
...you've always been waiting for him.

740
00:37:12.910 --> 00:37:14.370
No, Kate.

741
00:37:15.410 --> 00:37:17.500
But deep in your heart, Annie.

742
00:37:17.620 --> 00:37:21.540
- She ought to know, shouldn't she?
- Don't let them tell you what to think.

743
00:37:21.660 --> 00:37:24.620
Listen to your heart.
Only your heart.

744
00:37:25.870 --> 00:37:27.660
Why does your heart tell you
he's alive?

745
00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:30.660
Because he has to be.

746
00:37:30.790 --> 00:37:32.660
But... But why, Kate?

747
00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:36.500
Because certain things have to be
and certain things can never be.

748
00:37:37.040 --> 00:37:40.290
Like the sun has to be, has to rise.

749
00:37:40.410 --> 00:37:43.370
That's why there's God.
Otherwise, anything could happen.

750
00:37:43.500 --> 00:37:47.290
But there's God,
so certain things can never happen.

751
00:37:48.160 --> 00:37:50.750
Ann, I would know.

752
00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:55.120
Just like I knew the day
he went into that terrible battle.

753
00:37:55.250 --> 00:37:56.660
Did he write me?

754
00:37:56.790 --> 00:37:58.660
Was it in the papers? No.

755
00:37:58.790 --> 00:38:02.250
But that morning,
I couldn't raise my head from the pillow.

756
00:38:02.370 --> 00:38:05.660
Ask Joe.
Suddenly, I knew. I knew!

757
00:38:06.410 --> 00:38:09.660
And that day he was nearly killed.

758
00:38:10.540 --> 00:38:13.870
Ann... you know I'm right.

759
00:38:16.250 --> 00:38:17.750
No, Kate.

760
00:38:21.540 --> 00:38:22.790
I have to have some tea.

761
00:38:22.910 --> 00:38:24.000
Annie?

762
00:38:24.120 --> 00:38:26.870
How are ya? Gee whiz!

763
00:38:27.000 --> 00:38:30.660
- Why, Frank, you've lost so much weight!
- He's got responsibility.

764
00:38:30.790 --> 00:38:32.660
Gee whiz! Oh! (Chuckles)

765
00:38:33.290 --> 00:38:35.910
Without Frank, the stars wouldn't know
when to come out.

766
00:38:37.540 --> 00:38:40.160
God, you look more womanly.

767
00:38:40.290 --> 00:38:42.750
- Oh.
- I mean, you have matured.

768
00:38:42.870 --> 00:38:44.910
- Hey, hey, hey, Frank!
- Oh.

769
00:38:45.040 --> 00:38:47.750
- Take it easy, you're a married man!
- (Ann laughs)

770
00:38:47.870 --> 00:38:50.290
Hey, you still haberdashering?

771
00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:51.910
Oh. Well, why not?

772
00:38:52.040 --> 00:38:55.000
- Maybe I too can get to be president.
- (Ann) Oh.

773
00:38:55.120 --> 00:38:58.160
How's your brother?
He got his degree, I hear.

774
00:38:58.290 --> 00:39:01.410
- Oh, George has his own office now.
- Don't say!

775
00:39:01.540 --> 00:39:02.540
And...

776
00:39:04.160 --> 00:39:06.540
And your dad, is he...?

777
00:39:06.660 --> 00:39:08.040
Fine. I'll be in to see Lydia.

778
00:39:08.160 --> 00:39:11.500
Wh...? How about it?
Does Dad expect a parole soon?

779
00:39:11.620 --> 00:39:14.660
- Oh, I really don't know.
- I mean, because I feel, you know,

780
00:39:14.790 --> 00:39:18.870
that if an intelligent man like your father
is put in prison,

781
00:39:19.000 --> 00:39:22.160
well, there ought to be a law
to say either you execute him

782
00:39:22.290 --> 00:39:24.790
- or let him go after a year.
- Whoa! Whoa! Whoa! Hey!

783
00:39:24.910 --> 00:39:27.410
Hey, you want a hand
with that ladder?

784
00:39:28.290 --> 00:39:29.540
No, that's all right...

785
00:39:29.660 --> 00:39:31.410
Oh! Sure! Um... Yeah.

786
00:39:31.540 --> 00:39:34.660
Sorry. I'll get that horoscope
to you tonight, Kate.

787
00:39:37.410 --> 00:39:39.500
See you later, Ann.

788
00:39:39.620 --> 00:39:41.910
- God, you look wonderful.
- Oh.

789
00:39:42.040 --> 00:39:43.540
(Whispering)

790
00:39:43.660 --> 00:39:44.870
(Ann) Huh.

791
00:39:48.040 --> 00:39:50.540
Haven't they stopped talking
about Dad?

792
00:39:50.660 --> 00:39:53.790
- Uh... Nobody talks about him anymore.
- Gone and forgotten, kid.

793
00:39:53.910 --> 00:39:56.500
Tell me. I don't want to meet
anybody on the block if...

794
00:39:56.620 --> 00:39:58.500
I don't want you to worry about it.

795
00:39:59.910 --> 00:40:01.750
- Do they, uh...?
- Uh.

796
00:40:01.870 --> 00:40:03.910
Do they...?
Do they remember the case, Joe?

797
00:40:04.040 --> 00:40:05.910
Hey. Do they still talk about you?

798
00:40:07.040 --> 00:40:12.040
Well, the only one
who still talks about it is, uh...

799
00:40:12.160 --> 00:40:14.120
- (Whistles) ...my wife.
- Oh.

800
00:40:14.250 --> 00:40:16.910
That's because you keep on
playing policeman with the kids.

801
00:40:17.040 --> 00:40:19.290
- That's true.
- All their parents hear out of you

802
00:40:19.410 --> 00:40:21.540
is jail, jail, jail.

803
00:40:21.660 --> 00:40:26.040
Actually, what happened was
that when I come home from the penitentiary,

804
00:40:26.160 --> 00:40:29.040
the kids got very interested in me.

805
00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:30.620
I mean, you know kids.

806
00:40:30.750 --> 00:40:35.410
I became like the...
the expert on the jail situation.

807
00:40:35.540 --> 00:40:38.620
Ooh. But as time passed,
they got it confused

808
00:40:38.750 --> 00:40:41.540
and somehow I ended up a detective.

809
00:40:41.660 --> 00:40:44.160
- Except they did not get it confused.
- What?

810
00:40:44.290 --> 00:40:47.790
He hands out police badges
from the Post Toasties boxes.

811
00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:49.910
To look official.

812
00:40:50.040 --> 00:40:53.290
Gosh, it is wonderful
to hear you laughing about it.

813
00:40:53.410 --> 00:40:55.750
Why, why? What'd you expect?

814
00:40:55.870 --> 00:40:59.040
Well, the last thing that I remember
on this block was one word:

815
00:40:59.790 --> 00:41:00.790
"murderers".

816
00:41:02.040 --> 00:41:05.040
Do you...?
Do you remember that, Kate?

817
00:41:05.160 --> 00:41:08.000
Mrs. Hammond standing
in front of our house yelling that word.

818
00:41:08.120 --> 00:41:10.410
- She's still around, I suppose?
- They're all still around.

819
00:41:10.540 --> 00:41:12.160
Don't listen to her.

820
00:41:12.290 --> 00:41:17.120
Every Saturday, the whole gang
is playin' poker right here in this arbor.

821
00:41:17.250 --> 00:41:21.250
Oh. All the ones who yelled "murderer"
are takin' my money now.

822
00:41:21.370 --> 00:41:23.290
- Oh, don't, Joe.
- Yeah.

823
00:41:23.410 --> 00:41:27.540
She's a sensitive girl. Don't fool her.
They all still remember about Dad.

824
00:41:27.660 --> 00:41:30.790
It's different for him. He was exonerated.
Your father's still there.

825
00:41:30.910 --> 00:41:33.120
- Listen...
- That's why I wasn't so enthusiastic

826
00:41:33.250 --> 00:41:34.250
about your coming.

827
00:41:34.370 --> 00:41:36.790
- Listen...
- Honestly, I know how sensitive you are.

828
00:41:36.910 --> 00:41:39.160
- Listen...
- And I told Chris, I said...

829
00:41:39.290 --> 00:41:40.540
Listen, listen, listen.

830
00:41:40.660 --> 00:41:43.290
You do like I did
and you'll be all right.

831
00:41:43.410 --> 00:41:46.410
Look, the day I come home,
I get out of the car, I...

832
00:41:46.540 --> 00:41:49.790
But not in front of the house.
No, no. On the corner.

833
00:41:50.370 --> 00:41:54.040
Oh, you should've been here, Annie,
and you too, Chris. Oh!

834
00:41:54.160 --> 00:41:56.870
Oh, you'd-a seen something.
(Laughs)

835
00:41:57.000 --> 00:41:59.540
Everybody knew
I was getting out that day.

836
00:41:59.660 --> 00:42:02.000
The porches were loaded.

837
00:42:03.040 --> 00:42:04.410
Picture it now.

838
00:42:04.540 --> 00:42:06.410
No one believed I was innocent.

839
00:42:06.540 --> 00:42:11.040
The story goes that I pulled a fast one
to get exonerated.

840
00:42:11.160 --> 00:42:13.870
So... I get out of the car,

841
00:42:14.000 --> 00:42:16.540
and I walk down the street, but...

842
00:42:17.540 --> 00:42:19.910
...very slow.

843
00:42:22.750 --> 00:42:24.160
And with a smile.

844
00:42:26.910 --> 00:42:29.160
"Hey." (Laughs)

845
00:42:29.910 --> 00:42:31.620
"Ah."

846
00:42:34.500 --> 00:42:35.500
Oh.

847
00:42:39.540 --> 00:42:40.540
The beast.

848
00:42:42.660 --> 00:42:44.290
I was the beast.

849
00:42:46.290 --> 00:42:50.160
The guy who sold cracked cylinder heads
to the Army Air Force.

850
00:42:51.290 --> 00:42:55.250
The guy who made 21 P-40s
crash in Australia.

851
00:42:57.120 --> 00:43:01.160
Kid, I'll tell you,
walkin' down the street that day, boy,

852
00:43:01.790 --> 00:43:03.660
I was guilty as hell.

853
00:43:05.410 --> 00:43:07.040
Except I wasn't.

854
00:43:07.790 --> 00:43:10.620
And there was a court paper
in my pocket

855
00:43:10.750 --> 00:43:12.500
to prove that I wasn't.

856
00:43:13.370 --> 00:43:14.410
(Smacks lips)

857
00:43:14.540 --> 00:43:16.910
So, I walked past the porches.

858
00:43:17.040 --> 00:43:18.250
"Hey."

859
00:43:18.370 --> 00:43:19.410
(Chuckles)

860
00:43:19.540 --> 00:43:20.870
Result?

861
00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:23.540
Fourteen months later, oh, boy,

862
00:43:23.660 --> 00:43:27.540
I had one of the best shops
in the state again!

863
00:43:27.660 --> 00:43:30.790
Woo! A respected man again,
bigger than ever.

864
00:43:30.910 --> 00:43:32.160
(Chris) Joe McGuts!

865
00:43:32.290 --> 00:43:34.410
That's the only way you lick 'em,
is with guts!

866
00:43:34.540 --> 00:43:35.910
(Chris) Yeah.

867
00:43:36.500 --> 00:43:39.250
The worst thing you did, Annie,
was move away from here.

868
00:43:39.370 --> 00:43:42.370
You... You made it tough
for your father when he gets out.

869
00:43:42.500 --> 00:43:43.750
And that's why I tell you,

870
00:43:43.870 --> 00:43:46.540
I'd like to see him move back,
right on this block.

871
00:43:46.660 --> 00:43:48.290
How could they move back?

872
00:43:48.410 --> 00:43:50.750
It ain't gonna end
till they move back.

873
00:43:50.870 --> 00:43:53.620
Till... people play cards
with him again.

874
00:43:53.750 --> 00:43:55.160
And... And talk with him.

875
00:43:55.290 --> 00:43:57.620
And... And smile with him.

876
00:43:58.290 --> 00:44:02.660
You play cards with a man,
you know he can't be a murderer.

877
00:44:04.620 --> 00:44:05.910
And the next time you write Dad,

878
00:44:06.040 --> 00:44:07.910
I want you to tell him just what I said.
You hear me?

879
00:44:08.040 --> 00:44:12.750
- Don't you hold anything against him?
- I never believed in crucifying people.

880
00:44:12.870 --> 00:44:15.870
No, but he was your partner.
He dragged you through the mud.

881
00:44:16.000 --> 00:44:19.120
I mean, he ain't my sweetheart,
but you gotta forgive, don't you?

882
00:44:19.250 --> 00:44:22.290
- You either, Kate? You don't feel...?
- The next time you write Dad...

883
00:44:22.410 --> 00:44:25.540
- No, I don't write him.
- I mean, every now and then...

884
00:44:25.660 --> 00:44:28.410
I've never written. Neither has my brother.
Do you feel this way too?

885
00:44:28.540 --> 00:44:32.160
- He murdered 21 pilots.
- What the hell kinda talk is that?

886
00:44:32.290 --> 00:44:34.620
What else can you say about him?

887
00:44:35.500 --> 00:44:38.040
When they took him away,
I followed him.

888
00:44:38.160 --> 00:44:41.660
I went to him every visiting day.
I was crying all the time.

889
00:44:42.750 --> 00:44:44.790
Until the news came about Larry.

890
00:44:45.620 --> 00:44:49.870
Then I realized it is...
it is wrong to pity a man like that.

891
00:44:50.000 --> 00:44:53.290
Father or no father,
there is only one way to look at him.

892
00:44:53.410 --> 00:44:56.750
He knowingly shipped out parts
that would crash an airplane.

893
00:44:56.870 --> 00:44:58.660
How do you know
Larry wasn't one of them?

894
00:44:58.790 --> 00:45:00.790
- No.
- I was waiting for that.

895
00:45:02.120 --> 00:45:04.040
As long as you're here, Annie,

896
00:45:04.160 --> 00:45:06.160
I want to ask you
to never say that again.

897
00:45:06.290 --> 00:45:09.040
You... You surprise me.
I thought you'd be mad at him.

898
00:45:09.160 --> 00:45:12.000
What your father did
had nothing to do with Larry. Nothing.

899
00:45:12.120 --> 00:45:14.660
- We can't know that.
- As long as you're here. Put it out of your head!

900
00:45:14.790 --> 00:45:16.660
- 'Cause remember...
- That's all, enough.

901
00:45:16.790 --> 00:45:18.370
Come inside have some tea with me.

902
00:45:18.500 --> 00:45:21.870
- Yeah, yeah, yeah. But Annie...
- He's not dead!

903
00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:25.250
- So there's no argument. Now, come!
- In a minute!

904
00:45:26.410 --> 00:45:29.290
- Now look, Annie, you gotta remember...
- All right. Stop that, Dad.

905
00:45:29.410 --> 00:45:30.540
No, no, no, no.

906
00:45:30.660 --> 00:45:33.290
- She doesn't feel that way!
- I'm sick of the whole subject.

907
00:45:33.410 --> 00:45:35.790
- You want her to go on like that?
- Cut it out!

908
00:45:35.910 --> 00:45:39.660
Those cylinder heads
went into P-40s only.

909
00:45:39.790 --> 00:45:42.540
What's the matter with you?
You know Larry never flew a P-40.

910
00:45:42.660 --> 00:45:45.370
So... So, who flew those P-40s?

911
00:45:45.500 --> 00:45:46.790
Uh... Pigs?

912
00:45:48.410 --> 00:45:51.250
The man was a fool, but don't you
make a murderer out of him.

913
00:45:51.370 --> 00:45:54.370
What, you got no sense?
Look what it does to her!

914
00:45:54.870 --> 00:45:58.410
Listen, you gotta appreciate
what was doin' in that shop in the war.

915
00:45:58.540 --> 00:46:01.160
The both of ya.
It was a madhouse!

916
00:46:02.040 --> 00:46:05.410
Every 30 minutes
the major callin' for cylinder heads!

917
00:46:05.540 --> 00:46:08.290
They were whippin' us
with the telephone!

918
00:46:08.410 --> 00:46:11.540
The trucks were hauling 'em away hot,
damn near.

919
00:46:13.290 --> 00:46:16.000
I mean, just try to see it human.

920
00:46:16.660 --> 00:46:18.120
See it human.

921
00:46:19.910 --> 00:46:22.870
So, all of a sudden,
a batch come out with a crack?

922
00:46:23.000 --> 00:46:24.870
That happens, that's the business.

923
00:46:25.000 --> 00:46:28.290
Just a... a fine, hairline crack.

924
00:46:31.000 --> 00:46:34.660
All right, so...
so he's a little man, your father.

925
00:46:34.790 --> 00:46:36.540
Always scared of loud voices.

926
00:46:36.660 --> 00:46:39.410
"Oh! What'll the major say?"

927
00:46:39.540 --> 00:46:42.790
"Half a day's production shot.
What'll I say?"

928
00:46:42.910 --> 00:46:45.290
You know what I mean?
Human. Human.

929
00:46:48.410 --> 00:46:51.660
So, he... He takes out his tools

930
00:46:51.790 --> 00:46:53.370
and he covers over the cracks.

931
00:46:53.500 --> 00:46:55.910
OK, that's bad, it's wrong.

932
00:46:56.040 --> 00:46:58.500
But that is what a little man does.

933
00:46:58.620 --> 00:47:01.040
Now, if I could have gone in that day,
I'd have told him:

934
00:47:01.160 --> 00:47:03.370
"Junk 'em, Steve, we can afford it."

935
00:47:03.500 --> 00:47:05.620
But alone he was afraid.

936
00:47:05.750 --> 00:47:08.290
But I know he didn't mean no harm.

937
00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:11.410
He believed they'd hold up,
a hundred percent!

938
00:47:11.540 --> 00:47:13.410
Now, that's a mistake,
but it ain't murder.

939
00:47:13.540 --> 00:47:16.160
And you mustn't feel that way
about him. That ain't right.

940
00:47:16.290 --> 00:47:18.540
- Joe, let's forget it.
- No. Come here. Listen.

941
00:47:20.910 --> 00:47:23.540
The day the news came about Larry

942
00:47:24.410 --> 00:47:26.910
he was in the cell next to mine, Dad.

943
00:47:28.910 --> 00:47:30.790
And he cried, Annie.

944
00:47:31.410 --> 00:47:34.120
- He cried half the night.
- Mm.

945
00:47:35.790 --> 00:47:37.750
He shoulda cried all night.

946
00:47:39.540 --> 00:47:41.660
- Why do you wanna do that?
- Are you gonna stop?

947
00:47:41.790 --> 00:47:43.040
(Ann) Don't!

948
00:47:45.040 --> 00:47:47.540
Don't yell at him. He just...

949
00:47:49.040 --> 00:47:52.790
Oh, he, uh...
He just wants everybody happy.

950
00:47:54.250 --> 00:47:56.910
- Right?
- Yeah, that's my sentiments.

951
00:47:58.540 --> 00:48:00.040
(Mumbles) Aw.

952
00:48:07.290 --> 00:48:09.000
Hey. Oh, oh, oh. Hey.

953
00:48:09.870 --> 00:48:11.290
Can you stand steak?

954
00:48:11.410 --> 00:48:12.750
- Ha!
- Huh?

955
00:48:12.870 --> 00:48:14.160
Huh? Huh?

956
00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:17.370
- And champagne.
- Now you're operatin'!

957
00:48:17.500 --> 00:48:20.370
OK. I'm gonna call Swanson's
and get ourselves a table.

958
00:48:20.500 --> 00:48:22.250
We have got
a big night tonight, Annie!

959
00:48:22.370 --> 00:48:23.790
Can't scare me.

960
00:48:23.910 --> 00:48:26.910
Ooh, I like that girl.
Wrap her up.

961
00:48:27.040 --> 00:48:28.790
- (Ann) Ha.
- (Joe chuckles)

962
00:48:28.910 --> 00:48:31.290
- You know, you got nice legs, Annie.
- Joe!

963
00:48:31.410 --> 00:48:34.790
Sorry! I want to see
everybody drunk tonight.

964
00:48:35.660 --> 00:48:37.250
Look at him, he's blushin'.

965
00:48:37.370 --> 00:48:40.790
- All right. Drink your tea, Casanova.
- (Laughing) OK.

966
00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:48.250
Isn't he a great guy?

967
00:48:49.040 --> 00:48:50.040
Ha.

968
00:48:50.660 --> 00:48:53.160
You are the only one I know
who loves his parents.

969
00:48:53.290 --> 00:48:55.000
(Laughs)

970
00:48:55.120 --> 00:48:56.500
- I know.
- Hm.

971
00:48:57.290 --> 00:48:58.870
It went out of style, didn't it?

972
00:49:00.410 --> 00:49:02.160
It's all right. It's a good thing.

973
00:49:02.750 --> 00:49:04.500
(Soft instrumental music)

974
00:49:10.250 --> 00:49:14.370
You know, it is lovely here.
The, uh... The air is sweet.

975
00:49:15.750 --> 00:49:18.500
You're, uh...
You're not sorry you came?

976
00:49:20.290 --> 00:49:24.540
Not sorry, no, but...
I'm not going to stay.

977
00:49:25.790 --> 00:49:26.790
What? Why?

978
00:49:27.540 --> 00:49:30.160
In the first place,
your mother as much as told me to go.

979
00:49:30.290 --> 00:49:32.160
- Well...
- You saw that. And then you...

980
00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:33.660
- You've been kind of...
- What?

981
00:49:34.870 --> 00:49:35.870
Well...

982
00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:39.040
kind of embarrassed
ever since I got here.

983
00:49:41.750 --> 00:49:44.410
I... The... The trouble is

984
00:49:44.540 --> 00:49:47.120
I planned on kind of...

985
00:49:47.910 --> 00:49:51.290
sneaking up on you
over a period of a week or so.

986
00:49:52.290 --> 00:49:55.290
But they take it for granted
that we're all set.

987
00:49:55.410 --> 00:49:57.290
Oh, I knew they would.
Your mother anyway.

988
00:49:57.410 --> 00:49:58.750
How did you know?

989
00:49:59.500 --> 00:50:01.620
From her point of view,
why else would I come?

990
00:50:03.540 --> 00:50:04.540
Well...

991
00:50:05.290 --> 00:50:06.660
Would you want to?

992
00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:10.660
I guess you know
this is why I asked you to come.

993
00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:15.660
I guess this is why I came.

994
00:50:18.790 --> 00:50:20.410
(Audience laughs)

995
00:50:27.410 --> 00:50:28.750
Uh... Annie, Annie.

996
00:50:30.290 --> 00:50:31.540
I love you.

997
00:50:37.250 --> 00:50:38.250
I love you.

998
00:50:39.910 --> 00:50:41.040
A great deal.

999
00:50:46.870 --> 00:50:48.040
I love you.

1000
00:50:52.750 --> 00:50:54.750
I have no imagination, that's all.

1001
00:50:55.290 --> 00:50:58.250
I... I... I know to tell you...

1002
00:50:58.370 --> 00:51:01.540
I'm embarrassing you, I...
I didn't want to tell it to you here.

1003
00:51:01.660 --> 00:51:03.790
I wanted some place
we'd never been before,

1004
00:51:03.910 --> 00:51:06.290
a place where we'd be brand new
to each other.

1005
00:51:06.410 --> 00:51:09.410
You feel it's wrong here, don't you?
This... This yard.

1006
00:51:11.790 --> 00:51:14.040
I want you to be ready for me.

1007
00:51:14.160 --> 00:51:16.790
I don't want to win you away
from anything.

1008
00:51:32.540 --> 00:51:33.620
(Ann) Oh.

1009
00:51:34.250 --> 00:51:35.410
(Clears her throat)

1010
00:51:38.160 --> 00:51:41.540
Chris, I've been ready
a long, long time.

1011
00:51:43.790 --> 00:51:45.120
Then he's gone.

1012
00:51:46.870 --> 00:51:48.790
Forever. You're...? You're sure?

1013
00:51:50.160 --> 00:51:52.290
I almost got married two years ago.

1014
00:51:53.660 --> 00:51:56.160
What...? What...?
Why didn't you?

1015
00:51:56.660 --> 00:51:58.500
You started to write me.

1016
00:51:59.540 --> 00:52:01.660
You...? You felt something
that far back?

1017
00:52:01.790 --> 00:52:03.290
Every day since!

1018
00:52:04.120 --> 00:52:06.250
Annie, why didn't you let me know?

1019
00:52:06.370 --> 00:52:08.040
I was waiting for you, Chris.

1020
00:52:08.160 --> 00:52:10.660
Till then, you never wrote.
And when you did, what did you say?

1021
00:52:10.790 --> 00:52:14.370
God, you sure can be ambiguous,
you know?

1022
00:52:18.290 --> 00:52:19.790
Well, give me a kiss.

1023
00:52:19.910 --> 00:52:21.660
(Quiet laughter in audience)

1024
00:52:22.910 --> 00:52:23.910
Annie.

1025
00:52:32.290 --> 00:52:33.870
Give me a...

1026
00:52:35.540 --> 00:52:37.000
(Soft music continues)

1027
00:53:08.910 --> 00:53:10.910
(Audience laughs)

1028
00:53:13.660 --> 00:53:14.660
God.

1029
00:53:22.410 --> 00:53:24.000
I kissed you, Annie.

1030
00:53:25.120 --> 00:53:26.500
I kissed Annie.

1031
00:53:28.000 --> 00:53:31.040
How long, how long
I've been waiting to kiss you.

1032
00:53:31.160 --> 00:53:34.040
I will never forgive you.
Why did you wait all these years?

1033
00:53:34.160 --> 00:53:36.750
All I've done is sit and wonder
if I was crazy for thinking of you.

1034
00:53:36.870 --> 00:53:39.910
No, Annie, Annie, Annie.
We're going to live now.

1035
00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:42.370
- Oh.
- I'm going to make you so happy.

1036
00:53:45.620 --> 00:53:47.160
(Audience laughs)

1037
00:53:51.410 --> 00:53:53.540
Not like that you're not.

1038
00:53:54.660 --> 00:53:56.370
- I kissed you.
- Mm.

1039
00:53:58.160 --> 00:53:59.750
Like Larry's brother.

1040
00:54:01.000 --> 00:54:02.000
Do it...

1041
00:54:03.160 --> 00:54:05.540
Do it like you, Chris.

1042
00:54:11.250 --> 00:54:12.410
What is it, Chris?

1043
00:54:14.290 --> 00:54:17.620
I... Let's drive some place.
I want to be alone with you.

1044
00:54:17.750 --> 00:54:19.620
No. What is it, Chris?
Is it your mother?

1045
00:54:19.750 --> 00:54:22.250
- No, nothing like that.
- Then what's wrong?

1046
00:54:22.870 --> 00:54:26.040
Even in your letters,
there was something... ashamed.

1047
00:54:26.160 --> 00:54:29.160
Yeah. I suppose I have been.
But it's going from me.

1048
00:54:29.290 --> 00:54:31.160
- You got to tell me.
- I don't know how to start.

1049
00:54:31.290 --> 00:54:34.620
- It would never work this way.
- It's all mixed up with so many other things.

1050
00:54:37.160 --> 00:54:41.250
You remember, overseas,
I was in command of a company?

1051
00:54:42.040 --> 00:54:43.040
Yeah, sure.

1052
00:54:44.910 --> 00:54:46.250
Well, I lost them.

1053
00:54:46.910 --> 00:54:47.910
How many?

1054
00:54:49.160 --> 00:54:51.790
- Just about all.
- Oh, gee.

1055
00:54:51.910 --> 00:54:55.040
Yeah, it takes a little time
to... toss that off

1056
00:54:55.160 --> 00:54:58.160
because they weren't just men.

1057
00:55:00.660 --> 00:55:02.370
For instance, um...

1058
00:55:03.160 --> 00:55:05.750
One time,
it'd been raining several days

1059
00:55:05.870 --> 00:55:07.660
and this kid came to me,

1060
00:55:07.790 --> 00:55:11.790
and... and he gave me
his last pair of dry socks.

1061
00:55:11.910 --> 00:55:14.290
- Ha.
- He just put them in my pocket.

1062
00:55:16.540 --> 00:55:20.790
It's only a little thing, but...
that's the kind of guys I had,

1063
00:55:20.910 --> 00:55:25.040
they didn't die,
they... killed themselves for each other.

1064
00:55:25.160 --> 00:55:26.160
I mean that exactly.

1065
00:55:26.290 --> 00:55:30.000
A little more selfish
and they'd have been here today.

1066
00:55:32.410 --> 00:55:35.870
And I... I got an idea

1067
00:55:36.000 --> 00:55:37.160
watching them go down.

1068
00:55:37.290 --> 00:55:39.620
Everything was...
was being destroyed, see,

1069
00:55:39.750 --> 00:55:42.000
but it seemed to me
that one new thing was made,

1070
00:55:42.120 --> 00:55:44.910
a kind of a... a responsibility.

1071
00:55:45.040 --> 00:55:47.750
Man for man.
You understand me?

1072
00:55:47.870 --> 00:55:49.290
To show that,

1073
00:55:49.410 --> 00:55:52.040
to bring that onto the earth again

1074
00:55:52.160 --> 00:55:53.750
like some kind of a monument

1075
00:55:53.870 --> 00:55:57.410
and everyone would feel it
standing there, behind him,

1076
00:55:57.540 --> 00:55:59.910
and it would make
a difference to him.

1077
00:56:04.410 --> 00:56:07.040
And then I came home

1078
00:56:07.160 --> 00:56:10.040
and it was... incredible.

1079
00:56:10.160 --> 00:56:12.750
I... There was no meaning
in it here.

1080
00:56:12.870 --> 00:56:15.410
The whole thing to them
was a kind of a bus accident.

1081
00:56:15.540 --> 00:56:18.790
I went to work with Dad,
and that rat race again.

1082
00:56:18.910 --> 00:56:21.120
And I felt... Uh...

1083
00:56:21.250 --> 00:56:23.410
What you said, ashamed somehow

1084
00:56:23.540 --> 00:56:26.660
'cause... 'cause nobody was changed!

1085
00:56:27.540 --> 00:56:31.620
Like, at all! It seemed
to make suckers out of a lot of guys.

1086
00:56:31.750 --> 00:56:34.540
I felt wrong to be alive,
to open the bankbook,

1087
00:56:34.660 --> 00:56:37.500
to drive a new car,
to see a new refrigerator.

1088
00:56:37.620 --> 00:56:39.620
You can take those things
out of a war,

1089
00:56:39.750 --> 00:56:43.370
but when you drive that car,
you've got to know that it came

1090
00:56:43.500 --> 00:56:45.870
out of the love
a man can have for a man.

1091
00:56:46.000 --> 00:56:48.410
You've got to be a little better
because of that.

1092
00:56:49.040 --> 00:56:51.160
Otherwise, what you have is really...

1093
00:56:52.160 --> 00:56:53.160
...loot.

1094
00:56:54.120 --> 00:56:56.160
And there's blood on it.

1095
00:56:58.790 --> 00:57:00.620
I didn't want to take any of it.

1096
00:57:02.250 --> 00:57:05.910
And I guess that included you.

1097
00:57:07.160 --> 00:57:08.160
Hm.

1098
00:57:10.540 --> 00:57:12.370
And you still feel that way?

1099
00:57:14.660 --> 00:57:17.250
I want you now, Annie.

1100
00:57:19.290 --> 00:57:21.620
Because you mustn't feel
that way anymore.

1101
00:57:22.160 --> 00:57:25.250
Because you...
you have a right to whatever you have.

1102
00:57:25.370 --> 00:57:27.370
Everything, Chris.
You understand that?

1103
00:57:27.500 --> 00:57:29.500
Hey. To me too.

1104
00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:32.040
And, uh... And the money?

1105
00:57:32.660 --> 00:57:34.290
There is nothing wrong
in your money.

1106
00:57:34.410 --> 00:57:36.540
Your father put hundreds of planes
in the air.

1107
00:57:36.660 --> 00:57:38.040
You should be proud.

1108
00:57:38.750 --> 00:57:40.660
A man should be paid for that.

1109
00:57:43.790 --> 00:57:45.120
Oh, Annie.

1110
00:57:45.910 --> 00:57:46.910
Yeah?

1111
00:57:48.290 --> 00:57:50.750
Annie, I'm going
to make a fortune for you.

1112
00:57:52.790 --> 00:57:54.790
What'll I do with a fortune?

1113
00:58:00.000 --> 00:58:02.250
Annie, your brother's...
Oh, sheesh!

1114
00:58:02.370 --> 00:58:04.000
(Audience laughs)

1115
00:58:04.410 --> 00:58:07.250
What is this, Labor Day?

1116
00:58:07.370 --> 00:58:10.790
- All right, all right.
- You shouldn't burst out like that!

1117
00:58:10.910 --> 00:58:13.910
- No one told me it was Labor Day.
- All right! You said it once.

1118
00:58:14.040 --> 00:58:16.620
- Where are the hot dogs?
- (Chris) Oh, my God.

1119
00:58:16.750 --> 00:58:18.410
As long as I know it's Labor Day,

1120
00:58:18.540 --> 00:58:21.040
from now on,
I'll just wear a bell around my neck.

1121
00:58:21.160 --> 00:58:24.370
- Oh, he's so subtle.
- George Bernard Shaw as an elephant.

1122
00:58:24.500 --> 00:58:27.660
It's George. You kissed it out of my head.
Your brother's on the phone.

1123
00:58:27.790 --> 00:58:29.790
- My brother?
- Yeah, George. Long-distance.

1124
00:58:29.910 --> 00:58:31.620
What's the matter?
Is anything wrong?

1125
00:58:31.750 --> 00:58:35.160
I don't know, Kate is talking to him.
Hurry, she'll cost him five dollars.

1126
00:58:35.290 --> 00:58:39.000
Yeah. Oh. Hey, hey. I wonder
if we ought to tell your mother yet.

1127
00:58:39.120 --> 00:58:41.000
I mean, I'm not very good
in an argument.

1128
00:58:41.120 --> 00:58:43.540
We'll wait till tonight.

1129
00:58:44.120 --> 00:58:46.290
After dinner. Don't get tense.

1130
00:58:46.870 --> 00:58:49.290
I... Oh. Leave it to me.

1131
00:58:49.410 --> 00:58:53.040
- What are you gonna tell her?
- Go, go, go! Go ahead, Ann.

1132
00:58:57.620 --> 00:58:59.160
(Quiet laughter in audience)

1133
00:59:00.290 --> 00:59:01.660
We're getting married, Dad.

1134
00:59:03.290 --> 00:59:04.290
Ah.

1135
00:59:06.370 --> 00:59:07.870
Well, don't you say anything?

1136
00:59:08.000 --> 00:59:10.370
Oh, God. Oh. No, I'm...

1137
00:59:10.500 --> 00:59:12.410
I'm glad for you, Chris.

1138
00:59:12.540 --> 00:59:14.910
Yeah. It's... It's just that, uh...

1139
00:59:15.870 --> 00:59:17.540
George, he's calling from Columbus.

1140
00:59:19.040 --> 00:59:20.040
- Columbus?
- Yeah.

1141
00:59:21.160 --> 00:59:25.160
Now, did Annie tell you that her brother
was going to see his father today?

1142
00:59:25.290 --> 00:59:28.410
Uh... No. No. I don't think
she knew anything about it.

1143
00:59:30.540 --> 00:59:32.750
Chris, you... you think
you know her pretty good?

1144
00:59:33.750 --> 00:59:36.910
- What kind of a question...?
- No, I'm just wondering, see.

1145
00:59:37.040 --> 00:59:41.040
'Cause all these years
George don't go see his father.

1146
00:59:41.660 --> 00:59:44.910
Suddenly he goes, she comes here.

1147
00:59:45.040 --> 00:59:46.040
Uh...

1148
00:59:46.160 --> 00:59:47.160
Well, what about it?

1149
00:59:47.290 --> 00:59:49.410
I mean, it's crazy,
but it does come to my mind.

1150
00:59:49.540 --> 00:59:51.540
You don't think
she holds nothin' against me, do you?

1151
00:59:51.660 --> 00:59:54.750
- I don't know what you're talking about.
- No. I'm just... talkin'.

1152
00:59:54.870 --> 00:59:58.160
To his last last day in court,
the man blamed it all on me.

1153
00:59:58.290 --> 00:59:59.790
And this is his daughter.

1154
01:00:00.790 --> 01:00:03.120
I mean, if she was sent here
to find out something...

1155
01:00:03.250 --> 01:00:04.870
Why? What is there to find out?

1156
01:00:05.000 --> 01:00:07.540
Why are you so excited, George?
What happened there?

1157
01:00:07.660 --> 01:00:11.540
If they want to open up the case again
for the nuisance value, to hurt us.

1158
01:00:11.660 --> 01:00:13.540
Dad, how could you think that of her?

1159
01:00:13.660 --> 01:00:15.160
- It couldn't be, huh?
- Dad!

1160
01:00:15.290 --> 01:00:17.290
- All right.
- You amaze me.

1161
01:00:17.410 --> 01:00:19.000
- OK, forget it.
- Come on.

1162
01:00:19.120 --> 01:00:20.540
Hey! Come on! Hey!

1163
01:00:20.660 --> 01:00:23.410
I want a clean start for you, Chris.
OK? Oh. Ooh.

1164
01:00:23.540 --> 01:00:26.500
I'm gonna put a new sign
over the plant:

1165
01:00:26.620 --> 01:00:28.370
Christopher Keller, Incorporated.

1166
01:00:28.500 --> 01:00:31.500
"J. O. Keller" is good enough.

1167
01:00:31.620 --> 01:00:33.250
We'll talk about it, eh?

1168
01:00:33.370 --> 01:00:34.790
(Both chuckle)

1169
01:00:34.910 --> 01:00:36.910
I'm going to build you a house.

1170
01:00:37.040 --> 01:00:41.290
Stone, with a...
With a driveway from the road.

1171
01:00:41.410 --> 01:00:45.910
I want you to spread out, Chris,
I want you to use what I made for you.

1172
01:00:46.040 --> 01:00:49.040
I mean, with joy.
Without shame. With joy.

1173
01:00:49.160 --> 01:00:50.370
(Somber music)

1174
01:00:50.500 --> 01:00:51.910
I will, Dad.

1175
01:00:52.040 --> 01:00:54.500
All right.
Would you just say it to me?

1176
01:00:55.910 --> 01:00:56.910
Why?

1177
01:00:58.040 --> 01:01:01.000
Because sometimes I think
you're ashamed of the money.

1178
01:01:01.120 --> 01:01:02.660
- No, don't feel that.
- It's good money.

1179
01:01:02.790 --> 01:01:04.250
There's nothing wrong
with that money.

1180
01:01:04.370 --> 01:01:06.910
- I don't need you to tell me this.
- All right. Yeah.

1181
01:01:07.040 --> 01:01:09.660
And I'm going to work on Mother
for you, OK?

1182
01:01:10.540 --> 01:01:14.160
We will get her so drunk tonight,
oh, boy,

1183
01:01:14.290 --> 01:01:15.910
we'll all get married.

1184
01:01:19.290 --> 01:01:20.290
Hey.

1185
01:01:21.160 --> 01:01:23.540
There's gonna be a wedding, kid.

1186
01:01:23.660 --> 01:01:25.290
(Chuckling) Ooh!

1187
01:01:26.160 --> 01:01:28.040
Like there never was seen.

1188
01:01:28.160 --> 01:01:30.540
With champagne and tuxedos!

1189
01:01:30.660 --> 01:01:33.870
Simply because when you get excited
you don't control yourself.

1190
01:01:34.910 --> 01:01:37.910
Well, what did Dad tell you,
for God's sake?

1191
01:01:39.540 --> 01:01:40.910
All right, come, then.

1192
01:01:42.120 --> 01:01:44.120
Yeah, they'll all be here.

1193
01:01:44.870 --> 01:01:47.000
No. Nobody is running away from you.

1194
01:01:47.120 --> 01:01:49.660
And... And try to get hold of yourself,
will you?

1195
01:01:50.790 --> 01:01:51.790
All right.

1196
01:01:52.790 --> 01:01:54.160
All right. Goodbye.

1197
01:01:54.290 --> 01:01:55.750
(Somber music continues)

1198
01:02:04.250 --> 01:02:07.290
- (Chris) Something happen?
- He's coming here, then, huh?

1199
01:02:07.410 --> 01:02:10.540
On the seven o'clock. He's in Columbus.
I told him that would be all right.

1200
01:02:10.660 --> 01:02:13.870
Yeah, yeah, sure. So...
So, what, your dad took sick or...?

1201
01:02:14.000 --> 01:02:17.040
Oh, no, no, George didn't say
he was sick. I, um...

1202
01:02:17.790 --> 01:02:20.790
I don't know. I don't know.
I suppose it's something stupid.

1203
01:02:20.910 --> 01:02:24.000
- You know my brother. Right?
- Yeah. Oh, yeah!

1204
01:02:24.120 --> 01:02:25.410
(Awkward laughter)

1205
01:02:25.540 --> 01:02:29.500
Um... Hey, let's, uh...
Let's go for a drive or something.

1206
01:02:29.620 --> 01:02:32.910
Oh. Oh. Ooh. Uh, sure.
I'll get the keys, Dad.

1207
01:02:33.040 --> 01:02:35.290
(Kate) Drive through the park.
It's beautiful now.

1208
01:02:35.410 --> 01:02:37.040
- See ya.
- Be back right away.

1209
01:02:37.160 --> 01:02:39.290
No, take your time, huh.

1210
01:02:39.790 --> 01:02:40.910
(Joe chuckles)

1211
01:02:41.750 --> 01:02:43.000
(Joe sniffs)

1212
01:02:43.790 --> 01:02:45.750
So, what did George want, huh?

1213
01:02:45.870 --> 01:02:48.750
He's been in Columbus
since this morning with Steve.

1214
01:02:48.870 --> 01:02:50.750
He's gotta see Annie right away,
he says.

1215
01:02:50.870 --> 01:02:53.500
- Oh? What for?
- I don't know.

1216
01:02:54.660 --> 01:02:56.750
- He's a lawyer now, Joe.
- Yeah.

1217
01:02:56.870 --> 01:03:01.660
George is a lawyer. All these years
he never even sent a postcard to Steve.

1218
01:03:01.790 --> 01:03:03.410
Since he got back from the war.

1219
01:03:03.540 --> 01:03:05.620
- Not a postcard.
- So what?

1220
01:03:05.750 --> 01:03:08.620
Suddenly he takes an airplane
from New York to see him?

1221
01:03:08.750 --> 01:03:11.500
- An airplane?
- Well? So?

1222
01:03:11.620 --> 01:03:14.660
- Why?
- Well, I don't read minds. Do you?

1223
01:03:14.790 --> 01:03:16.370
Why, Joe?

1224
01:03:16.500 --> 01:03:21.540
What has Steve suddenly got to tell him
that he has to take an airplane to see him?

1225
01:03:21.660 --> 01:03:24.160
What do I care
what Steve's got to tell him?

1226
01:03:24.290 --> 01:03:26.160
- You're sure, Joe?
- Yeah, I'm sure.

1227
01:03:26.910 --> 01:03:29.160
- Ha. Be smart now, Joe.
- Oh, please.

1228
01:03:29.290 --> 01:03:31.120
The boy is coming. Be smart.

1229
01:03:31.250 --> 01:03:34.290
Did you hear what I said?
Once and for all, I'm sure.

1230
01:03:34.410 --> 01:03:36.370
- All right, Joe.
- (Laughing) Jeez!

1231
01:03:37.290 --> 01:03:39.250
Just be smart.

1232
01:03:39.370 --> 01:03:41.910
(♪ Johnny Cash:
"God's Gonna Cut You Down")

1233
01:03:50.250 --> 01:03:53.790
♪ You can run on for a long time

1234
01:03:53.910 --> 01:03:56.750
♪ Run on for a long time

1235
01:03:56.870 --> 01:03:59.660
♪ Run on for a long time

1236
01:03:59.790 --> 01:04:02.540
♪ Sooner or later
God'll cut you down

1237
01:04:02.660 --> 01:04:05.660
♪ Sooner or later
God'll cut you down

1238
01:04:07.290 --> 01:04:10.160
♪ Go tell that long-tongue liar

1239
01:04:10.290 --> 01:04:13.040
♪ Go and tell that midnight rider

1240
01:04:13.160 --> 01:04:16.370
♪ Tell the rambler
the gambler, the back-biter

1241
01:04:16.500 --> 01:04:19.290
♪ Tell 'em that God's gonna
cut 'em down

1242
01:04:19.410 --> 01:04:22.750
♪ Tell 'em that God's gonna
cut 'em down

1243
01:04:27.120 --> 01:04:30.160
♪ Well, my goodness gracious
let me tell you the news

1244
01:04:30.290 --> 01:04:33.250
♪ My head's been wet
with the midnight dew

1245
01:04:33.370 --> 01:04:36.120
♪ I've been down on bended knee

1246
01:04:36.250 --> 01:04:39.000
♪ Talkin' to the man from Galilee ♪

1247
01:04:39.120 --> 01:04:41.410
You notice there's more light
with that thing gone.

1248
01:04:41.540 --> 01:04:43.120
Why aren't you dressing?

1249
01:04:43.250 --> 01:04:45.160
It's suffocating upstairs.

1250
01:04:45.290 --> 01:04:47.790
I made a grape drink for Georgie.
He always loved grape.

1251
01:04:47.910 --> 01:04:50.370
- Come on, have some.
- Well, come on, get dressed.

1252
01:04:50.500 --> 01:04:52.620
And what's Dad sleeping so much for?

1253
01:04:53.620 --> 01:04:54.620
He's worried.

1254
01:04:55.290 --> 01:04:56.750
When he's worried he sleeps.

1255
01:04:59.410 --> 01:05:01.540
- We're dumb, Chris.
- What?

1256
01:05:01.660 --> 01:05:04.660
Dad and I are stupid people.
We don't know anything.

1257
01:05:04.790 --> 01:05:06.040
You have got to protect us.

1258
01:05:06.160 --> 01:05:08.750
You're silly.
What's there to be afraid of?

1259
01:05:09.750 --> 01:05:12.040
To his last day in court,

1260
01:05:12.160 --> 01:05:15.500
Steve never gave up the idea
that Dad made him do it.

1261
01:05:15.620 --> 01:05:18.290
If they open up the case again,
I won't live through it.

1262
01:05:18.410 --> 01:05:21.540
George is just a damn fool, Mom.
How can you take him seriously?

1263
01:05:21.660 --> 01:05:23.160
That family hates us!

1264
01:05:23.290 --> 01:05:24.910
- Maybe even Annie.
- Oh, come on!

1265
01:05:25.040 --> 01:05:27.620
You think because you like everybody,
they like you?

1266
01:05:27.750 --> 01:05:31.160
All right, stop working yourself up.
Just leave everything to me.

1267
01:05:31.290 --> 01:05:33.870
When George goes home,
tell her to go with him.

1268
01:05:34.000 --> 01:05:35.120
Don't worry about Annie.

1269
01:05:35.250 --> 01:05:37.620
- Steve's her father too!
- Are you going to cut it out?

1270
01:05:37.750 --> 01:05:40.040
You just don't realize
how people can hate, Chris.

1271
01:05:40.160 --> 01:05:43.040
They can hate so much
they'll tear the world to pieces.

1272
01:05:43.160 --> 01:05:46.660
Look, look, look.
She's dressed already.

1273
01:05:46.790 --> 01:05:49.410
I just got to put on a shirt.

1274
01:05:49.540 --> 01:05:52.790
- Are you feeling well, Kate?
- What's the difference, dear?

1275
01:05:53.790 --> 01:05:55.910
There are certain people,
you know,

1276
01:05:57.000 --> 01:06:00.790
the sicker they get,
the longer they live.

1277
01:06:07.540 --> 01:06:09.910
- You look nice.
- We're going to tell her tonight.

1278
01:06:10.040 --> 01:06:12.660
- Absolutely, don't worry.
- I wish we could tell her now.

1279
01:06:12.790 --> 01:06:15.040
I can't stand scheming.
My stomach gets hard.

1280
01:06:15.160 --> 01:06:17.500
It's not scheming,
we'll get her in a better mood.

1281
01:06:17.620 --> 01:06:20.290
(Kate) Joe!
Are you going to sleep all day?

1282
01:06:20.410 --> 01:06:23.120
The only one who's relaxed is your father.
He's fast asleep.

1283
01:06:23.250 --> 01:06:25.290
- I'm relaxed.
- Are you?

1284
01:06:25.910 --> 01:06:26.910
Mm-hm.

1285
01:06:27.540 --> 01:06:28.540
Look.

1286
01:06:40.160 --> 01:06:42.120
Let me know
when George gets here.

1287
01:06:48.790 --> 01:06:50.540
(Slow intriguing music)

1288
01:07:30.790 --> 01:07:32.790
- Is my husband...?
- (Gasps) Oh!

1289
01:07:32.910 --> 01:07:36.790
- Oh, God.
- I am terribly sorry. Oh.

1290
01:07:36.910 --> 01:07:39.000
It's all right. Ooh.

1291
01:07:39.120 --> 01:07:40.410
Oh.

1292
01:07:40.540 --> 01:07:42.870
I'm a... I'm a little silly
about the dark.

1293
01:07:43.000 --> 01:07:45.790
- It is getting dark.
- Are you looking for your husband?

1294
01:07:45.910 --> 01:07:48.910
As usual. He spends so much time here,
they'll be charging him rent.

1295
01:07:49.040 --> 01:07:53.000
Oh. Nobody was dressed, so he drove
over to the depot to pick up my brother.

1296
01:07:53.120 --> 01:07:54.160
Oh, your brother's in?

1297
01:07:54.290 --> 01:07:56.410
Yeah, they ought to be here
any minute now.

1298
01:07:56.540 --> 01:08:00.250
Oh, my husband.
Too hot to drive me to the beach.

1299
01:08:00.370 --> 01:08:01.750
Men are like little boys.

1300
01:08:01.870 --> 01:08:04.040
For the neighbors,
they'll always cut the grass.

1301
01:08:04.160 --> 01:08:07.660
People like to do things for the Kellers.
It's been that way since I can remember.

1302
01:08:07.790 --> 01:08:09.410
It's amazing.

1303
01:08:09.540 --> 01:08:11.910
I guess your brother's coming
to give you away, huh?

1304
01:08:13.000 --> 01:08:15.370
Uh... I don't know. I suppose.

1305
01:08:15.500 --> 01:08:16.910
You must be all nerved up.

1306
01:08:18.160 --> 01:08:20.750
It is always a problem
getting yourself married, isn't it?

1307
01:08:21.540 --> 01:08:22.660
Depends on your shape, of course.

1308
01:08:22.790 --> 01:08:24.660
I don't see
why you should have had a problem.

1309
01:08:24.790 --> 01:08:27.540
- Oh. I've had chances.
- I'll bet.

1310
01:08:28.540 --> 01:08:30.290
- It's romantic.
- Oh.

1311
01:08:31.120 --> 01:08:34.870
It is very unusual to me,
marrying the brother of your sweetheart.

1312
01:08:36.410 --> 01:08:37.410
I don't know.

1313
01:08:38.870 --> 01:08:40.660
I think it's mostly, uh...

1314
01:08:41.370 --> 01:08:44.870
Whenever I need somebody
to tell me the truth,

1315
01:08:45.000 --> 01:08:47.660
- I have always thought of Chris.
- Mm.

1316
01:08:47.790 --> 01:08:50.620
When he tells you something,
you know it's so. He...

1317
01:08:51.410 --> 01:08:55.540
- Oh. He relaxes me.
- And he's got money. That's important, you know.

1318
01:08:55.660 --> 01:08:58.120
- It wouldn't matter to me.
- You'd be surprised.

1319
01:08:58.250 --> 01:09:00.040
It makes all the difference.

1320
01:09:00.160 --> 01:09:02.540
I married an intern.
On my salary.

1321
01:09:02.660 --> 01:09:04.040
That was bad.

1322
01:09:04.160 --> 01:09:07.500
As soon as a woman supports a man,
he owes her something.

1323
01:09:07.620 --> 01:09:10.160
You can never owe somebody
without resenting them.

1324
01:09:10.290 --> 01:09:12.160
Mm. It's true, you know.

1325
01:09:13.540 --> 01:09:17.040
Well, underneath,
I think the doctor is very devoted.

1326
01:09:17.160 --> 01:09:18.290
Oh, certainly.

1327
01:09:18.410 --> 01:09:21.540
But it's bad when a man always sees
the bars in front of him.

1328
01:09:21.660 --> 01:09:24.290
- Jim thinks he's in jail all the time.
- Oh.

1329
01:09:25.040 --> 01:09:28.500
That's why I've been intending
to ask you a small favor, Ann.

1330
01:09:28.620 --> 01:09:30.910
It's something very important to me.

1331
01:09:31.040 --> 01:09:32.040
(Ann mumbles)

1332
01:09:32.660 --> 01:09:34.410
Certainly, if I can do it.

1333
01:09:34.540 --> 01:09:35.910
- You can.
- Hm.

1334
01:09:36.620 --> 01:09:40.410
When you take up housekeeping,
try to find a place away from here.

1335
01:09:43.000 --> 01:09:45.250
- Are you fooling?
- I'm very serious.

1336
01:09:45.370 --> 01:09:47.250
My husband is unhappy
with Chris around.

1337
01:09:47.370 --> 01:09:49.500
- How is that?
- Jim is a successful doctor.

1338
01:09:49.620 --> 01:09:53.250
But... Look, he's got an idea
he'd like to do medical research...

1339
01:09:53.370 --> 01:09:55.870
- Discover things. See?
- Well, isn't that good?

1340
01:09:56.000 --> 01:10:00.290
Research pays 25 dollars a week.
Minus laundering the hair shirt.

1341
01:10:00.410 --> 01:10:03.120
- You got to give up your life to go into it.
- How does Chris...?

1342
01:10:03.250 --> 01:10:06.790
Chris makes people want to be better
than it is possible to be.

1343
01:10:06.910 --> 01:10:09.000
- Yep. He does that to people.
- Is that bad?

1344
01:10:09.540 --> 01:10:11.540
My husband has a family, dear.

1345
01:10:11.660 --> 01:10:14.790
Every time he has a session with Chris,
he feels he's compromising

1346
01:10:14.910 --> 01:10:17.160
by not giving up everything
for research.

1347
01:10:17.290 --> 01:10:19.660
As though Chris or anybody else
isn't compromising.

1348
01:10:19.790 --> 01:10:21.660
It happens with Jim
every couple of years.

1349
01:10:21.790 --> 01:10:24.620
He meets a man,
he makes a statue out of him.

1350
01:10:24.750 --> 01:10:27.160
Maybe he is right.
I don't mean Chris is a statue...

1351
01:10:27.290 --> 01:10:29.660
- Darling, you know he's not right.
- I don't agree with you. Chris...

1352
01:10:29.790 --> 01:10:31.910
Let's face it, dear.
Chris is working with his father, isn't he?

1353
01:10:32.040 --> 01:10:33.750
He's taking money out of that business
every week of the year.

1354
01:10:33.870 --> 01:10:36.540
- What of it?
- You ask me what of it?

1355
01:10:36.660 --> 01:10:38.370
I certainly do ask you.

1356
01:10:38.500 --> 01:10:41.410
You oughtn't cast aspersions like that.
I am surprised at you.

1357
01:10:41.540 --> 01:10:43.000
You're surprised at me.

1358
01:10:43.120 --> 01:10:45.910
He would never take five cents out of that plant
if there was anything wrong in it.

1359
01:10:46.040 --> 01:10:47.910
- You know that?
- I know it. I resent everything you've said.

1360
01:10:48.040 --> 01:10:50.040
- You know what I resent, dear?
- I don't want to argue.

1361
01:10:50.160 --> 01:10:52.910
I resent living next door
to the holy family!

1362
01:10:53.040 --> 01:10:56.120
- It makes me look like a bum, you understand?
- I can't do anything about that.

1363
01:10:56.250 --> 01:10:58.410
Who is he to ruin a man's life?

1364
01:10:58.540 --> 01:11:00.660
Everybody knows Joe pulled a fast one
to get out of jail.

1365
01:11:00.790 --> 01:11:03.750
- That is not true!
- Why don't you go out and talk to people?

1366
01:11:03.870 --> 01:11:07.250
Go on. There is not a person on the block
that doesn't know the truth.

1367
01:11:07.370 --> 01:11:09.910
That's a lie. People come here
all the time for cards.

1368
01:11:10.040 --> 01:11:12.160
So what?
They give him credit for being smart!

1369
01:11:12.290 --> 01:11:14.370
I do too.
Look, I've got nothing against Joe.

1370
01:11:14.500 --> 01:11:16.790
But if Chris wants people
to put on the hair shirt,

1371
01:11:16.910 --> 01:11:18.790
let him take off his broadcloth.

1372
01:11:18.910 --> 01:11:21.790
He's driving my husband crazy
with that phony idealism of his!

1373
01:11:21.910 --> 01:11:23.410
I'm at the end of my rope on it!

1374
01:11:25.660 --> 01:11:28.290
- (Sue) Hello, darling. How's Mother?

1375
01:11:28.410 --> 01:11:31.870
- I thought George came.
- No, it was just us.

1376
01:11:32.370 --> 01:11:35.040
Huh. Uh, do me a favor, huh, Susie.

1377
01:11:35.160 --> 01:11:38.410
Go up to Mother and see if you can
calm her. She's all worked up.

1378
01:11:38.540 --> 01:11:40.250
She still doesn't know
about you two?

1379
01:11:40.370 --> 01:11:42.870
(Awkward laugh) She senses it.

1380
01:11:43.410 --> 01:11:46.250
- I guess. You know my mother.
- Oh, yeah, she's psychic.

1381
01:11:46.370 --> 01:11:49.290
Yeah. Maybe there's something
in the medicine chest.

1382
01:11:49.410 --> 01:11:52.910
I'll give her one of everything.
And hey, don't worry about Kate.

1383
01:11:53.040 --> 01:11:55.000
Couple of drinks,
dance her around a little,

1384
01:11:55.120 --> 01:11:56.250
she will love Ann.

1385
01:11:57.660 --> 01:11:59.750
'Cause you're the female version of him.

1386
01:12:00.750 --> 01:12:01.910
Don't be alarmed.

1387
01:12:03.040 --> 01:12:04.410
I said "version".

1388
01:12:13.910 --> 01:12:15.870
Interesting woman, isn't she?

1389
01:12:16.410 --> 01:12:18.370
Yeah, she is very interesting.

1390
01:12:18.500 --> 01:12:20.870
- She's a great nurse, you know, she...
- Are you still doing that?

1391
01:12:21.000 --> 01:12:22.290
Doing what?

1392
01:12:22.410 --> 01:12:25.620
As soon as you get to know somebody,
you find a distinction for them.

1393
01:12:25.750 --> 01:12:28.410
- How do you know she's a great nurse?
- What's the matter?

1394
01:12:28.540 --> 01:12:29.910
Chris, that woman hates you.
She despises you.

1395
01:12:30.040 --> 01:12:31.160
What hits you?

1396
01:12:31.290 --> 01:12:34.290
- What happened here?
- Why didn't you tell me?

1397
01:12:34.910 --> 01:12:37.790
- Tell you what?
- She says they think Joe is guilty?

1398
01:12:39.910 --> 01:12:43.500
- What difference does it make?
- No. I don't care what they think.

1399
01:12:43.620 --> 01:12:45.660
I just don't understand
why you took the trouble to deny it.

1400
01:12:45.790 --> 01:12:46.790
You said it was all forgotten.

1401
01:12:46.910 --> 01:12:49.370
I didn't want you to feel there was
anything wrong in you coming here, that's all.

1402
01:12:49.500 --> 01:12:51.410
I know a lot of people think
my father was guilty.

1403
01:12:51.540 --> 01:12:53.250
I assumed there might be
some question in your mind.

1404
01:12:53.370 --> 01:12:56.250
- I never once said I suspected him.
- Nobody says it.

1405
01:12:56.370 --> 01:12:58.660
Chris, I know how much
you love him, but...

1406
01:12:58.790 --> 01:13:01.160
Do you think I could forgive him
if he'd done that?

1407
01:13:01.290 --> 01:13:03.160
I am not here
out of a blue sky, Chris.

1408
01:13:03.290 --> 01:13:05.660
I turned my back on my father.
If there's anything wrong...

1409
01:13:05.790 --> 01:13:07.410
I know that, Ann!

1410
01:13:10.410 --> 01:13:11.410
Oh.

1411
01:13:12.000 --> 01:13:15.790
George is coming from Dad,
and I don't think it's with a blessing.

1412
01:13:15.910 --> 01:13:17.870
He's welcome.
You got nothing to fear from George.

1413
01:13:18.000 --> 01:13:20.750
- All right. Tell me that. Just tell me that.
- The man is innocent!

1414
01:13:21.410 --> 01:13:25.120
Remember, he was falsely accused once
and it put him through hell.

1415
01:13:25.750 --> 01:13:30.040
How would you behave if you were faced
with the same thing again, hm?

1416
01:13:31.790 --> 01:13:34.410
Annie, believe me,
there's nothing wrong for you here.

1417
01:13:34.540 --> 01:13:36.790
Believe me... kid.

1418
01:13:42.250 --> 01:13:44.290
- Aw.
- Hm?

1419
01:13:44.410 --> 01:13:45.410
All right.

1420
01:13:47.290 --> 01:13:48.660
- All right?
- (Chuckles)

1421
01:13:49.790 --> 01:13:51.250
All right, Chris.

1422
01:13:59.790 --> 01:14:01.040
(Audience laughs)

1423
01:14:01.160 --> 01:14:04.290
Every time I come out here
it looks like Playland!

1424
01:14:04.410 --> 01:14:08.500
- I thought you were going to shave.
- In a minute. I just woke up.

1425
01:14:08.620 --> 01:14:10.290
- I can't see nothin'.
- You look shaved.

1426
01:14:10.410 --> 01:14:12.620
Huh? Oh, no, no.

1427
01:14:12.750 --> 01:14:16.370
Gotta be extra special tonight.
Big time tonight, Annie!

1428
01:14:16.500 --> 01:14:18.660
(Laughs) So, tell me.

1429
01:14:19.250 --> 01:14:22.290
How does it feel
to be a married woman?

1430
01:14:22.410 --> 01:14:24.790
Well... I don't know yet.

1431
01:14:25.790 --> 01:14:27.290
What's the matter, you slippin'?

1432
01:14:27.410 --> 01:14:29.660
Oh, the great roué.

1433
01:14:29.790 --> 01:14:31.790
- What is that, roué?
- It's French.

1434
01:14:31.910 --> 01:14:33.870
- Mm-hm.
- Don't talk dirty!

1435
01:14:34.000 --> 01:14:36.290
(Chuckles) You ever meet
a bigger ignoramus?

1436
01:14:36.410 --> 01:14:39.290
- Somebody's got to make a living!
- Oh, that's telling him.

1437
01:14:39.410 --> 01:14:40.660
I don't know.

1438
01:14:40.790 --> 01:14:44.500
Everybody's gettin' so goddamn educated
in this country

1439
01:14:44.620 --> 01:14:47.160
there'll be nobody
to take away the garbage.

1440
01:14:47.790 --> 01:14:50.540
It's gettin' so the only dumb ones left
are the bosses.

1441
01:14:50.660 --> 01:14:53.410
- Ah, you're not so dumb, Joe.
- I know, but...

1442
01:14:53.540 --> 01:14:56.120
You go into our plant,
for instance, and...

1443
01:14:56.250 --> 01:14:59.410
I got so many lieutenants
and majors and colonels

1444
01:14:59.540 --> 01:15:01.910
I'm ashamed to ask somebody
to sweep the floor.

1445
01:15:02.620 --> 01:15:05.540
I gotta be careful
I'll insult somebody. Ooh.

1446
01:15:06.250 --> 01:15:07.660
No, no kiddin'. It's a tragedy.

1447
01:15:07.790 --> 01:15:11.790
You stand on a street today and spit,
you're gonna hit a college man.

1448
01:15:11.910 --> 01:15:14.000
Then... don't spit?

1449
01:15:14.910 --> 01:15:16.290
(Joe grunts)

1450
01:15:16.410 --> 01:15:19.540
I mean to say
it's comin' to a pass, huh?

1451
01:15:21.750 --> 01:15:23.540
Yeah, Annie,
I've been thinkin' about George.

1452
01:15:23.660 --> 01:15:25.910
I've been thinking about
your brother George.

1453
01:15:26.040 --> 01:15:28.540
When he comes, I'd like you
to brooch something to him.

1454
01:15:28.660 --> 01:15:30.540
(Laughs) It's "broach".

1455
01:15:31.790 --> 01:15:35.660
- What's the matter with brooch?
- It's not... English.

1456
01:15:35.790 --> 01:15:37.040
(Quiet laughter)

1457
01:15:37.160 --> 01:15:39.500
When I went to night school
it was brooch!

1458
01:15:39.620 --> 01:15:41.500
Well, in day school it's "broach".

1459
01:15:41.620 --> 01:15:44.160
Hey, don't surround me, will you?

1460
01:15:44.790 --> 01:15:47.410
Agh. But seriously, Annie,

1461
01:15:47.540 --> 01:15:49.410
you say he's not well, George?

1462
01:15:49.540 --> 01:15:50.660
And I've been thinkin',

1463
01:15:50.790 --> 01:15:53.290
why should he knock himself out
in New York

1464
01:15:53.410 --> 01:15:56.290
with that strict competition

1465
01:15:56.410 --> 01:15:59.370
when... when...
when I got so many friends here?

1466
01:15:59.500 --> 01:16:01.910
I'm very friendly
with some big lawyers here in town.

1467
01:16:02.040 --> 01:16:05.290
- I could set George up here.
- That is awfully nice of you, Joe.

1468
01:16:06.120 --> 01:16:08.910
Yeah. Well, look,
it ain't nice of me. Uh...

1469
01:16:09.040 --> 01:16:12.370
I want you to understand me.
I'm... I'm thinking of Chris.

1470
01:16:13.040 --> 01:16:14.370
It's... Well...

1471
01:16:14.500 --> 01:16:16.370
See? This is what I mean. Um...

1472
01:16:17.040 --> 01:16:18.040
You get older

1473
01:16:18.160 --> 01:16:22.120
and you want to feel
that you accomplished something.

1474
01:16:24.290 --> 01:16:26.540
My only accomplishment is my son.

1475
01:16:27.790 --> 01:16:29.160
I ain't brainy.

1476
01:16:29.290 --> 01:16:31.040
That's all I accomplished.

1477
01:16:31.160 --> 01:16:34.290
Now, a year, 18 months from now,

1478
01:16:34.410 --> 01:16:37.000
your father will be a free man.

1479
01:16:37.120 --> 01:16:40.040
Who is he going to come to, Annie?
His baby. You.

1480
01:16:40.160 --> 01:16:43.910
He will. He'll come, old and mad,
into your house.

1481
01:16:44.040 --> 01:16:45.540
That can't matter anymore, Joe.

1482
01:16:45.660 --> 01:16:49.290
Well, I just don't want
that hate to come between us.

1483
01:16:49.410 --> 01:16:52.160
No. I can only tell you
that could never happen.

1484
01:16:53.000 --> 01:16:54.290
Aw.

1485
01:16:54.870 --> 01:16:56.870
You're in love now, Annie.

1486
01:16:57.000 --> 01:16:59.290
But believe me,
I'm older than you and I know.

1487
01:16:59.410 --> 01:17:00.410
(Audience laughs)

1488
01:17:00.540 --> 01:17:03.620
A daughter is a daughter,
and a father is a father.

1489
01:17:03.750 --> 01:17:05.000
And it could happen.

1490
01:17:05.120 --> 01:17:08.540
Now, I'd like you and George
to go to him in prison and tell him:

1491
01:17:08.660 --> 01:17:12.540
"Dad, Joe wants to bring you
into the business when you get out."

1492
01:17:12.660 --> 01:17:14.620
You would have him as a partner?

1493
01:17:14.750 --> 01:17:16.370
No! No partner.

1494
01:17:16.910 --> 01:17:18.660
Just a good job.

1495
01:17:19.910 --> 01:17:23.410
I want him to know, Annie,
while he's sitting there...

1496
01:17:24.290 --> 01:17:26.660
I want him to know
that when he gets out

1497
01:17:26.790 --> 01:17:29.160
he has got a place waitin' for him.

1498
01:17:29.290 --> 01:17:31.750
- It'll take his bitterness away.
- Huh.

1499
01:17:31.870 --> 01:17:34.370
To know you got a place...

1500
01:17:34.500 --> 01:17:36.870
Oh, it just sweetens you.

1501
01:17:37.000 --> 01:17:38.660
Joe, you owe him nothing!

1502
01:17:38.790 --> 01:17:41.370
I owe him a good kick in the teeth,
but he's your father.

1503
01:17:41.500 --> 01:17:45.540
Then kick him in the teeth.
No, I don't want him in the plant.

1504
01:17:45.660 --> 01:17:46.910
So that's that.
You understand me?

1505
01:17:47.040 --> 01:17:49.870
Abs besides, don't talk about him like that.
People will misunderstand you.

1506
01:17:50.000 --> 01:17:52.500
I don't understand
why she has to crucify the man.

1507
01:17:52.620 --> 01:17:56.250
- What's it to you?
- No, no! A father is a father!

1508
01:17:57.160 --> 01:17:59.160
(Slow instrumental music)

1509
01:17:59.910 --> 01:18:02.370
Oh, sheesh, yeah.
I better shave.

1510
01:18:03.250 --> 01:18:06.660
Look, I... I didn't mean
to yell at you, Annie.

1511
01:18:06.790 --> 01:18:08.750
Let's forget the whole thing, Joe.

1512
01:18:09.290 --> 01:18:10.750
- Right.
- Right.

1513
01:18:11.910 --> 01:18:13.910
Oh, she is likable.

1514
01:18:14.040 --> 01:18:16.250
- Shave, will you?
- Oh. Right again. Yep.

1515
01:18:17.040 --> 01:18:19.790
I forgot all about it. Hiya!

1516
01:18:19.910 --> 01:18:22.410
I promised to fix Kate's hair
for tonight.

1517
01:18:22.540 --> 01:18:23.540
Did she comb it yet?

1518
01:18:23.660 --> 01:18:27.250
- Always with a smile, hey, Lydia?
- Sure, why not?

1519
01:18:27.370 --> 01:18:29.790
Yeah. Come on up
and comb my Katie's hair.

1520
01:18:29.910 --> 01:18:31.790
She's got a big night,
make her beautiful.

1521
01:18:31.910 --> 01:18:33.660
- I will.
- (Both chuckle)

1522
01:18:33.790 --> 01:18:36.120
Ooh. You know what?

1523
01:18:36.910 --> 01:18:38.750
That could be a song.

1524
01:18:39.910 --> 01:18:43.660
♪ Oh, come on up
and comb my Katie's hair

1525
01:18:43.790 --> 01:18:46.540
♪ Oh, come on up
'cause she's my lady fair ♪

1526
01:18:46.660 --> 01:18:49.660
Ooh. How's that
for one year of night school?

1527
01:18:49.790 --> 01:18:50.790
Huh?

1528
01:18:51.790 --> 01:18:52.790
Hello?

1529
01:18:52.910 --> 01:18:54.290
(Audience laughs)

1530
01:18:59.250 --> 01:19:00.870
(Ann) Hey. Hey.

1531
01:19:02.750 --> 01:19:06.040
- What's the matter? Where is he?
- Where's your mother?

1532
01:19:06.160 --> 01:19:08.660
- Uh... Upstairs, dressing.
- What happened to George?

1533
01:19:08.790 --> 01:19:12.500
I asked him to wait in the car.
Listen to me now.

1534
01:19:12.620 --> 01:19:14.290
- Can you take some advice?
- Uh-huh.

1535
01:19:14.410 --> 01:19:16.250
Don't bring him in here.

1536
01:19:16.370 --> 01:19:18.750
- Why?
- Kate is in bad shape.

1537
01:19:18.870 --> 01:19:21.290
You can't explode this
in front of her.

1538
01:19:21.410 --> 01:19:24.410
- Explode what?
- You know why he's here.

1539
01:19:24.540 --> 01:19:27.000
Don't try to kid it away.
There's blood in his eye.

1540
01:19:27.750 --> 01:19:30.290
Just drive him somewhere
and talk to him alone.

1541
01:19:30.410 --> 01:19:31.660
Don't be an old lady.

1542
01:19:31.790 --> 01:19:34.120
He's come to take her home.

1543
01:19:34.250 --> 01:19:38.040
Ah. What does that mean?
You know what that means, huh?

1544
01:19:38.160 --> 01:19:40.160
Fight it out with him
someplace else.

1545
01:19:40.290 --> 01:19:42.290
- I'll drive him somewhere.
- No.

1546
01:19:42.410 --> 01:19:45.160
- Will you stop being an idiot?
- Nobody's afraid of him! Cut that out!

1547
01:19:45.290 --> 01:19:47.660
(Sinister ticking)

1548
01:19:57.660 --> 01:19:58.660
(Chris) Uh...

1549
01:19:59.660 --> 01:20:02.500
Helluva way to do.
What're you sitting out there for?

1550
01:20:05.410 --> 01:20:07.500
Doctor said your mother isn't well.

1551
01:20:08.750 --> 01:20:11.410
(Chris) So what?
She'd want to see you, wouldn't she?

1552
01:20:11.540 --> 01:20:13.660
We've been waiting for you
all afternoon.

1553
01:20:15.290 --> 01:20:18.000
(Ann) Oh, this is filthy.
Didn't you bring another shirt?

1554
01:20:18.120 --> 01:20:20.790
- How about the beach, Jim?
- Oh, it's too hot to drive.

1555
01:20:20.910 --> 01:20:23.290
How did you get to the station,
Zeppelin?

1556
01:20:23.410 --> 01:20:25.620
This is, uh, Mrs. Bayliss, George.

1557
01:20:26.500 --> 01:20:27.790
Geo... George!

1558
01:20:30.540 --> 01:20:33.160
(Chris) Uh... Mrs. Bayliss.
(Sue) How do you do?

1559
01:20:42.540 --> 01:20:44.500
You're the people
who bought our house, aren't you?

1560
01:20:44.620 --> 01:20:47.160
That's right. Come and see
what we did with it before you leave.

1561
01:20:47.290 --> 01:20:49.290
I liked it the way it was.

1562
01:20:50.410 --> 01:20:51.870
He's frank, isn't he?

1563
01:20:52.000 --> 01:20:55.660
- I'll see you later. Take it easy, fella.
- (Chris) Yeah, thanks for driving him.

1564
01:20:55.790 --> 01:20:57.410
(Ticking continues)

1565
01:20:59.160 --> 01:21:01.290
How about some grape juice, George?

1566
01:21:01.410 --> 01:21:03.660
Mother made it especially for you.

1567
01:21:04.540 --> 01:21:06.500
Good old Kate,
remembered my grape juice.

1568
01:21:06.620 --> 01:21:09.660
You drank enough of it in this house.
(Chuckles)

1569
01:21:11.660 --> 01:21:14.120
Uh... How've you been, George?

1570
01:21:15.120 --> 01:21:17.120
- Sit down.
- It takes me a minute.

1571
01:21:17.250 --> 01:21:18.250
Just...

1572
01:21:20.540 --> 01:21:21.750
Seems impossible.

1573
01:21:22.540 --> 01:21:25.120
- What?
- That I'm back here.

1574
01:21:26.500 --> 01:21:29.660
Say, you've gotten a little nervous,
haven't you?

1575
01:21:31.040 --> 01:21:32.040
Well, yeah.

1576
01:21:32.910 --> 01:21:34.750
Toward the end of the day.

1577
01:21:35.410 --> 01:21:37.750
What are you,
big executive now, huh?

1578
01:21:38.250 --> 01:21:41.000
Uh... Kind of medium.

1579
01:21:42.000 --> 01:21:43.660
How's the law?

1580
01:21:44.790 --> 01:21:46.000
I don't know.

1581
01:21:46.910 --> 01:21:49.790
When I was studying in the hospital,
it seemed sensible,

1582
01:21:49.910 --> 01:21:52.910
but outside there doesn't seem
to be much of a law.

1583
01:21:54.660 --> 01:21:57.160
- The trees got thick, didn't they?
- (Ann) Uh...

1584
01:21:58.410 --> 01:21:59.540
What's that?

1585
01:21:59.660 --> 01:22:02.910
Oh. Uh, blew down last night.
We had it there for Larry, you know.

1586
01:22:03.040 --> 01:22:04.910
Why? Afraid you'll forget him?

1587
01:22:06.410 --> 01:22:09.790
- Kind of a remark is that?
- Hey! When did you start wearing a hat?

1588
01:22:09.910 --> 01:22:10.910
Today.

1589
01:22:12.120 --> 01:22:15.120
From now on, I decided
to look like a lawyer, anyway.

1590
01:22:16.160 --> 01:22:18.160
- Don't you recognize it?
- Why? Where...?

1591
01:22:18.290 --> 01:22:20.290
Your father's.
He asked me to wear it.

1592
01:22:21.410 --> 01:22:22.410
How is he?

1593
01:22:24.250 --> 01:22:25.540
He got smaller.

1594
01:22:26.540 --> 01:22:28.620
- Smaller?
- Yeah. Little.

1595
01:22:29.750 --> 01:22:31.290
He's a little man.

1596
01:22:31.410 --> 01:22:34.040
That's what happens to suckers.
It's good I went to him in time.

1597
01:22:34.160 --> 01:22:36.500
Another year,
there'd be nothing left but his smell.

1598
01:22:36.620 --> 01:22:38.500
What's the matter?
What's the trouble?

1599
01:22:38.620 --> 01:22:40.410
(George laughs)

1600
01:22:40.540 --> 01:22:42.660
- The trouble?
- Yeah.

1601
01:22:42.790 --> 01:22:44.290
All right, the trouble is

1602
01:22:44.410 --> 01:22:47.750
when you make suckers out of people once,
you shouldn't try and do it twice.

1603
01:22:47.870 --> 01:22:49.370
- What does that mean?
- You're not married yet, are you?

1604
01:22:49.500 --> 01:22:51.290
- George, will you sit down and...
- Are you married yet?

1605
01:22:51.410 --> 01:22:53.910
- No, I'm not married yet.
- You're not going to marry him.

1606
01:22:54.040 --> 01:22:57.040
- Why am I not going to marry him?
- Because his father destroyed your family.

1607
01:22:57.160 --> 01:22:58.410
- Look, George...
- Cut it short, Chris.

1608
01:22:58.540 --> 01:23:00.160
Tell her to come home with me.
Let's not argue.

1609
01:23:00.290 --> 01:23:02.250
- You know what I've got to say.
- You don't want to be the voice of God.

1610
01:23:02.370 --> 01:23:06.040
That's been your trouble all your life,
George, you dive into things.

1611
01:23:06.160 --> 01:23:09.870
What kind of a statement is that to make?
You're a big boy now.

1612
01:23:10.910 --> 01:23:12.910
- I'm a big boy now?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1613
01:23:13.040 --> 01:23:14.540
- Huh?
- Don't come bulling in here.

1614
01:23:14.660 --> 01:23:16.540
If you've got something to say,
be civilized about it.

1615
01:23:16.660 --> 01:23:18.160
Don't civilize me!

1616
01:23:18.290 --> 01:23:20.660
Are you going to talk like a grown man
or aren't you?

1617
01:23:20.790 --> 01:23:24.160
Hey, don't! Don't be angry.
Now, what is the matter?

1618
01:23:24.290 --> 01:23:27.750
Huh? Georgie, what happened?
You kissed me when I left you and now...

1619
01:23:27.870 --> 01:23:30.040
My life turned upside down
since then!

1620
01:23:31.000 --> 01:23:32.910
I couldn't go back to work
when you left!

1621
01:23:33.040 --> 01:23:35.910
I wanted to go to Dad, to tell him
you were going to be married.

1622
01:23:36.040 --> 01:23:38.540
It seemed impossible not to tell him.
He loved you so much.

1623
01:23:38.660 --> 01:23:41.660
Annie, we did a terrible thing.
We can never be forgiven.

1624
01:23:41.790 --> 01:23:44.540
Not even to send him a card
at Christmas.

1625
01:23:44.660 --> 01:23:47.410
I didn't see him once
since I got home from the war.

1626
01:23:47.540 --> 01:23:50.040
Annie, you don't know what was done
to that man. You don't know what happened.

1627
01:23:50.160 --> 01:23:53.290
- Of course I know!
- You can't know! You wouldn't be here!

1628
01:23:53.410 --> 01:23:55.040
Dad came to work that day.

1629
01:23:55.160 --> 01:23:57.540
The night foreman showed him
the cylinder heads.

1630
01:23:57.660 --> 01:24:01.120
They were coming out of the process with defects.
There was something wrong with the process.

1631
01:24:01.250 --> 01:24:05.500
Dad went directly to the phone, called here,
and told Joe to come down right away.

1632
01:24:05.620 --> 01:24:08.540
But the morning passed.
No sign of Joe. So Dad called again.

1633
01:24:08.660 --> 01:24:11.370
By this time,
he had over a hundred defectives.

1634
01:24:11.500 --> 01:24:14.660
The Army was screaming for stuff
and Dad didn't have anything to ship,

1635
01:24:14.790 --> 01:24:18.410
so Joe told him on the phone,
he told him to, uh, weld,

1636
01:24:18.540 --> 01:24:20.750
cover up the cracks
in any way he could and ship them out.

1637
01:24:20.870 --> 01:24:23.660
- Are you through now?
- No! No, I'm not through now!

1638
01:24:24.290 --> 01:24:25.410
Dad was afraid.

1639
01:24:25.540 --> 01:24:27.660
He wanted Joe there
if he was going to do it,

1640
01:24:27.790 --> 01:24:29.540
but Joe can't come down,
he's sick.

1641
01:24:29.660 --> 01:24:32.790
Right? Sick.
He suddenly gets the flu. Suddenly.

1642
01:24:32.910 --> 01:24:35.540
But he promised to take responsibility.
Do you understand what I'm saying?

1643
01:24:35.660 --> 01:24:38.160
On the telephone,
you can't have responsibility.

1644
01:24:38.290 --> 01:24:41.500
In a court, you can always deny a phone call
and that's exactly what he did.

1645
01:24:41.620 --> 01:24:43.370
They knew he was a liar
the first time,

1646
01:24:43.500 --> 01:24:45.540
but in the appeal,
they believed that rotten lie

1647
01:24:45.660 --> 01:24:48.250
and now Joe is a big shot
and your father is the patsy.

1648
01:24:48.370 --> 01:24:49.910
Now, what are you going to do?

1649
01:24:50.040 --> 01:24:53.370
Eat his food? Sleep in his bed? Huh?
Answer me. What are you going to do?

1650
01:24:53.500 --> 01:24:56.500
- What're you going to do, George?
- He's too smart for me, I can't prove a phone call.

1651
01:24:56.620 --> 01:24:58.790
- How dare you come in here with that rot?
- George, the court...

1652
01:24:58.910 --> 01:25:00.370
The court didn't know your father!

1653
01:25:00.500 --> 01:25:02.370
But you know him.
You know in your heart Joe did it!

1654
01:25:02.500 --> 01:25:05.040
- Lower your voice or I'll throw you out!
- She knows! She knows!

1655
01:25:05.160 --> 01:25:07.660
- Get him out of here!
- Georgie, I know everything you've said. OK?

1656
01:25:07.790 --> 01:25:11.870
- Dad, he told that whole thing in court.
- The court did not know him, Annie!

1657
01:25:12.000 --> 01:25:15.870
But Georgie, he will say anything, OK?
You know how quick he can lie!

1658
01:25:16.000 --> 01:25:18.410
I'll ask you something,
and look me in the eye when you answer me.

1659
01:25:18.540 --> 01:25:19.910
- I'll look you in the eye.
- You know your father.

1660
01:25:20.040 --> 01:25:21.910
- I know him well.
- And he's the kind of boss

1661
01:25:22.040 --> 01:25:23.750
to let 121 cylinder heads

1662
01:25:23.870 --> 01:25:26.250
be repaired and shipped out of his shop
without even knowing about it?

1663
01:25:26.370 --> 01:25:29.660
- He's that kind of boss.
- The same Joe Keller who never left his shop

1664
01:25:29.790 --> 01:25:32.160
without first going around
to see all the lights were out.

1665
01:25:32.290 --> 01:25:33.370
The same Joe Keller.

1666
01:25:33.500 --> 01:25:36.120
The man who knows how many minutes
his workers spend in the toilet.

1667
01:25:36.250 --> 01:25:39.290
- The same man.
- And my father, that frightened mouse

1668
01:25:39.410 --> 01:25:41.540
who'd never buy a shirt
without somebody along

1669
01:25:41.660 --> 01:25:43.410
would dare do such a thing
on his own?

1670
01:25:43.540 --> 01:25:45.540
On his own, and because
he's a frightened mouse,

1671
01:25:45.660 --> 01:25:48.540
this is another thing he'd do,
throw the blame on somebody else!

1672
01:25:48.660 --> 01:25:50.500
He's not man enough
to take it himself!

1673
01:25:50.620 --> 01:25:52.160
He tried it. In court!

1674
01:25:52.290 --> 01:25:54.410
It didn't work,
but with a fool like you it works!

1675
01:25:54.540 --> 01:25:56.910
- Oh, Chris, you're a liar to yourself!
- Don't talk like that!

1676
01:25:57.040 --> 01:25:58.040
Tell me, George, what happened?

1677
01:25:58.160 --> 01:26:01.290
The court record was good enough
for you all these years.

1678
01:26:01.410 --> 01:26:04.750
Why isn't it good now? Huh?
Why did you believe it all these years?

1679
01:26:04.870 --> 01:26:06.290
Because you believed it.

1680
01:26:06.410 --> 01:26:08.040
(Ticking stops)

1681
01:26:08.160 --> 01:26:10.040
That's the truth, Chris.

1682
01:26:10.660 --> 01:26:13.160
I believed everything
because I thought you did.

1683
01:26:13.290 --> 01:26:15.870
But today I heard it
from his mouth.

1684
01:26:17.160 --> 01:26:20.410
From his mouth
it's altogether different than the record.

1685
01:26:20.540 --> 01:26:22.750
Anyone who knows him
and knows your father

1686
01:26:22.870 --> 01:26:24.290
will believe it from his mouth.

1687
01:26:25.000 --> 01:26:28.160
Your Dad took everything we have!

1688
01:26:29.620 --> 01:26:31.000
I can't beat that.

1689
01:26:32.410 --> 01:26:35.160
But she's one item
he's not going to grab.

1690
01:26:35.290 --> 01:26:38.000
Get your things. Everything they have
is covered with blood.

1691
01:26:38.120 --> 01:26:40.910
You're not the kind of a girl
who can live with that. Get your things.

1692
01:26:41.040 --> 01:26:42.870
Annie, you're not going
to believe that, are you?

1693
01:26:43.660 --> 01:26:47.000
- You know it's not true, don't you?
- How can he tell you? It's his father!

1694
01:26:47.120 --> 01:26:48.870
None of these things
ever cross your mind?

1695
01:26:49.000 --> 01:26:51.290
Yeah, it crossed my mind
because anything can cross your mind!

1696
01:26:51.410 --> 01:26:53.790
- He knows, Annie! He knows!
- Oh, the voice of God!

1697
01:26:53.910 --> 01:26:56.120
Then why isn't your name
on the business? Explain that to me.

1698
01:26:56.250 --> 01:26:58.620
What the hell is that got to do with it?
Even when I don't own it?

1699
01:26:58.750 --> 01:27:00.750
Who are you kidding?
Who gets it when he dies?

1700
01:27:00.870 --> 01:27:02.290
- Huh?
- So?

1701
01:27:03.370 --> 01:27:05.410
Open your eyes.
You know the both of them.

1702
01:27:05.540 --> 01:27:08.040
Isn't that the first thing they'd do,
the way they love each other?

1703
01:27:08.160 --> 01:27:09.870
J. O. Keller &amp; Son.

1704
01:27:10.000 --> 01:27:11.040
Huh?

1705
01:27:11.160 --> 01:27:14.910
No. I'll settle it. Do you want
to settle it or are you afraid to?

1706
01:27:15.040 --> 01:27:17.540
- What do you mean?
- Let me go up and talk to your father.

1707
01:27:17.660 --> 01:27:20.370
In ten minutes you'll have the answer.
Are you afraid of the answer?

1708
01:27:20.500 --> 01:27:22.660
I'm not afraid of the answer.
I know the answer.

1709
01:27:22.790 --> 01:27:23.910
But my mother isn't well.

1710
01:27:24.040 --> 01:27:25.620
- I don't want to fight here.
- Let me go to him.

1711
01:27:25.750 --> 01:27:28.040
Hey, no! You're not going
to start a fight here now!

1712
01:27:28.160 --> 01:27:29.790
- What more do you want?
- Someone's coming.

1713
01:27:29.910 --> 01:27:32.040
- You won't say anything now.
- You'll go soon. I'll call a cab.

1714
01:27:32.160 --> 01:27:34.290
- You're coming with me.
- Don't mention marriage. We haven't told her yet.

1715
01:27:34.410 --> 01:27:36.410
- You're coming with me!
- You understand? Georgie, please don't.

1716
01:27:36.540 --> 01:27:38.250
You are not going
to start anything here now!

1717
01:27:38.370 --> 01:27:39.540
- Annie.
- (Kate) Georgie!

1718
01:27:39.660 --> 01:27:40.660
(Soft music)

1719
01:27:40.790 --> 01:27:43.120
Georgie. Aw.

1720
01:27:46.290 --> 01:27:48.290
- Georgie.
- Hey.

1721
01:27:48.410 --> 01:27:50.910
- Aw.
- Hey.

1722
01:27:52.120 --> 01:27:54.660
- Hello, Kate.
- Georgie. Aw.

1723
01:27:57.500 --> 01:27:59.790
They made an old man out of you.

1724
01:27:59.910 --> 01:28:01.540
- Huh?
- Look, you're gray.

1725
01:28:01.660 --> 01:28:02.870
Oh, I know.

1726
01:28:03.000 --> 01:28:06.500
Oh. I told you when you went away,
don't try for medals.

1727
01:28:06.620 --> 01:28:09.540
I didn't try, Kate.
They made it very easy for me.

1728
01:28:09.660 --> 01:28:11.040
Oh, go on. You're all alike.

1729
01:28:11.620 --> 01:28:14.290
Look at him.
I thought you said he was fine.

1730
01:28:14.410 --> 01:28:16.540
- He looks like a ghost.
- I feel all right.

1731
01:28:16.660 --> 01:28:18.750
I'm sick to look at ya.

1732
01:28:19.540 --> 01:28:22.250
What's the matter with your mother?
Why don't she feed you?

1733
01:28:22.370 --> 01:28:24.660
Oh, he just hasn't any appetite.

1734
01:28:24.790 --> 01:28:26.790
If he ate in my house,
he'd have an appetite.

1735
01:28:26.910 --> 01:28:28.250
I pity your husband.

1736
01:28:28.370 --> 01:28:30.540
Sit down.
I'll make you a sandwich.

1737
01:28:30.660 --> 01:28:32.410
Oh, I'm really not hungry.

1738
01:28:33.410 --> 01:28:38.040
Honest to God, it breaks my heart
to see what happened to all the children.

1739
01:28:38.540 --> 01:28:40.620
How we worked and planned for ya.

1740
01:28:40.750 --> 01:28:42.790
- Hm.
- And you end up no better than us.

1741
01:28:42.910 --> 01:28:45.540
Oh, you... You haven't changed at all,
you know that, Kate?

1742
01:28:45.660 --> 01:28:48.870
None of us changed, Georgie.
We all love you.

1743
01:28:49.000 --> 01:28:51.290
Joe was just talking
about the day you were born

1744
01:28:51.410 --> 01:28:52.870
and the water got shut off.

1745
01:28:53.000 --> 01:28:55.250
People were carrying basins
from a block away.

1746
01:28:55.370 --> 01:28:58.750
A stranger would have thought
the whole neighborhood was on fire.

1747
01:28:58.870 --> 01:29:00.540
(Ann laughs)

1748
01:29:02.540 --> 01:29:04.160
Why didn't you give him some juice?

1749
01:29:04.910 --> 01:29:07.750
- Oh, I offered it to him.
- You offered it to him.

1750
01:29:08.410 --> 01:29:09.410
Give it to him.

1751
01:29:09.540 --> 01:29:11.000
(Quiet laughter in audience)

1752
01:29:11.120 --> 01:29:13.410
- And now you're going to sit here...
- What? Oh.

1753
01:29:13.540 --> 01:29:16.790
...drink some juice
and look like something.

1754
01:29:16.910 --> 01:29:18.790
Oh, Kate, I feel hungry already.

1755
01:29:18.910 --> 01:29:23.250
Yeah, she could turn, uh, Mahatma Gandhi
into a heavyweight.

1756
01:29:23.370 --> 01:29:25.870
Listen, to hell with the restaurant.

1757
01:29:26.000 --> 01:29:29.040
I got a ham in the icebox,
and frozen strawberries, and avocados.

1758
01:29:29.160 --> 01:29:30.910
Train leaves at 8:30, Ann.

1759
01:29:31.040 --> 01:29:33.290
- You're leaving?
- No, Mom, she's not leaving.

1760
01:29:33.410 --> 01:29:37.790
You hardly got here. Just give yourself
a chance to get acquainted again.

1761
01:29:37.910 --> 01:29:39.870
Yeah, sure,
you don't even know us anymore.

1762
01:29:40.410 --> 01:29:41.910
Well, if they can't stay, don't...

1763
01:29:42.040 --> 01:29:44.790
- It's just a question of George...
- Now wait a minute, Chris.

1764
01:29:44.910 --> 01:29:48.410
Hey, hey! If you want to go,
I'll drive you to the station now.

1765
01:29:48.540 --> 01:29:52.040
But if you're staying,
no arguments while you're here.

1766
01:29:52.160 --> 01:29:54.290
Well, why should he argue?

1767
01:29:54.410 --> 01:29:56.870
Georgie and us
have got no arguments.

1768
01:29:57.000 --> 01:30:00.660
How can we have an argument, Georgie?
We all got hit by the same lightning.

1769
01:30:00.790 --> 01:30:02.120
How can you...?

1770
01:30:02.250 --> 01:30:03.790
(Soft music continues)

1771
01:30:07.160 --> 01:30:09.540
Did you see what happened
to Larry's tree?

1772
01:30:09.660 --> 01:30:11.500
- Yeah.
- Imagine.

1773
01:30:11.620 --> 01:30:14.000
While I'm dreaming of him
in the middle of the night,

1774
01:30:14.120 --> 01:30:15.790
- the wind comes along and...
- Hey.

1775
01:30:17.660 --> 01:30:18.660
Georgie.

1776
01:30:20.620 --> 01:30:24.000
Georgie, Georgie,
Georgie, Georgie, Georgie.

1777
01:30:26.370 --> 01:30:27.620
Hello, Laughy.

1778
01:30:30.040 --> 01:30:31.040
What'd you do, grow?

1779
01:30:32.910 --> 01:30:36.290
- I'm a big girl now.
- Look what she can do to a hat.

1780
01:30:36.410 --> 01:30:38.120
- Did you make that?
- In ten minutes.

1781
01:30:38.250 --> 01:30:40.790
- I only rearranged it.
- (George) Still make your own clothes?

1782
01:30:40.910 --> 01:30:44.160
Ain't she classy? All she needs now
is a Russian wolfhound.

1783
01:30:44.290 --> 01:30:47.370
- Feels like somebody's sitting on my head.
- Oh, no, it's beautiful, Kate.

1784
01:30:47.500 --> 01:30:49.870
She's a genius. You should've married her.
This one can feed you.

1785
01:30:50.000 --> 01:30:52.870
- Stop that, Kate.
- Didn't I hear you had a baby?

1786
01:30:53.000 --> 01:30:55.410
Oh, you didn't hear so good.
She got three babies.

1787
01:30:56.410 --> 01:30:57.410
Ah.

1788
01:30:58.040 --> 01:31:00.410
No kidding. Three.

1789
01:31:01.540 --> 01:31:02.790
- Yeah.
- (George chuckles)

1790
01:31:02.910 --> 01:31:04.660
Yeah, it was one.

1791
01:31:05.790 --> 01:31:07.000
Two, three.

1792
01:31:08.620 --> 01:31:11.790
- You've been away a long time, Georgie.
- I'm beginning to realize.

1793
01:31:11.910 --> 01:31:14.160
The trouble with you kids
is you think too much.

1794
01:31:14.290 --> 01:31:15.540
Well, we think too.

1795
01:31:15.660 --> 01:31:18.290
- Yeah, but not all the time.
- They never took Frank, huh?

1796
01:31:18.410 --> 01:31:19.790
(Soft music continues)

1797
01:31:19.910 --> 01:31:23.500
No, he was, uh, always
one year ahead of the draft.

1798
01:31:23.620 --> 01:31:27.540
It's amazing. When they were calling
boys 27, Frank was just 28.

1799
01:31:27.660 --> 01:31:29.910
When they made it 28,
he was just 29.

1800
01:31:30.040 --> 01:31:31.870
That's why he took up astrology.

1801
01:31:32.000 --> 01:31:34.160
It's all in when you were born,
it goes to show.

1802
01:31:34.290 --> 01:31:35.870
What does it go to show?

1803
01:31:36.000 --> 01:31:37.660
Don't be so intelligent!

1804
01:31:37.790 --> 01:31:39.040
(Audience laughs)

1805
01:31:39.160 --> 01:31:41.660
Some superstitions are very nice!

1806
01:31:45.250 --> 01:31:46.660
Did he finish Larry's horoscope?

1807
01:31:46.790 --> 01:31:49.540
I'll go ask him now. I'm going in.

1808
01:31:50.160 --> 01:31:51.160
Um...

1809
01:31:53.040 --> 01:31:56.370
Would you...
like to come see my babies?

1810
01:31:56.500 --> 01:31:57.500
Come on.

1811
01:32:00.910 --> 01:32:02.620
I don't think so, Lydia.

1812
01:32:04.370 --> 01:32:05.370
All right.

1813
01:32:08.750 --> 01:32:09.750
(George) Oh.

1814
01:32:21.000 --> 01:32:22.540
(Clears her throat)

1815
01:32:22.660 --> 01:32:24.540
Uh, good luck to you, George.

1816
01:32:24.660 --> 01:32:27.040
Yeah. Thank you.
And to you.

1817
01:32:27.160 --> 01:32:28.500
(Soft music continues)

1818
01:32:29.410 --> 01:32:31.120
- And Frank.
- Oh.

1819
01:32:32.000 --> 01:32:33.660
- Frank.
- Yeah.

1820
01:32:36.160 --> 01:32:38.160
- She got pretty, eh?
- Yeah, very pretty.

1821
01:32:38.290 --> 01:32:41.250
Oh, she's beautiful,
you damned fool!

1822
01:32:41.370 --> 01:32:43.160
She makes it seem so nice
around here.

1823
01:32:43.290 --> 01:32:45.370
Look what happened
because you wouldn't listen to me.

1824
01:32:45.500 --> 01:32:47.660
- Huh?
- I told you to marry that girl

1825
01:32:47.790 --> 01:32:49.120
and stay out of the war.

1826
01:32:49.250 --> 01:32:52.790
- She used to laugh too much.
- And you didn't laugh enough.

1827
01:32:53.290 --> 01:32:55.410
While you were getting all mad
about fascism,

1828
01:32:55.540 --> 01:32:57.290
Frank was getting into her bed.

1829
01:32:57.410 --> 01:32:59.790
- He won the war, Frank.
- All the battles.

1830
01:32:59.910 --> 01:33:02.160
I told you, Georgie,
when they started the draft,

1831
01:33:02.290 --> 01:33:03.660
that you loved that girl.

1832
01:33:03.790 --> 01:33:07.410
- (Chris) And truer love hath no man.
- I'm smarter than any of yous.

1833
01:33:07.540 --> 01:33:11.410
- (George) She's wonderful.
- And now you're going to listen to me.

1834
01:33:11.540 --> 01:33:13.620
You had big principles.

1835
01:33:13.750 --> 01:33:15.870
- Eagle Scouts the three of you.
- (Chris) Yes.

1836
01:33:16.000 --> 01:33:19.160
So now I got a tree, and this one.

1837
01:33:19.290 --> 01:33:22.160
When the weather's bad,
he can't stand on his feet.

1838
01:33:22.290 --> 01:33:25.790
And that big dope next door
who never reads anything but Andy Gump

1839
01:33:25.910 --> 01:33:28.660
has got three children
and his house paid off.

1840
01:33:28.790 --> 01:33:32.160
Stop being a philosopher
and look after yourself.

1841
01:33:32.290 --> 01:33:34.040
Like Joe was saying,

1842
01:33:34.160 --> 01:33:37.290
you move back here,
he'll help you get set,

1843
01:33:37.410 --> 01:33:40.540
and I'll find you a girl
and put a smile on your face.

1844
01:33:40.660 --> 01:33:43.410
Wait, Joe?
Joe wants me here?

1845
01:33:43.540 --> 01:33:47.290
Yeah. Yeah. He asked me to tell you,
and I think it's a good idea.

1846
01:33:47.410 --> 01:33:48.790
Certainly.

1847
01:33:49.290 --> 01:33:52.040
Why must you make believe
that you hate us?

1848
01:33:52.540 --> 01:33:54.660
Is that another principle?

1849
01:33:54.790 --> 01:33:56.620
That you have to hate us?

1850
01:33:58.040 --> 01:33:59.910
You don't hate us, Georgie.

1851
01:34:00.040 --> 01:34:02.500
I know you, you can't fool me.

1852
01:34:02.620 --> 01:34:04.290
- I diapered you.
- Hey.

1853
01:34:07.410 --> 01:34:09.790
Ooh! You remember
Mr. Marcy's daughter?

1854
01:34:09.910 --> 01:34:12.000
Oh! She's got you hooked already.

1855
01:34:12.120 --> 01:34:15.410
You look her over, George,
you'll see she's the most beautiful...

1856
01:34:15.540 --> 01:34:17.620
She's got warts, George.

1857
01:34:18.250 --> 01:34:20.500
She hasn't got warts!

1858
01:34:21.040 --> 01:34:23.370
So the girl's got a little beauty mark
on her chin...

1859
01:34:23.500 --> 01:34:25.790
Yeah, she got two on her nose.

1860
01:34:26.660 --> 01:34:29.160
You remember her father's
the retired police inspector.

1861
01:34:29.290 --> 01:34:31.160
He's a sergeant, George.

1862
01:34:31.290 --> 01:34:33.910
- Oh, he's a very kind man.
- He looks like a gorilla.

1863
01:34:34.040 --> 01:34:35.660
Well, he never shot anybody!

1864
01:34:35.790 --> 01:34:38.410
- Hey. Hey.
- Well! Look who's here!

1865
01:34:38.540 --> 01:34:39.910
(Laughs)

1866
01:34:40.040 --> 01:34:42.290
Georgie! Oh!

1867
01:34:42.410 --> 01:34:43.910
Good to see ya!

1868
01:34:49.370 --> 01:34:51.120
- How are you, Joe?
- Eh.

1869
01:34:51.250 --> 01:34:52.790
Yep. So-so.

1870
01:34:52.910 --> 01:34:54.040
Gettin' old.

1871
01:34:54.660 --> 01:34:57.790
- You comin' out to dinner with us?
- No, got to be back in New York.

1872
01:34:57.910 --> 01:34:59.790
I'll, uh... I'll call you a cab.

1873
01:34:59.910 --> 01:35:02.620
Well, it's too bad
you can't stay, George.

1874
01:35:02.750 --> 01:35:04.620
Hey. He looks fine.

1875
01:35:04.750 --> 01:35:05.910
He looks terrible.

1876
01:35:06.040 --> 01:35:08.370
That's what I said.
You look terrible, George.

1877
01:35:09.040 --> 01:35:11.500
I wear the pants,
she beats me with the belt.

1878
01:35:11.620 --> 01:35:15.000
I, uh... I saw your factory
on the way from the station.

1879
01:35:15.120 --> 01:35:17.290
- It looks like General Motors.
- Oof.

1880
01:35:17.410 --> 01:35:19.790
I wish it was General Motors,
but it ain't.

1881
01:35:21.040 --> 01:35:22.250
So, uh...

1882
01:35:23.040 --> 01:35:25.160
You finally went
to see your father, I hear.

1883
01:35:25.290 --> 01:35:29.410
Yeah. Yeah. This morning.
What kind of stuff do you make now?

1884
01:35:30.290 --> 01:35:32.910
Oh, uh...
little of everything, you know.

1885
01:35:33.040 --> 01:35:37.370
Pressure cookers,
an assembly line for washing machines.

1886
01:35:37.500 --> 01:35:40.000
We got a nice flexible plant now,
you know.

1887
01:35:40.620 --> 01:35:43.660
So, tell me, how'd you find Dad?
Is he feeling all right?

1888
01:35:43.790 --> 01:35:44.790
No.

1889
01:35:46.290 --> 01:35:47.410
No, he's not well, Joe.

1890
01:35:47.540 --> 01:35:50.120
Oh. It's not his heart again, is it?

1891
01:35:50.250 --> 01:35:51.620
(Somber instrumental music)

1892
01:35:51.750 --> 01:35:53.370
Uh... Well, it's everything, Joe.

1893
01:35:53.500 --> 01:35:55.410
- It's his soul.
- His soul?

1894
01:35:55.540 --> 01:35:57.660
How about seeing
what they did with your house?

1895
01:35:57.790 --> 01:35:59.290
- Leave him be.
- I'd like to talk to him.

1896
01:35:59.410 --> 01:36:03.750
Sure, he just got here.
Boy, that's what they do, huh, George?

1897
01:36:03.870 --> 01:36:06.790
A little man makes a mistake,
they hang him by the thumbs.

1898
01:36:06.910 --> 01:36:09.660
The big ones
become ambassadors, right?

1899
01:36:09.790 --> 01:36:14.620
Ah. You know, I wish I knew
you were going to see Dad today.

1900
01:36:14.750 --> 01:36:18.910
- I didn't know you were interested.
- Yeah. Yeah, in a way I am.

1901
01:36:19.040 --> 01:36:21.660
You see, George,
I'd like him to know,

1902
01:36:21.790 --> 01:36:23.910
as far as I'm concerned,
any time he wants,

1903
01:36:24.040 --> 01:36:27.540
he has got a place with me.
I'd like him to know that.

1904
01:36:29.040 --> 01:36:32.290
He hates your guts, Joe.
Don't you know that?

1905
01:36:34.790 --> 01:36:38.410
Well, I imagined it, but...
Hey, that could change too.

1906
01:36:38.540 --> 01:36:40.870
Steve was never like that.

1907
01:36:41.000 --> 01:36:42.410
- Oh, he's like that now.
- Oh.

1908
01:36:42.540 --> 01:36:44.910
He'd like to take every man
who made money in the war

1909
01:36:45.040 --> 01:36:46.410
and put 'em up against a wall.

1910
01:36:46.540 --> 01:36:49.660
(Chris) He'll need a lot of bullets.
(George) He'd better not get any.

1911
01:36:49.790 --> 01:36:51.410
That's a sad thing to hear.

1912
01:36:51.540 --> 01:36:54.500
Why? What'd you expect him
to think of you?

1913
01:36:58.620 --> 01:37:01.620
I'm sad to see
that he hasn't changed.

1914
01:37:01.750 --> 01:37:05.160
All the time I've known him,
25 years,

1915
01:37:05.290 --> 01:37:08.000
the man has never learned
how to take the blame.

1916
01:37:08.120 --> 01:37:09.620
- You know that, George.
- Well, I...

1917
01:37:09.750 --> 01:37:11.500
But you do know that.

1918
01:37:12.370 --> 01:37:16.410
'Cause the way you come in here
you don't look like you remember it.

1919
01:37:17.000 --> 01:37:18.120
Hm?

1920
01:37:18.250 --> 01:37:21.540
I mean, like the time we had
that shop on Flood Street

1921
01:37:21.660 --> 01:37:25.370
and he damn near blew us all up
with that heater

1922
01:37:25.500 --> 01:37:28.870
he left burning for three days
without water.

1923
01:37:29.000 --> 01:37:31.370
He wouldn't accept
that was his fault either.

1924
01:37:31.500 --> 01:37:34.620
I had to fire a mechanic to save his face.
You remember that.

1925
01:37:34.750 --> 01:37:36.910
- Yeah, but...
- I'm just mentioning it, George,

1926
01:37:37.040 --> 01:37:41.040
because it's just another one
of a lot of things.

1927
01:37:41.160 --> 01:37:45.910
Oh, like... like the time he gave Frank
that money to invest in oil stock.

1928
01:37:46.040 --> 01:37:47.040
I knew that.

1929
01:37:47.160 --> 01:37:50.160
It's important to remember
those things, kid.

1930
01:37:50.910 --> 01:37:55.660
I mean, the way he cursed Frank
because the stock went down.

1931
01:37:55.790 --> 01:37:58.290
Was...? (Chuckles)
Was that Frank's fault?

1932
01:37:58.870 --> 01:38:01.790
To listen to him
Frank was a swindler.

1933
01:38:01.910 --> 01:38:04.160
And all the man did
was give him a bad tip.

1934
01:38:04.290 --> 01:38:06.790
- I know those things.
- Well, then, remember them.

1935
01:38:08.500 --> 01:38:09.870
Remember them, son.

1936
01:38:10.000 --> 01:38:11.620
(Somber music continues)

1937
01:38:11.750 --> 01:38:15.250
Why, there... there are
certain men in the world

1938
01:38:15.370 --> 01:38:19.910
who'd rather see everybody hung
before they'll take blame.

1939
01:38:22.160 --> 01:38:23.790
You understand me,
don't you, George?

1940
01:38:23.910 --> 01:38:26.250
The cab's on its way.
Would you like to wash?

1941
01:38:26.370 --> 01:38:28.660
Why must he go?
Make the midnight, George.

1942
01:38:28.790 --> 01:38:30.410
Sure. You'll have dinner with us.

1943
01:38:30.540 --> 01:38:32.120
(Ann) Hey! How about it?
Why not?

1944
01:38:32.250 --> 01:38:34.410
We're eating at the lake,
we could have a swell time.

1945
01:38:34.540 --> 01:38:36.160
(Percussion beat)

1946
01:38:49.370 --> 01:38:50.790
- All right.
- (Percussion beat)

1947
01:38:50.910 --> 01:38:51.750
Now you're talking!

1948
01:38:51.870 --> 01:38:53.790
I've got a shirt that'll go right with that suit.

1949
01:38:53.910 --> 01:38:55.750
Size 15 and a half, right, George?

1950
01:38:55.870 --> 01:38:57.790
Is Lydia...? I mean,
are Frank and Lydia coming?

1951
01:38:57.910 --> 01:38:59.870
Ooh! I'll find you a date
that'll make her look like...

1952
01:39:00.000 --> 01:39:02.290
- No, I don't want a date.
- I know somebody just for you.

1953
01:39:02.410 --> 01:39:04.540
Uh... Charlotte Tanner.

1954
01:39:04.660 --> 01:39:08.160
- Sure! Call her up!
- Go up and pick out a shirt and tie.

1955
01:39:08.290 --> 01:39:11.290
I never felt at home
anywhere but here.

1956
01:39:11.410 --> 01:39:13.120
I feel so...

1957
01:39:13.790 --> 01:39:16.410
- Kate, you look so young.
- Ooh! (Laughs)

1958
01:39:16.540 --> 01:39:19.410
You didn't change at all.
It rings an old bell.

1959
01:39:19.540 --> 01:39:21.660
You too, Joe,
you're amazingly the same.

1960
01:39:21.790 --> 01:39:24.750
- The whole atmosphere is.
- Heck, I ain't got time to get sick.

1961
01:39:24.870 --> 01:39:27.660
He hasn't been laid up in 15 years.

1962
01:39:27.790 --> 01:39:28.790
(Percussion beat)

1963
01:39:29.750 --> 01:39:32.250
Except for my flu during the war.

1964
01:39:34.500 --> 01:39:35.500
(Kate) Huh?

1965
01:39:35.620 --> 01:39:39.000
My flu, when I was sick
during the war.

1966
01:39:39.120 --> 01:39:41.660
Well, sure.
I meant except for that flu.

1967
01:39:41.790 --> 01:39:44.500
- Well, it slipped my mind.
- (Chuckling) I know.

1968
01:39:45.040 --> 01:39:47.290
- What...?
- No. Don't look at me that way.

1969
01:39:47.410 --> 01:39:49.660
- Huh?
- He wanted to go into the shop.

1970
01:39:49.790 --> 01:39:52.620
But he couldn't lift himself from the bed.
I thought he had pneumonia.

1971
01:39:52.750 --> 01:39:56.410
- Why did you say he's never...?
- Listen, kid, I know how you feel.

1972
01:39:56.540 --> 01:39:59.870
I'll never forgive myself.
If I could've gone in that day,

1973
01:40:00.000 --> 01:40:02.540
I never would've allowed Dad
to touch those heads.

1974
01:40:02.660 --> 01:40:04.750
She said you've never been sick.
Annie, didn't you hear?

1975
01:40:04.870 --> 01:40:07.660
- Do you remember every time you were sick?
- I'd remember pneumonia.

1976
01:40:07.790 --> 01:40:11.410
Especially if I got it just the day my partner
was going to patch up cylinder heads.

1977
01:40:11.540 --> 01:40:13.790
- What happened that day, Joe?
- Kate! Kate!

1978
01:40:13.910 --> 01:40:16.160
- Frank, did you see George?
- Oh!

1979
01:40:17.040 --> 01:40:18.290
Lydia told me.

1980
01:40:19.040 --> 01:40:23.040
Uh, I'm very glad...
Sorry, you'll have to pardon me.

1981
01:40:23.160 --> 01:40:25.540
I have got something amazing
for you, Kate.

1982
01:40:25.660 --> 01:40:27.250
I finished Larry's horoscope.

1983
01:40:27.370 --> 01:40:29.540
Oh, this'll interest you, George.
It's wonderful.

1984
01:40:29.660 --> 01:40:31.250
George, the girl's on the phone!

1985
01:40:31.370 --> 01:40:35.000
- He finished Larry's horoscope!
- Frank, can't you pick a better time than this?

1986
01:40:35.120 --> 01:40:37.910
The greatest men who ever lived
believed in the stars!

1987
01:40:38.040 --> 01:40:39.870
(Chris) Stop filling her head with that junk.

1988
01:40:40.000 --> 01:40:43.910
Is it junk to believe
there's a greater power than ourselves?

1989
01:40:44.040 --> 01:40:47.370
I have studied the stars of his life.
I won't argue with you.

1990
01:40:47.500 --> 01:40:51.040
I am telling you,
somewhere in this world

1991
01:40:51.160 --> 01:40:52.790
your brother is alive.

1992
01:40:53.870 --> 01:40:57.040
- Why isn't it possible?
- Because it's insane.

1993
01:40:57.160 --> 01:40:59.290
Just a minute now.
I will tell you something.

1994
01:40:59.410 --> 01:41:02.290
You can do as you please.
Just let me say it.

1995
01:41:02.410 --> 01:41:05.620
He was supposed to have died
on November 25th,

1996
01:41:05.750 --> 01:41:08.870
but November 25th
was his favorable day.

1997
01:41:09.000 --> 01:41:10.290
- Mother.
- Listen to him!

1998
01:41:10.410 --> 01:41:13.410
It was a day where everything good
was shining on him,

1999
01:41:13.540 --> 01:41:16.040
the kind of day
he should've married on.

2000
01:41:16.160 --> 01:41:20.040
No, you can laugh at a lot of it,
I can understand you laughing.

2001
01:41:20.160 --> 01:41:22.870
But the odds are a million to one

2002
01:41:23.000 --> 01:41:26.410
that a man won't die
on his favorable day.

2003
01:41:26.540 --> 01:41:29.370
I mean, that is known.
That is known.

2004
01:41:29.500 --> 01:41:30.500
Chris!

2005
01:41:30.620 --> 01:41:32.910
Why isn't it possible?
Why isn't it possible, Chris?

2006
01:41:33.040 --> 01:41:35.620
Don't you understand what she's saying?
She's telling you to go!

2007
01:41:35.750 --> 01:41:38.290
- What are you waiting for?
- Nobody can tell her to go!

2008
01:41:38.410 --> 01:41:40.660
- (Horn honking)
- Thank you darling, for your troubles.

2009
01:41:40.790 --> 01:41:43.410
- Will you tell him to wait, Frank?
- Yeah, sure thing.

2010
01:41:43.540 --> 01:41:46.500
- Driver, they'll be right out!
- She's not leaving, Mom!

2011
01:41:46.620 --> 01:41:49.290
- You heard her, he's never been sick.
- He misunderstood me, Chris!

2012
01:41:49.410 --> 01:41:52.160
He simply told your father to kill pilots,
and covered himself in bed!

2013
01:41:52.290 --> 01:41:55.410
- You'd better answer him. Answer him, Annie!
- I packed your bag, darling.

2014
01:41:56.250 --> 01:41:57.040
What?

2015
01:41:58.500 --> 01:42:01.660
I packed your bag.
All you have to do is close it.

2016
01:42:02.660 --> 01:42:03.660
Huh.

2017
01:42:04.870 --> 01:42:08.040
- I am not closing anything.
- (Chris) Oh.

2018
01:42:08.790 --> 01:42:13.250
He asked me here
and I'm staying until he tells me to go.

2019
01:42:13.370 --> 01:42:15.000
Until Chris tells me.

2020
01:42:15.120 --> 01:42:17.540
- That's all. Now get out of here, George.
- But if that's how he feels...

2021
01:42:17.660 --> 01:42:20.040
That's all.
Nothing more till Christ comes,

2022
01:42:20.160 --> 01:42:23.160
about the case or Larry
as long as I'm here!

2023
01:42:23.290 --> 01:42:24.910
Now get out of here, George!

2024
01:42:28.000 --> 01:42:29.290
You tell me.

2025
01:42:30.540 --> 01:42:31.790
I want to hear you tell me.

2026
01:42:31.910 --> 01:42:33.540
(Somber music continues)

2027
01:42:38.660 --> 01:42:39.910
Go, George.

2028
01:42:48.160 --> 01:42:51.370
- (Ann) Hey, Georgie. Georgie.
- No, no, Annie. Annie.

2029
01:42:51.500 --> 01:42:53.660
Georgie, please,
don't take it that way!

2030
01:42:53.790 --> 01:42:56.250
What do you mean, you packed her bag?
How dare you pack her bag?

2031
01:42:56.370 --> 01:42:58.790
- How dare you pack her bag?
- She doesn't belong here!

2032
01:42:58.910 --> 01:43:00.160
Then I don't belong here?

2033
01:43:00.290 --> 01:43:01.290
God.

2034
01:43:02.540 --> 01:43:03.910
She's Larry's girl.

2035
01:43:04.040 --> 01:43:07.290
And I'm his brother and he's dead!

2036
01:43:08.790 --> 01:43:10.160
I'm marrying his girl.

2037
01:43:11.870 --> 01:43:13.410
- Never.
- Um...

2038
01:43:14.500 --> 01:43:17.290
Never in this world!

2039
01:43:17.410 --> 01:43:19.790
- You lost your mind?
- You have nothing to say!

2040
01:43:19.910 --> 01:43:22.250
I've got plenty to say!

2041
01:43:22.370 --> 01:43:26.410
Three and a half years
you've been talking like a maniac!

2042
01:43:26.540 --> 01:43:29.250
Nothing!
You have nothing to say!

2043
01:43:29.370 --> 01:43:32.750
Now I say he's coming back!

2044
01:43:32.870 --> 01:43:36.000
- And everybody has got to wait!
- Mother! Mother!

2045
01:43:36.120 --> 01:43:37.660
- Wait! Wait!
- How long? How long?

2046
01:43:37.790 --> 01:43:40.160
Until he comes!
Forever and ever till he comes!

2047
01:43:40.290 --> 01:43:41.410
Mother, I am going ahead with it!

2048
01:43:42.500 --> 01:43:43.540
Chris,

2049
01:43:44.410 --> 01:43:47.540
I have never said no to you
in my life.

2050
01:43:47.660 --> 01:43:49.410
Now I say no.

2051
01:43:49.540 --> 01:43:51.290
You'll never let him go
until I do it.

2052
01:43:51.410 --> 01:43:53.370
I'll never let him go!
You'll never let him go!

2053
01:43:53.500 --> 01:43:55.040
I have let him go!
I let him go a long...

2054
01:43:55.160 --> 01:43:57.410
- Then let your father go!
- (Joe) What?

2055
01:43:57.540 --> 01:43:59.250
Altogether!

2056
01:44:02.160 --> 01:44:05.660
Your brother... is alive, darling.

2057
01:44:05.790 --> 01:44:06.870
(Soft chanting)

2058
01:44:07.000 --> 01:44:09.040
Because if he's dead,

2059
01:44:09.160 --> 01:44:10.870
your father killed him.

2060
01:44:13.370 --> 01:44:15.120
Do you understand me now?

2061
01:44:17.540 --> 01:44:19.540
As long as you live,

2062
01:44:20.250 --> 01:44:22.870
that boy is alive.

2063
01:44:23.000 --> 01:44:27.290
God does not let a son
be killed by his father.

2064
01:44:29.120 --> 01:44:30.120
Now you see.

2065
01:44:31.160 --> 01:44:32.160
Don't you?

2066
01:44:35.160 --> 01:44:36.160
Now you see.

2067
01:44:36.790 --> 01:44:37.790
(Sniffs)

2068
01:44:55.290 --> 01:44:57.040
(Joe) She's out of her mind.

2069
01:45:00.120 --> 01:45:01.870
Then you did it?

2070
01:45:02.870 --> 01:45:05.040
He never flew a P-40.

2071
01:45:05.160 --> 01:45:07.040
- But the others...
- She's out of her mind.

2072
01:45:07.160 --> 01:45:08.290
Dad...

2073
01:45:09.790 --> 01:45:11.370
You did it?

2074
01:45:11.500 --> 01:45:14.910
He never flew a P-40.
What's the matter with you?

2075
01:45:17.500 --> 01:45:19.160
Then you did it to the others.

2076
01:45:19.290 --> 01:45:21.290
What's the matter with you?
What the hell is the matter with you?

2077
01:45:21.410 --> 01:45:23.660
- How could you do that?
- What is the matter with you?

2078
01:45:23.790 --> 01:45:26.870
- Dad, you killed 21 men.
- What, killed?

2079
01:45:27.000 --> 01:45:29.660
- You killed them. You murdered them, Dad.
- I didn't kill anybody!

2080
01:45:29.790 --> 01:45:32.790
Then explain it to me!
What did you do?

2081
01:45:33.370 --> 01:45:36.290
Explain it to me
or I'll tear you to pieces!

2082
01:45:36.410 --> 01:45:39.290
- Oh, come on, Chris.
- I want to know what you did!

2083
01:45:39.410 --> 01:45:43.540
Now what did you do?
You had 120 cracked engine heads!

2084
01:45:43.660 --> 01:45:45.870
- What did you do?
- If you're going to hang me...

2085
01:45:46.000 --> 01:45:47.500
- I'm listening.
- I can't defend myself.

2086
01:45:47.620 --> 01:45:50.160
God almighty, I am listening!

2087
01:45:50.290 --> 01:45:52.120
(Somber music and chanting)

2088
01:46:06.500 --> 01:46:07.660
You're a boy.

2089
01:46:09.790 --> 01:46:11.160
I'm in business.

2090
01:46:12.160 --> 01:46:14.910
A man... is in business.

2091
01:46:16.040 --> 01:46:19.790
You got 120 cracked,
you're out of business.

2092
01:46:21.000 --> 01:46:24.540
You got a process
and the process don't work,

2093
01:46:24.660 --> 01:46:26.160
you're out of business.

2094
01:46:26.750 --> 01:46:30.040
You don't know how to operate,
your stuff is no good,

2095
01:46:30.160 --> 01:46:31.790
they'll close you up.

2096
01:46:31.910 --> 01:46:35.040
They'll tear up your contracts!
What the hell's it to them?

2097
01:46:36.000 --> 01:46:39.290
You lay 40 years into a business

2098
01:46:39.410 --> 01:46:41.750
and they knock you out
in five minutes.

2099
01:46:43.000 --> 01:46:45.160
What could I do?

2100
01:46:45.290 --> 01:46:49.370
Let them just take 40 years?
Let them take my life away?

2101
01:46:50.910 --> 01:46:54.750
Chris, I never thought
that they would install 'em.

2102
01:46:54.870 --> 01:46:58.120
I swear to God, I thought
they'd stop 'em before anybody took off.

2103
01:46:58.250 --> 01:47:00.750
- Why'd you ship them out?
- By the time they'd spot 'em,

2104
01:47:00.870 --> 01:47:02.790
I thought I'd get
the process going again,

2105
01:47:02.910 --> 01:47:05.040
and I'd show them
that they needed me

2106
01:47:05.160 --> 01:47:07.910
and they would let it go by,
but weeks passed

2107
01:47:08.040 --> 01:47:11.040
and I got no kick-back,
and I was going to tell them.

2108
01:47:11.160 --> 01:47:14.160
- Then why didn't you tell them?
- Because it was too late.

2109
01:47:14.290 --> 01:47:17.870
It was in the paper,
it was all over the front page, it was...

2110
01:47:19.410 --> 01:47:21.160
Twenty-one went down.

2111
01:47:21.910 --> 01:47:23.160
It was too late.

2112
01:47:26.790 --> 01:47:31.040
Chris, they came with handcuffs
into the shop. What could I do?

2113
01:47:31.160 --> 01:47:32.910
(Nervous chuckle)

2114
01:47:33.040 --> 01:47:36.040
No, no, no.
I did it for you, Chris.

2115
01:47:36.160 --> 01:47:39.290
It was a chance
and I took it for you.

2116
01:47:40.500 --> 01:47:41.910
Look, I'm 61 years old.

2117
01:47:42.040 --> 01:47:45.620
When would I have another chance
to make something for ya, huh?

2118
01:47:45.750 --> 01:47:48.370
At 61 years old,
you don't get another chance, do ya?

2119
01:47:48.500 --> 01:47:51.160
- You knew they wouldn't hold up in the air.
- I didn't say that.

2120
01:47:51.290 --> 01:47:53.660
- You were going to warn them not to use them.
- That don't mean they wouldn't...

2121
01:47:53.790 --> 01:47:56.040
- It means you knew they'd crash.
- It don't mean that!

2122
01:47:56.160 --> 01:47:58.540
- Then you thought they'd crash!
- I was afraid maybe.

2123
01:47:58.660 --> 01:48:01.160
(Shouting) Oh! You were afraid!

2124
01:48:01.290 --> 01:48:02.540
Maybe!

2125
01:48:04.790 --> 01:48:07.160
God in heaven,
what kind of a man are you?

2126
01:48:07.290 --> 01:48:11.290
Kids were hanging in the air
by those heads. You knew that.

2127
01:48:11.910 --> 01:48:13.750
For you, Chris.

2128
01:48:13.870 --> 01:48:15.370
A business for you!

2129
01:48:15.500 --> 01:48:16.790
- For me?
- Yeah.

2130
01:48:16.910 --> 01:48:18.540
(Laughing)

2131
01:48:20.290 --> 01:48:22.290
Where...? Where...?
Where do you...?

2132
01:48:22.410 --> 01:48:25.250
Where...? Where...?
Where do you live...?

2133
01:48:26.660 --> 01:48:28.290
I was dying!

2134
01:48:29.500 --> 01:48:30.910
Every day!

2135
01:48:32.040 --> 01:48:35.290
You were killing my boys
and you did it for me?

2136
01:48:35.410 --> 01:48:38.910
What the hell do you think I was thinking of,
the goddamn business?

2137
01:48:39.040 --> 01:48:42.040
Is that as far as your mind can see,
the business?

2138
01:48:42.160 --> 01:48:45.040
What is that, huh?
The world, the business...

2139
01:48:45.160 --> 01:48:47.620
What the hell do you mean,
you did it for me?

2140
01:48:47.750 --> 01:48:49.290
Don't you have a country?

2141
01:48:51.040 --> 01:48:53.120
Don't you live in the world?

2142
01:48:54.620 --> 01:48:56.000
What the hell are you?

2143
01:48:57.540 --> 01:49:00.290
You're not even an animal!

2144
01:49:01.040 --> 01:49:03.410
No animal kills his own!

2145
01:49:03.540 --> 01:49:04.790
What are you?

2146
01:49:06.290 --> 01:49:07.410
What must I do to you?

2147
01:49:07.540 --> 01:49:10.250
I ought to tear the tongue
out of your mouth!

2148
01:49:10.370 --> 01:49:12.660
- No, no, no!
- What must I do, huh?

2149
01:49:12.790 --> 01:49:15.620
- What must I do?
- No, no, no. No. No.

2150
01:49:19.040 --> 01:49:20.250
Oh.

2151
01:49:20.370 --> 01:49:21.790
(Chris crying)

2152
01:49:24.000 --> 01:49:25.000
OK.

2153
01:49:25.790 --> 01:49:27.660
Oh, Jesus.

2154
01:49:31.160 --> 01:49:32.500
(Deep somber music)

2155
01:49:34.290 --> 01:49:36.040
What must I do?

2156
01:49:38.410 --> 01:49:40.250
- Chris.
- No.

2157
01:49:41.160 --> 01:49:42.660
- No.
- Chris.

2158
01:49:45.660 --> 01:49:46.660
Chris!

2159
01:49:46.790 --> 01:49:49.410
(♪ Leonard Cohen:
"It Seemed the Better Way")

2160
01:50:04.040 --> 01:50:06.040
♪ Seemed the better way

2161
01:50:08.500 --> 01:50:11.410
♪ When first I heard him speak

2162
01:50:13.410 --> 01:50:15.660
♪ Now it's much too late

2163
01:50:17.750 --> 01:50:20.750
♪ To turn the other cheek

2164
01:50:22.410 --> 01:50:24.750
♪ Sounded like the truth

2165
01:50:27.120 --> 01:50:29.290
♪ Seemed the better way

2166
01:50:31.750 --> 01:50:34.040
♪ Sounded like the truth

2167
01:50:35.910 --> 01:50:38.500
♪ But it's not the truth today

2168
01:50:40.870 --> 01:50:43.410
♪ I wonder what it was

2169
01:50:45.160 --> 01:50:48.160
♪ I wonder what it meant

2170
01:50:50.290 --> 01:50:52.790
♪ First he touched on love

2171
01:50:54.910 --> 01:50:57.040
♪ Then he touched on death

2172
01:50:59.540 --> 01:51:01.910
♪ Sounded like the truth

2173
01:51:04.000 --> 01:51:06.660
♪ Seemed the better way

2174
01:51:08.660 --> 01:51:11.370
♪ Sounded like the truth

2175
01:51:12.910 --> 01:51:15.660
♪ But it's not the truth today... ♪

2176
01:51:19.290 --> 01:51:21.910
- Any news?
- No news.

2177
01:51:22.040 --> 01:51:23.540
(Kate sniffs)

2178
01:51:23.660 --> 01:51:27.040
You can't stay here all night, dear.
Why don't you go to bed?

2179
01:51:27.160 --> 01:51:29.160
I'm waiting for Chris.

2180
01:51:30.290 --> 01:51:32.540
Don't worry about me, Jim,
I'm perfectly all right.

2181
01:51:32.660 --> 01:51:35.160
- It's almost two o'clock.
- I can't sleep.

2182
01:51:40.410 --> 01:51:42.790
- You have an emergency?
- Uh...

2183
01:51:42.910 --> 01:51:46.250
Somebody had a headache
and thought he was dying.

2184
01:51:47.160 --> 01:51:49.540
Half my patients are quite mad.

2185
01:51:50.370 --> 01:51:53.290
Nobody realizes how many people
are walking around loose,

2186
01:51:53.410 --> 01:51:56.160
and they are cracked as coconuts.

2187
01:51:57.410 --> 01:51:59.040
Agh! Money.

2188
01:51:59.870 --> 01:52:03.000
Money, money,
money, money, money, money.

2189
01:52:03.120 --> 01:52:06.910
You... You say it long enough
it doesn't mean anything.

2190
01:52:07.040 --> 01:52:10.790
Ha! Oh, how I'd love to be around
when that happens.

2191
01:52:11.790 --> 01:52:15.160
You're so childish, Jim.
Sometimes you are.

2192
01:52:17.790 --> 01:52:19.790
Kate, what happened?

2193
01:52:20.790 --> 01:52:21.790
I told you.

2194
01:52:22.660 --> 01:52:24.870
He had an argument with Joe.

2195
01:52:25.540 --> 01:52:27.410
Then he got in the car
and drove away.

2196
01:52:27.540 --> 01:52:30.660
- What kind of an argument?
- An argument.

2197
01:52:31.160 --> 01:52:33.290
Joe... (Sniffs)

2198
01:52:33.410 --> 01:52:36.000
He was crying
like a child before.

2199
01:52:36.790 --> 01:52:40.160
- They argued about Ann?
- No, not Ann.

2200
01:52:41.660 --> 01:52:43.040
Imagine.

2201
01:52:43.160 --> 01:52:46.410
She hasn't come out
of that room since he left.

2202
01:52:47.000 --> 01:52:48.910
All night in that room.

2203
01:52:49.040 --> 01:52:50.660
What did Joe do?

2204
01:52:51.790 --> 01:52:52.790
Tell him?

2205
01:52:55.250 --> 01:52:56.540
Tell him what?

2206
01:52:58.040 --> 01:53:00.250
Don't be afraid, Kate.

2207
01:53:01.750 --> 01:53:02.750
I know.

2208
01:53:04.500 --> 01:53:06.120
I've always known.

2209
01:53:06.910 --> 01:53:07.910
How?

2210
01:53:08.660 --> 01:53:11.370
Uh, it occurred to me
a long time ago.

2211
01:53:12.120 --> 01:53:14.790
(Sighs) I've always
had the feeling that,

2212
01:53:14.910 --> 01:53:18.660
somewhere at the back of his head,
Chris almost knew.

2213
01:53:18.790 --> 01:53:21.410
I didn't think it would come
as such a shock.

2214
01:53:21.540 --> 01:53:24.910
Chris would never know
how to live with a thing like that.

2215
01:53:26.040 --> 01:53:27.290
Oh.

2216
01:53:27.410 --> 01:53:30.410
It takes a certain talent for lying.

2217
01:53:31.660 --> 01:53:33.870
You have it, and I do.

2218
01:53:35.500 --> 01:53:36.750
But not him.

2219
01:53:37.660 --> 01:53:39.870
What do you mean,
he's not coming back?

2220
01:53:40.000 --> 01:53:41.790
Oh, no, he'll come back.

2221
01:53:43.540 --> 01:53:45.540
We all come back, Kate.

2222
01:53:47.120 --> 01:53:50.120
These private little revolutions,
they always die.

2223
01:53:50.250 --> 01:53:53.660
The... compromise is always made.

2224
01:53:56.370 --> 01:53:59.160
Ah, in a peculiar way,
Frank is right.

2225
01:54:00.790 --> 01:54:03.540
Every man does have a star.

2226
01:54:04.790 --> 01:54:07.120
It's the star of one's honesty.

2227
01:54:09.120 --> 01:54:11.660
And you spend your life
groping for it,

2228
01:54:11.790 --> 01:54:14.290
but... once it's out,

2229
01:54:15.910 --> 01:54:17.410
it never lights again.

2230
01:54:20.290 --> 01:54:21.910
I don't think he went very far.

2231
01:54:22.040 --> 01:54:26.660
He probably just wanted to be alone
to watch his star go out.

2232
01:54:27.750 --> 01:54:29.540
Just as long as he comes back.

2233
01:54:29.660 --> 01:54:30.660
(Sniffs)

2234
01:54:30.790 --> 01:54:32.370
I wish he wouldn't, Kate.

2235
01:54:33.660 --> 01:54:37.790
One year, I simply took off, I...
I went to New Orleans.

2236
01:54:39.290 --> 01:54:43.040
For two months,
I lived on bananas and milk,

2237
01:54:43.160 --> 01:54:45.910
and studied a certain disease.

2238
01:54:46.040 --> 01:54:48.410
It was beautiful.

2239
01:54:51.040 --> 01:54:53.910
And then she came, and she cried.

2240
01:54:55.410 --> 01:54:57.500
And I went back home with her.

2241
01:54:59.790 --> 01:55:02.540
Now I live in the usual darkness.

2242
01:55:06.370 --> 01:55:09.040
I... can't find myself.

2243
01:55:11.870 --> 01:55:15.660
It's even hard sometimes to remember
the kind of man I wanted to be.

2244
01:55:15.790 --> 01:55:16.790
I'm...

2245
01:55:18.000 --> 01:55:19.500
I'm a good husband.

2246
01:55:20.790 --> 01:55:23.790
And Chris is a good son.
He'll come back.

2247
01:55:27.120 --> 01:55:29.160
I have a feeling he's in the park.

2248
01:55:31.160 --> 01:55:32.870
I'll look around for him.

2249
01:55:37.620 --> 01:55:41.410
Put her to bed, Joe.
This is no good for what she's got.

2250
01:55:48.410 --> 01:55:49.910
What does he want here?

2251
01:55:51.540 --> 01:55:53.120
His friend's not home.

2252
01:55:54.120 --> 01:55:57.910
- I don't like him mixing in so much.
- It's too late, Joe. He knows.

2253
01:55:59.250 --> 01:56:00.370
How does he know?

2254
01:56:01.870 --> 01:56:03.790
He guessed a long time ago.

2255
01:56:05.620 --> 01:56:08.040
- I don't like that.
- Oh. What you don't like.

2256
01:56:08.160 --> 01:56:09.790
Yeah, what I don't like!

2257
01:56:09.910 --> 01:56:12.910
You can't go bulling yourself
through this one, Joe.

2258
01:56:13.790 --> 01:56:16.620
You better be smart now.
This thing is not over yet.

2259
01:56:18.120 --> 01:56:21.290
And what's she doing up there?
She don't come out of the room.

2260
01:56:21.410 --> 01:56:23.500
I don't know.
What is she doing?

2261
01:56:30.160 --> 01:56:31.410
Sit down.

2262
01:56:31.910 --> 01:56:33.620
Stop being mad.

2263
01:56:33.750 --> 01:56:36.040
You want to live?
You better figure out your life.

2264
01:56:37.910 --> 01:56:39.410
She don't know, does she?

2265
01:56:40.160 --> 01:56:44.160
She saw Chris storming out of here.
It's one and one. She knows how to add.

2266
01:56:45.410 --> 01:56:47.620
- You think I ought to talk to her?
- Don't ask me, Joe.

2267
01:56:47.750 --> 01:56:48.910
Then who do I ask?

2268
01:56:50.620 --> 01:56:52.410
I don't think
she'll do nothing about it. Huh?

2269
01:56:52.540 --> 01:56:54.660
- You're asking me again.
- Yeah, I'm askin' you!

2270
01:56:54.790 --> 01:56:58.000
What am I, a stranger?
I thought I had a family here.

2271
01:56:58.120 --> 01:57:00.410
- What happened to my family?
- You've got a family.

2272
01:57:00.540 --> 01:57:04.160
I'm simply telling you
I have no strength left to think anymore.

2273
01:57:04.290 --> 01:57:05.790
You have no strength!

2274
01:57:05.910 --> 01:57:08.410
The moment there's trouble
you have no strength!

2275
01:57:09.290 --> 01:57:11.870
You're doing
the same thing again, Joe.

2276
01:57:12.000 --> 01:57:14.410
All your life,
whenever there's trouble,

2277
01:57:14.540 --> 01:57:16.790
you yell at me
and think that settles it.

2278
01:57:19.870 --> 01:57:21.160
Then what do I do?

2279
01:57:22.250 --> 01:57:23.540
Huh?

2280
01:57:23.660 --> 01:57:25.790
Tell me, talk to me.
What do I do?

2281
01:57:30.040 --> 01:57:32.410
- Hey.
- Joe, I've been thinking this way.

2282
01:57:32.540 --> 01:57:34.660
- If he comes back...
- What do you mean, if?

2283
01:57:34.790 --> 01:57:35.790
He's comin' back!

2284
01:57:37.540 --> 01:57:39.910
I think if you sit him down

2285
01:57:40.040 --> 01:57:42.910
and you explain yourself...

2286
01:57:43.040 --> 01:57:44.790
I mean, you ought
to make it clear to him

2287
01:57:44.910 --> 01:57:47.540
that you know
you did a terrible thing.

2288
01:57:47.660 --> 01:57:51.040
I mean, if he saw that you realized
what you did. Do you see?

2289
01:57:52.000 --> 01:57:53.500
What ice would that cut?

2290
01:57:53.620 --> 01:57:57.500
I mean, if you told him
that you wanted to pay for what you did.

2291
01:57:58.250 --> 01:57:59.540
How can I pay?

2292
01:58:01.290 --> 01:58:05.160
Tell him... Tell him
you're willing to go to prison.

2293
01:58:07.370 --> 01:58:09.750
(Laughing)

2294
01:58:09.870 --> 01:58:13.370
Oh! Whoa!
I'm willing to go...?

2295
01:58:13.500 --> 01:58:16.790
You wouldn't have to go!
He wouldn't ask you to.

2296
01:58:16.910 --> 01:58:18.750
But if you told him
that you wanted to,

2297
01:58:18.870 --> 01:58:22.040
if he could feel that you wanted to pay,
maybe he'd forgive you.

2298
01:58:23.910 --> 01:58:26.500
He'd forgive me? For what?

2299
01:58:26.620 --> 01:58:27.790
You know what I mean.

2300
01:58:27.910 --> 01:58:29.660
No, I don't know
what you mean.

2301
01:58:29.790 --> 01:58:32.910
You wanted money,
so I made money.

2302
01:58:33.040 --> 01:58:36.410
What must I be forgiven?
You wanted money, didn't you?

2303
01:58:36.540 --> 01:58:37.790
I didn't want it that way!

2304
01:58:37.910 --> 01:58:41.120
I didn't want it that way either!
What difference is it what you want?

2305
01:58:41.660 --> 01:58:43.290
I spoiled you, the both of you!

2306
01:58:43.410 --> 01:58:46.290
I should have put him out
when he was ten

2307
01:58:46.410 --> 01:58:48.000
like I was put out!

2308
01:58:48.660 --> 01:58:50.540
Make him earn his keep!

2309
01:58:50.660 --> 01:58:53.660
Then he'd know
how a buck is made in this world!

2310
01:58:53.790 --> 01:58:55.870
Gee! Huh! Forgiven!

2311
01:58:57.160 --> 01:59:00.790
I could live
off a quarter a day myself!

2312
01:59:00.910 --> 01:59:02.290
But I got a family, so I work!

2313
01:59:02.410 --> 01:59:05.750
Joe, it don't excuse it
that you did it for the family!

2314
01:59:05.870 --> 01:59:08.410
- It's got to excuse it.
- There is something bigger than family to him.

2315
01:59:08.540 --> 01:59:10.500
- There's nothing bigger!
- To him!

2316
01:59:10.620 --> 01:59:12.750
There's nothin' he could do
I wouldn't forgive,

2317
01:59:12.870 --> 01:59:14.000
'cause he's my son.

2318
01:59:14.120 --> 01:59:16.370
Because I'm his father
and he's my son.

2319
01:59:16.500 --> 01:59:18.660
- I tell you...
- There's nothing bigger than that!

2320
01:59:18.790 --> 01:59:20.660
You're gonna tell him,
you understand?

2321
01:59:20.790 --> 01:59:23.040
Because I'm his father
and he's my son.

2322
01:59:23.160 --> 01:59:25.370
If there's something bigger,
I'll put a bullet in my head.

2323
01:59:25.500 --> 01:59:27.290
- You stop that.
- No, you heard me.

2324
01:59:28.040 --> 01:59:29.660
Now you know
what to tell him.

2325
01:59:35.040 --> 01:59:36.040
But he...

2326
01:59:37.040 --> 01:59:39.910
He wouldn't...
put me away, though.

2327
01:59:40.040 --> 01:59:41.540
Would he?
He wouldn't do that.

2328
01:59:42.410 --> 01:59:45.290
He loved you, Joe.
You broke his heart.

2329
01:59:49.410 --> 01:59:50.790
But to put me away...

2330
01:59:50.910 --> 01:59:52.660
(Somber instrumental music)

2331
01:59:53.790 --> 01:59:55.540
- I don't know.
- Ah.

2332
01:59:56.370 --> 01:59:59.410
I'm beginning to think
that we don't really know him.

2333
01:59:59.540 --> 02:00:03.620
They say in the war he was such a killer.
Here he was always afraid of mice.

2334
02:00:04.540 --> 02:00:05.790
I don't know.

2335
02:00:06.870 --> 02:00:08.410
I don't know what he'll do.

2336
02:00:11.000 --> 02:00:12.370
Goddamn.

2337
02:00:13.660 --> 02:00:17.910
If Larry was alive,
he wouldn't act like this.

2338
02:00:18.660 --> 02:00:21.160
He understood
the way the world was made.

2339
02:00:21.290 --> 02:00:22.790
He listened to me.

2340
02:00:22.910 --> 02:00:25.790
To him, the world had a 40-foot front.

2341
02:00:25.910 --> 02:00:27.870
It ended at the building line.

2342
02:00:28.000 --> 02:00:31.160
This one... Ha!
Everything bothers him.

2343
02:00:31.750 --> 02:00:34.410
You make a deal,
overcharge two cents,

2344
02:00:34.540 --> 02:00:36.250
and his hair falls out!

2345
02:00:37.000 --> 02:00:38.790
He don't understand money.

2346
02:00:40.910 --> 02:00:43.910
It was too easy.
It came too easy.

2347
02:00:50.660 --> 02:00:51.660
Ah.

2348
02:00:54.790 --> 02:00:55.790
Yes, sir.

2349
02:00:58.790 --> 02:00:59.790
Larry.

2350
02:01:03.000 --> 02:01:05.120
That was a boy we lost.

2351
02:01:07.540 --> 02:01:08.540
Larry.

2352
02:01:12.290 --> 02:01:13.290
Larry.

2353
02:01:23.410 --> 02:01:25.040
What am I gonna do, Kate?

2354
02:01:26.660 --> 02:01:28.790
Huh? What am I gonna do?

2355
02:01:28.910 --> 02:01:30.250
Joe.

2356
02:01:30.370 --> 02:01:31.540
- Joe, please.
- Huh?

2357
02:01:31.660 --> 02:01:34.660
Come on. You'll be all right,
nothing's going to happen.

2358
02:01:34.790 --> 02:01:37.250
It's only for you, Kate,
the both of you.

2359
02:01:37.370 --> 02:01:40.250
- That's all I ever lived for.
- I know, darling.

2360
02:01:40.370 --> 02:01:42.160
I know. I know.

2361
02:01:42.290 --> 02:01:43.290
I know.

2362
02:01:46.870 --> 02:01:49.660
(Ann) Why do you stay up?
I'll tell you when he comes.

2363
02:02:03.620 --> 02:02:05.910
You didn't eat no supper, did you?

2364
02:02:06.040 --> 02:02:08.000
- Why don't you make her something?
- Sure.

2365
02:02:08.120 --> 02:02:09.870
Never mind, Kate, I'm all right.

2366
02:02:15.160 --> 02:02:17.410
There's something
I want to tell you.

2367
02:02:20.790 --> 02:02:22.910
I'm not going
to do anything about it.

2368
02:02:23.040 --> 02:02:24.540
She's a good girl. See? She's...

2369
02:02:24.660 --> 02:02:26.750
I'll do nothing about Joe,

2370
02:02:27.370 --> 02:02:29.660
but you're going
to do something for me.

2371
02:02:33.250 --> 02:02:35.870
You made Chris feel guilty with me.

2372
02:02:36.000 --> 02:02:38.040
Whether you wanted to or not,

2373
02:02:38.160 --> 02:02:40.750
you crippled him in front of me.

2374
02:02:43.000 --> 02:02:46.040
I would like you to tell him
that Larry is dead

2375
02:02:46.160 --> 02:02:47.540
and that you know it.

2376
02:02:48.540 --> 02:02:49.790
You understand me?

2377
02:02:51.160 --> 02:02:55.120
I am not going out of here alone.
There's no life for me that way. I...

2378
02:02:56.160 --> 02:02:58.410
I want you to set him free.

2379
02:02:59.250 --> 02:03:01.790
And then I promise you
everything will end,

2380
02:03:01.910 --> 02:03:04.120
and we'll go away, and...

2381
02:03:05.290 --> 02:03:06.410
that's all.

2382
02:03:11.500 --> 02:03:12.500
Hey.

2383
02:03:14.790 --> 02:03:16.910
You'll do that, huh?
You'll... You'll tell him.

2384
02:03:20.870 --> 02:03:24.040
I know what I'm asking, Kate.
You had two sons.

2385
02:03:24.160 --> 02:03:26.250
But you've only got one now.

2386
02:03:27.410 --> 02:03:29.370
You'll tell him, huh?

2387
02:03:30.160 --> 02:03:33.410
And you have got to say it to him
so he knows you mean it.

2388
02:03:37.160 --> 02:03:38.160
My dear,

2389
02:03:40.160 --> 02:03:42.370
if the boy was dead,

2390
02:03:43.410 --> 02:03:46.500
it wouldn't depend on my words
to make Chris know it.

2391
02:03:47.410 --> 02:03:51.870
The night he gets in your bed,
his heart will dry up

2392
02:03:52.410 --> 02:03:55.370
because he knows and you know

2393
02:03:56.120 --> 02:04:00.500
to his dying day
he'll wait for his brother.

2394
02:04:02.660 --> 02:04:05.410
No, dear. No such thing.

2395
02:04:07.160 --> 02:04:11.040
You're going in the morning,
and you're going alone.

2396
02:04:12.160 --> 02:04:13.620
That's your life.

2397
02:04:14.620 --> 02:04:17.000
That's your lonely life.

2398
02:04:26.500 --> 02:04:27.500
Larry is dead, Kate!

2399
02:04:27.620 --> 02:04:31.250
- Don't speak to me!
- I said he's dead. I know.

2400
02:04:32.250 --> 02:04:34.870
He crashed off the coast of China
November 25th.

2401
02:04:35.000 --> 02:04:36.790
His engine didn't fail him,
but he died.

2402
02:04:38.040 --> 02:04:39.040
I know.

2403
02:04:40.290 --> 02:04:41.540
How did he die?

2404
02:04:42.250 --> 02:04:43.870
You're lying to me.

2405
02:04:44.000 --> 02:04:45.870
If you know, how did he die?

2406
02:04:46.000 --> 02:04:48.040
I loved him.
You know that I loved him.

2407
02:04:48.160 --> 02:04:51.540
Would I have looked at anyone else
if I wasn't sure? That's enough for you!

2408
02:04:51.660 --> 02:04:54.790
- What's enough for me? What're you talking about?
- You're hurting my wrist!

2409
02:04:54.910 --> 02:04:57.290
- What are you talking about?
- Joe, go in the house.

2410
02:04:57.910 --> 02:05:00.160
- Why should I...?
- Please! Go.

2411
02:05:00.290 --> 02:05:02.290
(Melancholic stringed music)

2412
02:05:10.290 --> 02:05:12.620
You let me know
when he comes, though, huh?

2413
02:05:20.160 --> 02:05:22.000
- What's that?
- First you've got to understand,

2414
02:05:22.120 --> 02:05:25.290
when I came,
I didn't have any idea that Joe...

2415
02:05:26.410 --> 02:05:28.910
I had nothing against him or you.

2416
02:05:29.040 --> 02:05:31.160
I came to get married.
I hoped...

2417
02:05:32.660 --> 02:05:33.660
So...

2418
02:05:34.790 --> 02:05:38.410
So, I didn't bring this
to hurt you, Kate.

2419
02:05:39.290 --> 02:05:42.370
I thought that I would show it to you
only if there was no other way

2420
02:05:42.500 --> 02:05:45.910
to settle Larry... in your mind.

2421
02:05:47.540 --> 02:05:48.540
Larry?

2422
02:05:51.160 --> 02:05:53.250
He wrote it to me
just before he...

2423
02:05:57.660 --> 02:05:59.410
I'm not trying to hurt you, Kate.

2424
02:05:59.540 --> 02:06:02.120
You're making me do this.
Now, just remember...

2425
02:06:03.500 --> 02:06:04.500
Remember.

2426
02:06:23.620 --> 02:06:24.620
No!

2427
02:06:29.750 --> 02:06:33.250
I have been so lonely, Kate.
I can't leave here alone again.

2428
02:06:33.370 --> 02:06:35.870
- Oh, my God, no!
- You made me show it to you.

2429
02:06:36.000 --> 02:06:38.790
I told you a hundred times
and you wouldn't believe me.

2430
02:06:38.910 --> 02:06:41.120
Why wouldn't you believe me?
Oh. Kate, dear.

2431
02:06:41.660 --> 02:06:43.120
Kate, please.

2432
02:06:43.870 --> 02:06:45.160
Kate, I'm so sorry.

2433
02:06:45.910 --> 02:06:47.410
I'm so sorry.

2434
02:06:48.290 --> 02:06:49.500
What's the matter?

2435
02:06:53.750 --> 02:06:55.910
Where were you? Huh?

2436
02:06:56.040 --> 02:06:58.290
You're all perspired.
Where were you?

2437
02:06:59.160 --> 02:07:01.120
Drove around a little.

2438
02:07:02.040 --> 02:07:03.540
Thought you'd be gone.

2439
02:07:04.410 --> 02:07:06.540
Where do I go?
I have nowhere to go.

2440
02:07:07.660 --> 02:07:08.870
Where's Dad?

2441
02:07:09.660 --> 02:07:12.660
He's, uh...
He's inside lying down.

2442
02:07:15.410 --> 02:07:18.410
Sit down, both of you.
I'll say what there is to say.

2443
02:07:19.290 --> 02:07:21.410
(Kate) I didn't hear the car.

2444
02:07:21.540 --> 02:07:25.910
- I left it in the garage.
- (Sobbing) Jim's out looking for you.

2445
02:07:26.040 --> 02:07:27.370
I'm going away.

2446
02:07:29.910 --> 02:07:34.160
There are a couple of firms in Cleveland,
I think I can get a place. I mean...

2447
02:07:36.040 --> 02:07:37.660
I'm going away for good.

2448
02:07:38.790 --> 02:07:41.410
I know what you're thinking,
Annie. It's true.

2449
02:07:41.540 --> 02:07:43.160
I'm yellow.

2450
02:07:44.160 --> 02:07:48.620
I was made yellow in this house
'cause I suspected my father

2451
02:07:48.750 --> 02:07:50.620
and I did nothing about it.

2452
02:07:52.290 --> 02:07:54.660
If I knew that night when I came home
what I know now,

2453
02:07:54.790 --> 02:07:57.660
he'd be in the district attorney's office
by this time.

2454
02:07:58.910 --> 02:08:00.500
I'd have brought him there.

2455
02:08:02.620 --> 02:08:05.370
Now if I look at him,
all I'm able to do is cry.

2456
02:08:05.500 --> 02:08:07.620
What are you talking about?
What else can you do?

2457
02:08:07.750 --> 02:08:08.750
(Kate sniffs)

2458
02:08:08.870 --> 02:08:10.160
I could jail him.

2459
02:08:11.790 --> 02:08:15.040
I could jail him
if I were human anymore.

2460
02:08:15.160 --> 02:08:18.040
But I'm like everybody else now.

2461
02:08:18.660 --> 02:08:21.370
I'm practical now.
You made me practical.

2462
02:08:21.500 --> 02:08:24.910
- But you have to be.
- The cats in that alley are practical!

2463
02:08:26.120 --> 02:08:29.540
The bums who ran away
when we were fighting were practical!

2464
02:08:29.660 --> 02:08:31.870
Only the dead ones
weren't practical!

2465
02:08:32.000 --> 02:08:35.370
But now I'm practical,
and I spit on myself!

2466
02:08:39.910 --> 02:08:42.000
I'm going away. I'm going now.

2467
02:08:42.120 --> 02:08:43.910
- No, I'm coming with you.
- No, Ann.

2468
02:08:44.040 --> 02:08:47.120
Chris, I don't ask you
to do anything about Joe.

2469
02:08:47.250 --> 02:08:49.790
- You do, you do.
- No, I swear I never will.

2470
02:08:49.910 --> 02:08:51.750
In your heart you always will.

2471
02:08:53.370 --> 02:08:55.750
- Then do what you have to do.
- Do what?

2472
02:08:56.500 --> 02:08:58.540
Huh? What is there to do?

2473
02:08:58.660 --> 02:09:00.790
I looked all night
for a reason to make him suffer.

2474
02:09:00.910 --> 02:09:02.910
- There's reason, there's reason.
- What?

2475
02:09:03.040 --> 02:09:06.040
Do I raise the dead
when I put him behind bars?

2476
02:09:07.540 --> 02:09:09.040
Then what'll I do it for?

2477
02:09:09.160 --> 02:09:12.120
We used to shoot a man
who acted like a dog!

2478
02:09:12.250 --> 02:09:16.160
But honor was real there.
You were protecting something.

2479
02:09:16.290 --> 02:09:19.620
But here? This is the land
of the great big dogs!

2480
02:09:19.750 --> 02:09:22.410
You don't love a man here,
you eat him!

2481
02:09:23.250 --> 02:09:24.750
That's the principle.

2482
02:09:25.660 --> 02:09:27.040
It's the only one we live by.

2483
02:09:27.160 --> 02:09:30.120
Just happened to kill a few people
this time, that's all.

2484
02:09:30.250 --> 02:09:32.250
The world's that way.

2485
02:09:34.040 --> 02:09:37.250
How can I take it out on him?
What sense does that make?

2486
02:09:39.160 --> 02:09:40.290
This is a zoo.

2487
02:09:41.790 --> 02:09:44.120
- A zoo.
- You know what he's got to do. Tell him.

2488
02:09:44.250 --> 02:09:45.910
- Let him go.
- I won't let him go!

2489
02:09:46.040 --> 02:09:48.540
Tell him what he's got to do!
Then I will.

2490
02:09:51.910 --> 02:09:52.910
(Joe) Hey.

2491
02:09:53.750 --> 02:09:54.750
Listen.

2492
02:09:56.410 --> 02:09:57.500
No, no, hey.

2493
02:09:57.620 --> 02:10:00.250
What's the matter with you?
I want to talk to you.

2494
02:10:00.370 --> 02:10:02.160
I got nothing to say to you.

2495
02:10:04.160 --> 02:10:05.540
I want to...

2496
02:10:06.290 --> 02:10:08.410
Hey, come here.

2497
02:10:08.540 --> 02:10:09.790
I want to talk to you.

2498
02:10:09.910 --> 02:10:12.750
Don't do that!
I'm going to hurt you if you do that.

2499
02:10:12.870 --> 02:10:14.750
There is nothing to say.

2500
02:10:15.660 --> 02:10:17.120
So say it quick.

2501
02:10:22.660 --> 02:10:23.660
All right.

2502
02:10:27.660 --> 02:10:29.620
What exactly is the matter?

2503
02:10:31.500 --> 02:10:32.660
What's the matter?

2504
02:10:34.370 --> 02:10:38.160
Have you got too much money?
Is that what bothers you?

2505
02:10:40.790 --> 02:10:42.290
It bothers me.

2506
02:10:44.790 --> 02:10:46.540
If you can't get used to it,

2507
02:10:47.540 --> 02:10:49.290
then just throw it away.

2508
02:10:50.370 --> 02:10:51.660
You hear me?

2509
02:10:51.790 --> 02:10:55.290
Take every cent and...
and give it to charity.

2510
02:10:56.040 --> 02:10:57.410
Throw it in the sewer.

2511
02:10:58.660 --> 02:11:01.370
Does that settle it?
In the sewer, that's all.

2512
02:11:03.120 --> 02:11:05.290
You think I'm kidding.

2513
02:11:06.120 --> 02:11:07.410
Uh-uh.

2514
02:11:07.540 --> 02:11:09.250
I'm tellin' you what to do.

2515
02:11:10.290 --> 02:11:13.000
If it's dirty, just burn it.

2516
02:11:13.620 --> 02:11:15.910
It's your money,
that's not my money.

2517
02:11:18.120 --> 02:11:19.410
I'm a dead man.

2518
02:11:20.910 --> 02:11:23.160
I'm an old, dead man.

2519
02:11:23.790 --> 02:11:25.540
Nothing is mine.

2520
02:11:25.660 --> 02:11:27.620
(Melancholic music continues)

2521
02:11:29.910 --> 02:11:30.910
Hey.

2522
02:11:35.290 --> 02:11:38.120
Come on.
Talk to me, huh? Come on.

2523
02:11:41.120 --> 02:11:42.540
What do you want to do?

2524
02:11:45.120 --> 02:11:47.000
It's not what I want to do.

2525
02:11:48.540 --> 02:11:50.500
It's what you want to do.

2526
02:11:53.500 --> 02:11:55.290
What should I want to do?

2527
02:12:00.500 --> 02:12:01.500
Oh.

2528
02:12:04.500 --> 02:12:05.500
Jail.

2529
02:12:08.540 --> 02:12:10.540
You want me to go to jail?

2530
02:12:14.160 --> 02:12:15.160
All right.

2531
02:12:18.790 --> 02:12:22.000
If you want me to go, say so.

2532
02:12:25.620 --> 02:12:27.540
Is that where I belong?

2533
02:12:29.750 --> 02:12:30.910
Then tell me so.

2534
02:12:36.160 --> 02:12:38.910
What's the matter?
Why can't you tell me?

2535
02:12:39.660 --> 02:12:42.250
You say everything else to me,
say that.

2536
02:12:46.500 --> 02:12:49.790
I'll tell you why you can't say it.

2537
02:12:51.660 --> 02:12:55.540
It's because you know
I don't belong there.

2538
02:12:57.540 --> 02:12:59.160
Because you know.

2539
02:13:05.620 --> 02:13:09.540
I mean, who...?
Who worked for nothin' in that war? Huh?

2540
02:13:10.250 --> 02:13:13.250
When they work for nothin',
I'll work for nothin'.

2541
02:13:13.870 --> 02:13:16.620
You think they shipped a gun

2542
02:13:16.750 --> 02:13:20.250
or a truck outta Detroit
before they got their price?

2543
02:13:21.290 --> 02:13:23.160
Is that clean? Pss!

2544
02:13:25.660 --> 02:13:28.000
It's dollars and cents.

2545
02:13:28.500 --> 02:13:30.540
Nickels and dimes.

2546
02:13:32.000 --> 02:13:33.870
War and peace.

2547
02:13:38.040 --> 02:13:39.660
Nickels and dimes.

2548
02:13:41.790 --> 02:13:43.040
What's clean?

2549
02:13:45.370 --> 02:13:48.870
Half the goddamn country
has gotta go if I go.

2550
02:13:49.500 --> 02:13:51.750
Yeah. And that's why
you can't tell me.

2551
02:13:53.620 --> 02:13:55.620
Yeah, that's exactly why.

2552
02:13:56.160 --> 02:13:58.660
Then why am I bad?

2553
02:13:58.790 --> 02:14:03.000
Oh, I know you're no worse
than most men, but...

2554
02:14:05.160 --> 02:14:07.540
...I thought you were better.

2555
02:14:10.410 --> 02:14:12.410
I never saw you as a man.

2556
02:14:12.540 --> 02:14:15.910
I saw you... as my father.

2557
02:14:18.040 --> 02:14:20.910
And I can't look at you
this way, I...

2558
02:14:23.160 --> 02:14:25.540
- I can't look at myself.
- Come on.

2559
02:14:27.910 --> 02:14:30.750
- Oh, no, give me that!
- No, he's going to read it.

2560
02:14:32.410 --> 02:14:33.620
Larry.

2561
02:14:34.290 --> 02:14:36.250
- He wrote it to me the day he died.
- Larry?

2562
02:14:36.370 --> 02:14:38.540
Chris, that's not for you.
Joe, go away.

2563
02:14:38.660 --> 02:14:41.540
- No. She said something about Larry.
- Joe, go to the street. Just go to the street.

2564
02:14:41.660 --> 02:14:44.250
Chris, don't. Don't tell him.

2565
02:14:50.910 --> 02:14:53.620
Three and one half years talking.

2566
02:14:57.250 --> 02:14:58.290
Talking.

2567
02:15:01.160 --> 02:15:03.660
Now you tell me
what you must do.

2568
02:15:03.790 --> 02:15:06.660
This is how he died. Huh?

2569
02:15:08.370 --> 02:15:10.160
Now tell me where you belong.

2570
02:15:12.870 --> 02:15:13.870
Huh?

2571
02:15:15.120 --> 02:15:17.410
- Chris... (Mumbles)
- What?

2572
02:15:17.910 --> 02:15:20.910
A man can't be a Jesus
in this world!

2573
02:15:21.040 --> 02:15:24.540
Oh, no, no.
I know all about the world, I...

2574
02:15:25.620 --> 02:15:27.790
I know the whole crap story.

2575
02:15:28.790 --> 02:15:30.160
Now listen to this

2576
02:15:30.290 --> 02:15:34.290
and tell me
what a man has got to be.

2577
02:15:43.500 --> 02:15:45.410
"My dear Ann..."

2578
02:15:45.540 --> 02:15:47.410
Are you...?
Are you listening?

2579
02:15:48.160 --> 02:15:49.160
Huh?

2580
02:15:50.660 --> 02:15:53.910
He wrote this the day he died!

2581
02:15:54.660 --> 02:15:56.620
So, listen! Don't cry!

2582
02:15:57.290 --> 02:16:00.290
- Listen!
- OK. OK.

2583
02:16:12.250 --> 02:16:13.790
"My dear Ann...

2584
02:16:16.160 --> 02:16:18.620
...it is impossible to put down
the things I feel,

2585
02:16:18.750 --> 02:16:20.750
but I got to tell you something.

2586
02:16:23.040 --> 02:16:26.000
Yesterday, they flew in
a load of papers from the States

2587
02:16:26.120 --> 02:16:29.540
and I read about Dad
and your father being convicted.

2588
02:16:33.000 --> 02:16:34.790
I can't express myself.

2589
02:16:36.370 --> 02:16:40.620
I can't tell you how I feel.
I can't bear to live anymore."

2590
02:16:40.750 --> 02:16:42.410
(Sobbing)

2591
02:16:43.410 --> 02:16:46.040
"Last night, I circled the base
for 20 minutes

2592
02:16:46.160 --> 02:16:48.160
before I could bring myself in.

2593
02:16:50.160 --> 02:16:51.870
How could he have done that?

2594
02:16:54.290 --> 02:16:56.620
Every day, three or four men
never come back

2595
02:16:56.750 --> 02:17:00.160
and he sits back there
doing business.

2596
02:17:04.250 --> 02:17:06.750
I don't know how to tell you
how I feel.

2597
02:17:08.040 --> 02:17:09.790
I can't face anybody.

2598
02:17:12.500 --> 02:17:16.410
I'm going out on a mission
in a couple of minutes.

2599
02:17:18.500 --> 02:17:21.160
They will probably
report me missing.

2600
02:17:23.910 --> 02:17:27.250
If they do, I want you to know
that you mustn't wait for me.

2601
02:17:28.660 --> 02:17:29.660
I tell you, Ann...

2602
02:17:31.660 --> 02:17:34.040
...if I had him here now,
I could kill him."

2603
02:17:41.160 --> 02:17:43.290
(Sorrowful music slowly building)

2604
02:18:06.120 --> 02:18:08.000
Now blame the world.

2605
02:18:52.660 --> 02:18:54.500
Do you understand that letter?

2606
02:19:04.160 --> 02:19:05.160
Yes, I...

2607
02:19:07.660 --> 02:19:09.750
I think I do.

2608
02:19:15.120 --> 02:19:18.790
Get the car. I'll put on my jacket.

2609
02:19:19.910 --> 02:19:21.620
(Kate) Why are you going?

2610
02:19:21.750 --> 02:19:24.540
You'll sleep.
Why are you going?

2611
02:19:25.540 --> 02:19:27.040
No. No.

2612
02:19:27.620 --> 02:19:28.660
I...

2613
02:19:28.790 --> 02:19:30.910
I can't sleep here. No.

2614
02:19:33.000 --> 02:19:34.750
I'll feel better if I go.

2615
02:19:34.870 --> 02:19:36.410
You're so foolish.

2616
02:19:36.540 --> 02:19:39.410
Larry was your son too, wasn't he?

2617
02:19:39.540 --> 02:19:43.040
You know he would never tell you
to do this.

2618
02:19:50.000 --> 02:19:51.000
Then...

2619
02:19:52.000 --> 02:19:54.620
...what is this
if it isn't telling me?

2620
02:20:02.660 --> 02:20:04.540
Yes, he was my son.

2621
02:20:08.500 --> 02:20:10.790
But I think, to him...

2622
02:20:12.790 --> 02:20:14.870
...they were all my sons.

2623
02:20:18.370 --> 02:20:20.370
And I guess they were.

2624
02:20:23.370 --> 02:20:25.290
I guess they were.

2625
02:20:34.370 --> 02:20:37.290
I'll... I'll be right down.

2626
02:20:48.790 --> 02:20:50.370
(Coughing)

2627
02:21:15.120 --> 02:21:16.290
You're not taking him.

2628
02:21:16.410 --> 02:21:18.290
I'm taking him.

2629
02:21:18.410 --> 02:21:19.540
It's up to you.

2630
02:21:19.660 --> 02:21:22.250
If you tell him to stay, he'll stay.
Now go and tell him.

2631
02:21:22.370 --> 02:21:23.750
Nobody could stop him now.

2632
02:21:23.870 --> 02:21:25.250
You'll stop him.

2633
02:21:26.160 --> 02:21:30.040
How long do you think he'll live in prison?
Are you trying to kill him?

2634
02:21:30.160 --> 02:21:33.790
- I thought you read this.
- The war is over.

2635
02:21:33.910 --> 02:21:36.160
Didn't you hear? It's over.

2636
02:21:37.910 --> 02:21:41.750
Then what was Larry to you?
A stone that fell into the water?

2637
02:21:43.250 --> 02:21:44.290
Huh?

2638
02:21:46.120 --> 02:21:48.290
It's not enough for him to be sorry.

2639
02:21:48.410 --> 02:21:50.000
(Music becomes sinister)

2640
02:21:50.120 --> 02:21:53.660
Larry didn't kill himself
to make you and Dad sorry.

2641
02:21:54.160 --> 02:21:56.040
What more can we be?

2642
02:21:56.910 --> 02:21:59.290
You can be better!

2643
02:22:01.120 --> 02:22:02.660
Once and for all you can know

2644
02:22:02.790 --> 02:22:05.870
that there's a universe
of people outside

2645
02:22:06.000 --> 02:22:08.290
and you are responsible to it.

2646
02:22:09.160 --> 02:22:10.750
And unless you know that,

2647
02:22:11.660 --> 02:22:15.410
you threw away your son
'cause that's why he died!

2648
02:22:15.540 --> 02:22:16.540
(Gunshot)

2649
02:22:44.290 --> 02:22:46.040
- No. No, no, no.
- (Kate) Joe!

2650
02:22:46.160 --> 02:22:47.910
Dad, Dad, Dad, Dad, Dad. Dad!

2651
02:22:48.040 --> 02:22:49.500
(Kate) Joe!

2652
02:22:49.620 --> 02:22:51.910
Joe! Joe!

2653
02:22:52.040 --> 02:22:54.290
(Sobbing) Mom, I didn't mean to...!

2654
02:22:54.410 --> 02:22:55.410
(Kate) Don't.

2655
02:22:57.540 --> 02:22:58.540
Don't, dear.

2656
02:22:59.370 --> 02:23:02.000
- Don't take it on yourself.
- (Chris sobbing)

2657
02:23:02.120 --> 02:23:03.500
Forget now.

2658
02:23:04.290 --> 02:23:05.370
Live.

2659
02:23:05.500 --> 02:23:06.910
Shh.

2660
02:23:07.910 --> 02:23:09.370
(Chris sobbing)

2661
02:23:11.500 --> 02:23:12.910
(Kate) Shh.

2662
02:23:18.410 --> 02:23:20.160
Hush, hush.

2663
02:23:20.290 --> 02:23:21.790
(Kate and Chris sobbing)

2664
02:23:29.160 --> 02:23:31.620
(Sobbing and music stop)

2665
02:23:31.750 --> 02:23:33.660
(Applause)

2666
02:23:57.660 --> 02:23:59.410
(Cheering)

2667
02:28:21.540 --> 02:28:24.540
Subtitling by MPS
